A.Pz=new B.f(A.Z,"What do we run with?","When we run fast, which body parts are working?",A.E,"Everyone, when we run fast after a ball, which part of our body works the hardest? Point to it!","Have the children point and guess. Keep it fun.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Nl=new B.f(A.Y,"Parts of the body","Head, body, arms, legs.",A.v,'Our bodies have lots of parts: a head on top, a body in the middle, two arms and two legs. Let\'s sing "Head, Shoulders, Knees and Toes" and touch them!',"Sing the body parts song together while touching each part.","Draw a simple child and label the head, arms and legs.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bak=new B.m("kepala","\ud83e\udde0","Head","HEAD: where the brain thinks. It has eyes, ears and a nose too. A helmet protects the head and brain on a motorbike.")
A.bdt=new B.m("tangan","\u270b","Hands","HANDS: for holding, writing, eating and hugging.")
A.bbL=new B.m("kaki","\ud83e\uddb5","Legs","LEGS: for walking, running, jumping and standing.")
A.bai=new B.m("badan","\ud83e\udec0","Body","BODY: the middle part, with the heart and tummy. It holds us up.")
A.aUJ=s([A.bak,A.bdt,A.bbL,A.bai],t.w)
A.MU=new B.f(A.W,"What is each part for?","Tap each part to find out.",A.v,"Let's find out what each body part is for!",null,"Write what each body part is for next to it.",A.aUJ,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Q5=new B.f(A.X,"Show what it does!","Act it out: hands hold, legs jump.",A.v,"Now let's act out what each part does!","Name a body part and have the children act out what it does: hands clap, legs jump, head nods. Keep it fun and active.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b0c=new B.D("We use our hands to walk.","Legs are for walking and jumping; hands are for holding and writing. Have the children act out both.")
A.aZW=new B.D("All fingers are the same length and do the same job.","Fingers are all different; the thumb helps us grip a pencil. Have the children hold a pencil and watch how the thumb works.")
A.aVp=s([A.b0c,A.aZW],t.I)
A.Sr=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Which part do we use to hold a pencil?",A.E,"Si Jabrik is scratching his head. Let's help him. Which part of our body do we use to hold a pencil? And which part do we use to jump?","Ask the children to answer while pointing. Praise every attempt.",null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aVp)
A.N0=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","One hand has 27 tiny bones!",A.a_,"Remember our question? Now you know: our legs do the running, but the whole body helps. Listen to this. In just one of your hands there are 27 tiny bones! That's why our fingers can move so well to write, draw, and hold all sorts of things. Hands are amazing! Try this at home: bend your index finger slowly. In how many places can it bend? Now try your thumb \u2014 the same or different? The places where bones can bend are called joints.","Let's clap \u2014 with all 27 bones in each hand!",null,null,!1,null,"Indonesian para-badminton players, women and men, have won gold medals at the Paralympic Games \u2014 the world sports festival for athletes with disabilities. Their bodies are all different, but training has made their hands and feet very skilful!","When you make a fist, the muscles in your arm send out a very weak electrical signal. In many countries, women and men engineers build artificial hands that pick up that signal through the skin of the arm, and then their fingers grip too.",null)
A.aL9=s([A.Pz,A.Nl,A.MU,A.Q5,A.Sr,A.N0],t.n)
A.aew=s(["Your own bodies",'The song "Head, Shoulders, Knees and Toes" (optional)'],t.s)
A.aSe=s(['Sing "Head, shoulders, knees and toes" while touching each part.',"Repeat faster and faster: the children point to each part named.","Ask what each part is for: what are hands for, what are feet for?","Have the children act out what each body part does."],t.s)
A.a0C=new B.J("Body Parts Song","Each body part has a name and a job.",A.aew,A.aSe,"Our bodies have many parts (head, hands, legs and more), and each part has a job: legs for walking, hands for holding, eyes for seeing. They all work together so we can do things.",A.D,null,10)
A.ayz=s(["Your own bodies","A safe, open floor space"],t.s)
A.aLy=s(["Ask the children to stand on one leg with their eyes open. Count how long they can stay up.","Repeat with eyes CLOSED.","Compare: with eyes closed they usually wobble sooner.","Discuss which parts of the body help us stand up straight."],t.s)
A.a_h=new B.J("Balance Test","The body uses the eyes and feet to keep its balance.",A.ayz,A.aLy,'To keep our balance, the body uses the eyes (seeing our position), the feet and the inner ear. With the eyes closed, one of these "helpers" is gone, so it is harder to keep our balance.',A.D,"Do this in an open space, away from hard objects so no one bumps into them.",10)
A.aW0=s([A.a0C,A.a_h],t.T)
A.l8=s(["Independence","Critical reasoning"],t.s)
A.b6w=new B.aD("Cannot yet point to or name body parts when asked.","Points to and names body parts, but does not yet say what they are for.",'Names 3 body parts AND one thing each is for, e.g. "legs are for walking".')
A.b2i=new B.aC(u.nt,"Which body parts do we use to run?","Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Have the children touch each body part as it is named.","Main activity (20 minutes): Introduce the main body parts (head, hands, legs, body) and what they do, through song and movement. Have the children act out what each part does.","Closing (5 minutes): A short, light Q&A. End with the fact: our hands have many small bones so they can hold things nimbly.","Pupils can name at least 3 body parts and one thing each is for.",A.l8,u.E,A.b6w)
A.bmj=new B.aw("bagian_tubuhku","My Body Parts","\ud83e\uddd2",A.cX,A.bd,u.nt,"Which body parts do we use to run?",25,"Songs and movement are the best way with young children. Let them move a lot: an active body helps them remember.",A.aL9,A.aW0,A.b2i,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to name 5 body parts and one job for each part while pointing to it.",'Sing "Head, Shoulders, Knees and Toes" while pointing; repeat slowly, saying what each body part is for.',null)
A.Rs=new B.f(A.Z,"Whose sound is that?",'"Cluck-cluck!" Which animal makes that sound?',A.E,'Everyone, listen: "Cluck... cluck... cluck!" Which animal do you think sounds like that? Does anyone know?',"Imitate an animal sound and let the children have fun guessing.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.NG=new B.f(A.Y,"Animals around us","Chickens, cats, ducks, cows.",A.v,"There are lots of animals around us: chickens, cats, ducks, cows, and more. Have you ever seen a cat asleep on a motorbike seat that the sun has warmed? Each animal has its own sound and its own way of moving. Tell me the animals you see at home!","Ask the children to name animals near their homes.","Draw a few simple animals: a chicken, a cat, a duck.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bcC=new B.m("ayam","\ud83d\udc14","Chicken",'CHICKEN: says "cluck-cluck", walks and scratches the ground for food.')
A.bay=new B.m("kucing","\ud83d\udc31","Cat",'CAT: says "meow", likes to jump and creep along quietly.')
A.bc2=new B.m("bebek","\ud83e\udd86","Duck",'DUCK: says "quack-quack", waddles when it walks and is a good swimmer.')
A.bdY=new B.m("sapi","\ud83d\udc2e","Cow",'COW: says "moo", has a big body and eats grass.')
A.awz=s([A.bcC,A.bay,A.bc2,A.bdY],t.w)
A.LS=new B.f(A.W,"Animal sounds & moves","Tap each animal to find out.",A.v,"Let's learn the sound and moves of each animal!",null,"Write each animal's sound next to its picture.",A.awz,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Rt=new B.f(A.X,"Be the animal!","Copy the animal sounds & moves.",A.v,"Now it's your turn to be animals!","Name an animal and have the children imitate its sound and movement: waddle like a duck, meow like a cat. Have fun together.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b_2=new B.D("All animals eat the same food.","Each animal eats different food: cows eat grass, cats eat fish or meat. Ask the children to name their favourite animal's food.")
A.b0T=new B.D("Fish can live on land like us.","Fish breathe in water using gills; on land they cannot breathe. That is why fish live in water.")
A.b_e=new B.D("Plastic rubbish on the ground doesn't hurt animals.","Cows, goats and fish can mistake plastic for food and get ill. Ask the children to put plastic rubbish in its proper place.")
A.aQx=s([A.b_2,A.b0T,A.b_e],t.I)
A.P8=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding",'Which animal says "moo"?',A.E,'Oh no, Si Jabrik forgot! Who can remind him? Which animal says "moo" and eats grass? And which animal is good at swimming in a pond?',"Ask the children to answer while imitating. Praise every attempt.",null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aQx)
A.O1=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Cats can hear super quiet sounds!",A.a_,"Remember our question? Yes, a chicken! And now you know other animal sounds too. This is so cool! A cat's ears are much sharper than ours. Cats can hear the tiny, quiet footsteps of a mouse from far away. That's why cats are such good hunters at night! Tomorrow morning, listen carefully and count: how many different animal sounds can you hear around your home?","A round of applause for our animal friends!",null,null,!1,null,"Pelung chickens from Cianjur in West Java are famous for their long, tuneful crowing \u2014 there are even pelung crowing contests!","Scientists in many countries leave sound recorders in forests for weeks. Then computers that have learnt from thousands of example sounds pick out the birds, frogs and insects on the recordings.",null)
A.aS7=s([A.Rs,A.NG,A.LS,A.Rt,A.P8,A.O1],t.n)
A.aNI=s(["Your voices, to imitate the animals","A list of animals the children know (chicken, cat, cow, duck)"],t.s)
A.aOp=s(['The teacher or one child imitates an animal sound (e.g. "cluck-cluck").',"The other children guess which animal it is.","Imitate the movements too: waddle like a duck, hop like a frog.","Discuss: where does that animal usually live?"],t.s)
A.Zy=new B.J("Guess the Animal Sound","Each animal has its own special sound and way of moving.",A.aNI,A.aOp,"Many kinds of animals live around us. Each animal has its own sound (a rooster crows, a cat meows) and its own way of moving (birds fly, fish swim). Recognising sounds and movements helps us tell these animals apart.",A.D,"If observing real animals, do not handle them carelessly.",10)
A.aNO=s(["Animals nearby (ants, chickens, cats) or animal pictures","Exercise book & pencil"],t.s)
A.aEX=s(["Observe a few animals and count their legs.","Group them: 2 legs (chicken, bird), 4 legs (cat, cow), many legs (ant 6, spider 8).","Record them in a simple table.","Discuss: which animals have no legs (fish, snake)?"],t.s)
A.a__=new B.J("Count the Animal Legs","Animals can be grouped by their number of legs & body features.",A.aNO,A.aEX,"Animals have different numbers of legs: insects have 6, spiders 8, four-legged animals 4, birds 2, and some have none, like fish and snakes. Counting and grouping is how scientists get to know animals.",A.D,"Only watch \u2014 do not touch or disturb the animals.",15)
A.aTE=s([A.Zy,A.a__],t.T)
A.aUb=s(["Independence","Caring for the environment"],t.s)
A.b6I=new B.aD("Cannot yet name animals around them.","Names a few animals, but cannot yet imitate their sound or movement.","Names 3 animals AND imitates their special sound or movement.")
A.b1E=new B.aC(u.hY,'Which animal says "cluck-cluck"?',"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question while imitating the animal sound. Let the children guess.","Main activity (20 minutes): Introduce animals around us and their sounds and movements through a sound-guessing game and by imitating how the animals move. Discuss where each animal lives.","Closing (5 minutes): A short, light Q&A. End with the fact: cats can hear very faint sounds; their hearing is much sharper than ours.","Pupils can name at least 3 animals and imitate their sound or movement.",A.aUb,u.E,A.b6I)
A.blr=new B.aw("hewan_di_sekitarku","Animals Around Me","\ud83d\udc14",A.cX,A.bd,u.hY,'Which animal says "cluck-cluck"?',25,"Imitating animal sounds and movements makes children laugh and join in. The livelier the imitating, the better they remember.",A.aS7,A.aTE,A.b1E,null,!1,!1,'Ask the children to sort the animals they know into "lives on land" and "lives in water", then name one feature of each group.',"Show pictures or imitate animal sounds; have the children guess the animal's name and where it lives. Praise every guess.",null)
A.T7=new B.f(A.Z,"Stone or cotton wool?","Hold a stone, then cotton wool. How do they feel?",A.E,"Everyone, imagine one hand holding a stone and the other hand holding cotton wool. What difference would you feel?","Have the children touch their desk (hard) and their clothes (soft) and feel the difference.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Nr=new B.f(A.Y,"Things have properties","Some are hard/soft, rough/smooth.",A.v,"Touch your desk, then touch your clothes. Do they feel the same or different? Now hold a stone, then hold some cotton wool. What do you think makes these things feel different in our hands?","Ask the children to touch a few objects and say how they feel.","Make 2 columns: HARD | SOFT. Fill them in with the class.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bbY=new B.m("keras","\ud83e\udea8","Hard","HARD: not easy to press or change shape. Examples: stone, table, wood.")
A.bdA=new B.m("lembut","\u2601\ufe0f","Soft","SOFT: squishy and easy to press. Examples: cotton wool, pillow, foam.")
A.be6=new B.m("kasar","\ud83e\uddf1","Rough","ROUGH: bumpy, not flat, feels scratchy. Examples: sandpaper, brick, tree bark.")
A.bbl=new B.m("halus","\ud83e\udee7","Smooth","SMOOTH: flat and slippery. Examples: glass, banana leaf, a phone screen.")
A.aL7=s([A.bbY,A.bdA,A.be6,A.bbl],t.w)
A.Ox=new B.f(A.W,"How do things feel?","Tap each word to find out.",A.v,"Let's learn how different things feel!",null,"Write example objects under each property.",A.aL7,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.TE=new B.f(A.X,"Play the feely box!","Feel the thing in the box. How does it feel?",A.v,"Now let's play the feely box game!","Put different objects in a closed box. Ask the children to feel without looking and guess hard/soft or rough/smooth, then check.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b08=new B.D("All things are hard.","Some things are hard (stone) and some are soft (cotton wool, cloth). Have the children touch two objects and compare how they feel.")
A.aZ6=new B.D("Big things are always heavy, small things are always light.","Not always: a big balloon feels light, a small stone feels heavy. Have the children weigh two objects in their hands.")
A.aRE=s([A.b08,A.aZ6],t.I)
A.LE=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Is cotton wool hard or soft?",A.E,"Si Jabrik needs your help. Is cotton wool hard or soft? And is a stone rough or smooth?","Ask the children to answer while touching an example. Praise every attempt.",null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aRE)
A.Sx=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Your skin can feel even the tiniest drop of water!",A.a_,"Remember our question? Now you know: a stone is hard and cotton wool is soft. Get ready for a surprise! Our skin is full of tiny feeling spots. That's why we can feel a tiny drop of water, a gentle breeze, or the difference between smooth and rough cloth, even with our eyes closed. Our skin is like a magic feeling machine! Tonight, feel some cloth with your fingertip, then with your elbow. Which one feels it better? Tell us tomorrow!","A round of applause for our clever, feeling skin!",null,null,!1,null,"Ikat cloth from East Nusa Tenggara is woven by hand from thread dyed with plant roots and leaves. One cloth can take weeks to make; if you touch it, its texture feels different from smooth factory cloth.",'Scientists in many countries make "electronic skin" for robot hands: a thin, bendy sheet full of sensors (artificial feeling spots). With it, a robot knows to hold gently, for example when it picks up an egg.',null)
A.aLv=s([A.T7,A.Nr,A.Ox,A.TE,A.LE,A.Sx],t.n)
A.aLj=s(["A box (e.g. a cardboard box) with a hole for a hand (or a cloth bag)","Objects with different textures: stone, cotton wool, leaf, sponge, sandpaper, a used plastic bottle cap"],t.s)
A.awk=s(["Put a few objects in the box without the children seeing.","A child puts a hand in and feels one object.","Ask them to guess: hard or soft? Rough or smooth?","Take the object out. Was the guess right?"],t.s)
A.a_p=new B.J("Feely Box","Things feel different: hard/soft, rough/smooth.",A.aLj,A.awk,"The things around us have different properties that we can feel by touching: some are hard (stone) and some soft (cotton wool), some are rough (sandpaper) and some smooth (glass). Sorting things by their properties helps us get to know the world.",A.D,"Use safe objects that are not sharp or dirty.",10)
A.aL8=s(["A clothes hanger, or a ruler hung up by its middle","2 plastic bags/cups as containers","Small objects to weigh (stone, cotton wool, pencil)"],t.s)
A.aOc=s(["Hang the hanger so it can swing freely. Tie a container to each end.","Put one object in the left container and one in the right container.","Observe: the container that goes down holds the heavier object.","Compare several pairs of objects."],t.s)
A.YQ=new B.J("Hanger Balance Scale","We can compare which object is heavier with a simple scale.",A.aL8,A.aOc,"A simple scale shows which object is heavier: the side that goes down is heavier, the side that goes up is lighter. Weight is one property of objects that we can compare.",A.D,null,15)
A.ael=s([A.a_p,A.YQ],t.T)
A.b6a=new B.aD("Cannot yet tell hard/soft or rough/smooth apart when touching.","Feels the difference when touching, but is still unsure how to sort the objects.","Correctly sorts 3 objects by hard/soft or rough/smooth.")
A.b1e=new B.aC(u.kn,u.l6,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Have the children touch their desks and their clothes and feel the difference.","Main activity (20 minutes): Introduce properties of objects (hard/soft, rough/smooth) by touching real objects. Sort objects by their properties. Play the feely box game.","Closing (5 minutes): A short, light Q&A. End with the fact: our skin is full of tiny nerve endings that can feel rough and smooth.","Pupils can sort at least 3 objects by hard/soft or rough/smooth.",A.l8,u.E,A.b6a)
A.bln=new B.aw("benda_di_sekitarku","Things Around Me","\ud83e\uddfa",A.cX,A.ci,u.kn,u.l6,25,"Classroom objects are free teaching aids. Let the children touch lots of things: hands-on touch is the main way to learn at this age.",A.aLv,A.ael,A.b1e,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to sort 5 objects into hard/soft or rough/smooth and give their reasons.","Prepare a box of objects; have the children feel them with their eyes closed and guess rough/smooth, hard/soft.",null)
A.NY=new B.f(A.Z,"Float or sink?","A leaf floats, a stone sinks. Why are they different?",A.E,"Everyone, when we put a leaf in water, it floats. But a stone goes straight down to the bottom. Why do you think they're so different?",u.n,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Qv=new B.f(A.Y,"Some go up, some go down","Some things float, some things sink.",A.v,"When we put things in water, some stay on top (they float) and some go down to the bottom (they sink). Have you ever seen a plastic bottle floating in a river or the sea? Can you guess: what other things usually float?","Show the bucket of water and a few objects to test later.","Make 2 columns: FLOAT | SINK.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bb2=new B.m("daun","\ud83c\udf43","Leaf","LEAF: FLOATS. It is light and thin, so it stays on top of the water.")
A.bbU=new B.m("batu","\ud83e\udea8","Stone","STONE: SINKS. It is solid and heavy, so it drops straight to the bottom.")
A.baQ=new B.m("gabus","\ud83e\uddfd","Cork","CORK: FLOATS. It is full of air pockets and light, so it floats easily.")
A.bdT=new B.m("sendok","\ud83e\udd44","Metal spoon","METAL SPOON: SINKS. Metal is solid and heavy for its size, so it goes to the bottom.")
A.aMT=s([A.bb2,A.bbU,A.baQ,A.bdT],t.w)
A.SR=new B.f(A.W,"Guess each object","Tap each object to find out.",A.v,"Let's guess which things float and which sink!",null,"Write each object in the float or sink column.",A.aMT,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.So=new B.f(A.X,"Test it in water!","Guess first, then drop it in & watch.",A.v,"Now let's test things in the water!","Before each object goes in, ask the children to guess float or sink. Then drop it in and watch together. Sort the results.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b__=new B.D("Heavy things always sink, light things always float.","Not always: an iron ship is very heavy but floats because of its shape. Have the children test a few objects in water; the results are sometimes surprising.")
A.b_k=new B.D("Big things always sink.","Being big does not mean something will sink; a big piece of wood still floats. Test a few objects together in the basin.")
A.aZb=new B.D("Plastic that floats away down a river just disappears.","Plastic is light, so it floats and the current can carry it all the way to the sea; it does not disappear. Ask the children to put rubbish in its proper place or reuse old bottles, for example as plant pots.")
A.aTT=s([A.b__,A.b_k,A.aZb],t.I)
A.MK=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Does cork float or sink?",A.E,"Si Jabrik is confused. Can you help him? Does cork float or sink? And what about a metal spoon?","Ask the children to answer and explain. Praise every attempt.",null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aTT)
A.P5=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Giant iron ships can float on the sea!",A.a_,"Remember our question? Now you know the answer. Did you know? Even a small stone sinks, but a huge, heavy ship made of iron can FLOAT! The secret: a ship is hollow and wide, so it can float on top of the water. Aren't people clever? At home, with a grown-up's permission, try a basin of water: a sandal, a bottle cap, a fruit. Guess first, then test! Scientists often guess wrong at first too.","A round of applause for the giant floating ships!",null,null,!1,null,"At the Cirata Reservoir in West Java (a very big human-made lake), more than 300 thousand solar panels FLOAT on hollow plastic floats, like our capped bottle. Solar panels are boards that turn sunlight into electricity.","In the Dead Sea, a very salty lake in the Middle East, the water is so salty that people can float while sitting back and relaxing \u2014 just like an egg floating in salt water!",null)
A.aem=s([A.NY,A.Qv,A.SR,A.So,A.MK,A.P5],t.n)
A.aQf=s(["A bucket or basin of water","Things from home: stone, leaf, spoon, cork or styrofoam, pencil, marble, a used plastic bottle with its cap on"],t.s)
A.aTr=s(["Fill the bucket with water.","Before dropping each one in, ask the children to guess: will this float or sink?","Put the objects in one at a time and watch what happens.","Sort the objects into those that float and those that sink."],t.s)
A.Zi=new B.J("Float or Sink?","Some things float in water, and some sink.",A.aQf,A.aTr,"Some things float in water (cork, leaves, wood) and some sink (stones, marbles, metal spoons). Things that are light for their size tend to float; things that are dense and heavy tend to sink. Guessing first and then testing trains children to think like scientists.",A.D,"Supervise children near water. Dry the floor so it is not slippery.",15)
A.aHn=s(["1 egg","2 large glasses of water","Table salt (a few tablespoons)"],t.s)
A.aRB=s(["Put the egg in a glass of plain water \u2014 the egg SINKS.","In the second glass, dissolve lots of salt in the water and stir well.","Put the egg in the salt water \u2014 the egg FLOATS!","Discuss why the results are different."],t.s)
A.Za=new B.J("Egg Floating in Salt Water","Salt water is heavier (denser), so things float in it more easily.",A.aHn,A.aRB,"Salt water is denser (heavier for its size) than plain water. Because the salt water is heavier, it can hold the egg up so it floats. This is why our bodies float more easily in salty sea water.",A.D,"This water and egg are not for eating after the experiment.",15)
A.aTQ=s(["Plasticine or clay","A basin of water"],t.s)
A.aNZ=s(["Roll the plasticine into a solid ball and put it in the water \u2014 it SINKS.","Take it out and shape it into a hollow bowl/boat.","Place the boat gently on the water \u2014 now it FLOATS!","Discuss why the same material can give a different result."],t.s)
A.ZU=new B.J("Plasticine Boat","An object's shape decides whether it floats or sinks, not just its material.",A.aTQ,A.aNZ,"The same material can sink or float depending on its shape. A wide, hollow boat shape pushes more water aside, so the water holds it up and it floats. This is the secret of how iron ships can float.",A.D,null,15)
A.aMx=s([A.Zi,A.Za,A.ZU],t.T)
A.b2=s(["Critical reasoning","Independence"],t.s)
A.b6m=new B.aD("Cannot yet guess or observe whether things float or sink.","Guesses float/sink, but does not yet compare the guess with the test result.","Guesses FIRST and then tests, and sorts 3 objects by the result.")
A.b2m=new B.aC(u.i,"Why does a leaf float but a stone sink?","Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Show a bucket of water and a few objects.","Main activity (20 minutes): Have the children guess whether each object will float or sink before testing, then drop it in and watch. Sort the results. Stress the habit of guess-then-test.","Closing (5 minutes): A short, light Q&A. End with the fact: a heavy iron ship can float because its shape is hollow.","Pupils can sort at least 3 objects into float or sink after testing.",A.b2,u.E,A.b6m)
A.blS=new B.aw("terapung_tenggelam","Float & Sink","\ud83d\udedf",A.cX,A.ci,u.i,"Why does a leaf float but a stone sink?",25,"Always ask the children to GUESS before dropping an object in. Guess-then-test is the heart of scientific thinking, and children love it.",A.aem,A.aMx,A.b2m,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to guess first (float/sink) for 5 objects, then test them in water and count how many guesses were right.","Prepare a basin of water; test objects one at a time while the children guess. Link the results to what each object is made of.",null)
A.Mn=new B.f(A.Z,"Is rain coming?","When rain is coming, what does the sky look like?",A.E,"Everyone, when it's about to rain, what does the sky above us look like? Bright and sunny, or dark and cloudy?","Have the children look out of the window and tell you what they see.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Mz=new B.f(A.Y,"Many kinds of sky","Sunny, cloudy, rainy.",A.v,"Look out of the window. Farmers and fishers look at the sky before they start work too. Weather experts at Indonesia's weather agency (BMKG) watch the sky every day! What's the sky like today? Was it the same yesterday? What do you think \u2014 what different ways can our sky look?","Ask the children to describe the sky they can see right now.","Draw 3 skies: a sun, a cloud, and a cloud with raindrops.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bc3=new B.m("cerah","\u2600\ufe0f","Sunny","SUNNY: bright blue sky, the sun is shining. Great for playing outside, but remember to drink water.")
A.bbf=new B.m("berawan","\u2601\ufe0f","Cloudy","CLOUDY: the sky is covered in white or grey clouds, and the air is cooler.")
A.bbu=new B.m("hujan","\ud83c\udf27\ufe0f","Rainy","RAINY: water drops fall from the clouds. Use an umbrella or raincoat.")
A.be2=new B.m("pelangi","\ud83c\udf08","Rainbow","RAINBOW: appears after rain when the sun shines, with beautiful colours across the sky.")
A.aNW=s([A.bc3,A.bbf,A.bbu,A.be2],t.w)
A.NA=new B.f(A.W,"Know your weather","Tap each kind of weather to find out.",A.v,"Let's learn what each kind of weather looks like in the sky!",null,"Write the signs of each weather next to its picture.",A.aNW,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Ns=new B.f(A.X,"Make a weather calendar!","Look at the sky & record the weather every morning.",A.v,"Now we're going to be little weather watchers!","Make a one-week weather calendar. Every morning, have the children look at the sky and stick on that day's weather symbol. At the end of the week, count together.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b0b=new B.D("A dark sky in the daytime means it is already night.","The sky is dark in the daytime because thick clouds hold lots of water droplets, a sign that rain is coming, not night. Point to the clock to convince the children.")
A.aZx=new B.D("The Sun disappears when it rains because it goes to sleep.","The Sun is still there, just hidden by thick clouds. When the clouds move away, we can see the Sun again.")
A.aQe=s([A.b0b,A.aZx],t.I)
A.Na=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","The sky is full of dark clouds. What weather might come?",A.E,"Si Jabrik is scratching his head. Let's help him. If the sky is full of dark clouds, what weather might be coming? And what should we take with us?","Ask the children to answer cheerfully. Praise every attempt.",null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aQe)
A.MW=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Fluffy clouds are really millions of water drops!",A.a_,"Remember our question? Now you can read the sky yourselves. Listen to this. Clouds that look soft and fluffy like cotton wool are really made of MILLIONS of very tiny, light water droplets floating in the air. When the droplets join together and get heavy, down comes the rain. The sky is full of wonders! Tomorrow, pick one cloud, then look again after counting slowly to 100. What has it turned into? You're watching the sky just like a weather expert!","A round of applause for the sky and its clouds!",null,null,!1,null,"From space, weather satellites \u2014 machines made by people that circle the Earth \u2014 can see the clouds over the whole of Indonesia at once. Weather experts at Indonesia's weather agency (BMKG) use their pictures to forecast rain.","In very cold countries, the water in clouds freezes into ice crystals and falls as snow \u2014 tiny flakes shaped like six-pointed stars.",null)
A.aTd=s([A.Mn,A.Mz,A.NA,A.Ns,A.Na,A.MW],t.n)
A.aP8=s(["A large sheet of paper for the calendar","Markers/crayons","Home-made weather symbols: sun, cloud, rain"],t.s)
A.aSy=s(["Draw a 7-day table on the large sheet of paper.","Every morning, take the children to look at the sky outside.","Stick on or draw that day's weather symbol on the calendar.","After a week, count: how many days were sunny, cloudy and rainy?"],t.s)
A.a0x=new B.J("One-Week Weather Calendar","The weather changes every day, and we can observe it.",A.aP8,A.aSy,"Weather is what the sky is like today: it can be sunny, cloudy or rainy. The weather changes from day to day. By observing and recording it for a week, children learn that the sky has many looks and can be observed, just as weather scientists do.",A.D,null,10)
A.aMG=s(["A square of paper","A thin palm-leaf stick or straw + a dressmaker's pin or drawing pin","Scissors"],t.s)
A.aL4=s(["The TEACHER prepares the windmill: cut from each corner towards the centre (do not cut all the way), then fold one point of each side to the centre.","The TEACHER holds the folds in place with the pin and pushes it into the stick/straw.","The children blow on the windmill or take it somewhere windy.","Watch the windmill spin when the wind hits it."],t.s)
A.a0r=new B.J("Paper Windmill","Wind (moving air) can make things move.",A.aMG,A.aL4,'Wind is moving air. When the wind hits the windmill\'s blades, it pushes them so the windmill spins. With a windmill we can "see" and feel the strength of the invisible wind.',A.ct,"Pins & scissors are for the teacher only. The teacher assembles the windmill first; the children only blow and observe.",15)
A.aP7=s([A.a0x,A.a0r],t.T)
A.b6C=new B.aD("Cannot yet say what today's weather is.","Names the weather (sunny/rainy), but cannot yet describe how the sky looks.","Names 2 types of weather AND describes how they look in the sky.")
A.b1M=new B.aC(u.ff,u.dS,"Opening (5 minutes): Have the children look out of the window. Ask what the sky looks like today.","Main activity (20 minutes): Introduce sunny, cloudy and rainy weather and how each looks in the sky. Start a weather calendar. Discuss which clothes suit each kind of weather.","Closing (5 minutes): A short, light Q&A. End with the fact: clouds that look light are actually made of millions of tiny water droplets.","Pupils can name at least 2 types of weather and describe how they look in the sky.",A.l8,u.E,A.b6C)
A.blp=new B.aw("cuaca_langit","Weather & Sky","\ud83c\udf24\ufe0f",A.cX,A.bw,u.ff,u.dS,25,"The sky outside the window is a living lesson. Have the children really look outside: observing first-hand is stronger than any picture.",A.aTd,A.aP7,A.b1M,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to observe today's sky and draw the weather (sunny/cloudy/rainy), then guess the weather this afternoon.","Point to the sky through the window; talk about the colour of the clouds and guess together what weather is coming.",null)
A.Qe=new B.f(A.Z,"How do we know?","Without looking, how do we know a flower is near us?",A.E,"Everyone, if your eyes were closed, how could you tell there's a sweet-smelling flower near you? What would you use?","Have the children close their eyes and guess. Keep it fun.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Mw=new B.f(A.Y,"Our five senses","Eyes, ears, nose, tongue, skin.",A.v,"Close your eyes for a moment. Can you still hear your friends? Can you still smell things? Our bodies have special tools for getting to know the world. How many tools do you think there are, and where are they?","Have the children point to their eyes, ears, nose, tongue and skin.","Draw a simple face with eyes, ears, a nose and a mouth.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.baf=new B.m("mata","\ud83d\udc40","Eyes","EYES are for SEEING: colours, shapes and things around us.")
A.beN=new B.m("telinga","\ud83d\udc42","Ears","EARS are for HEARING: music, friends calling us and rain pattering on the roof.")
A.bbp=new B.m("hidung","\ud83d\udc43","Nose","The NOSE is for SMELLING: sweet flowers or yummy cooking.")
A.bdx=new B.m("lidah","\ud83d\udc45","Tongue","The TONGUE is for TASTING: sweet, salty, sour and bitter.")
A.bbm=new B.m("kulit","\ud83d\udd90\ufe0f","Skin","SKIN is for FEELING: hot, cold, smooth and rough.")
A.a2x=s([A.baf,A.beN,A.bbp,A.bdx,A.bbm],t.w)
A.MR=new B.f(A.W,"What does each sense do?","Tap each sense to find out.",A.v,"Let's find out what each sense does!",null,"Write what each sense does next to it on the face.",A.a2x,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.PN=new B.f(A.X,"Guess the object!","Eyes closed: guess the object with your other senses.",A.v,"Now let's play guess the object!","Cover one child's eyes and give them a safe object to touch or smell. Ask them to guess what it is, then name the sense they used.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.aZi=new B.D("We hear with our mouths.","We hear with our ears. The mouth is for talking and eating. Have the children cover their ears and notice sounds get quieter.")
A.aZI=new B.D("The tongue is used for smelling.","The nose is for smelling; the tongue is for tasting. Have the children hold their nose while eating: the food tastes weaker.")
A.b0P=new B.D("Sweet is only tasted at the tip of the tongue, bitter only at the back.",'That old "tongue map" turned out to be wrong \u2014 it came from a misreading of a German study from 1901. Taste buds all over the tongue can taste every taste: sweet, salty, sour and bitter.')
A.aMm=s([A.aZi,A.aZI,A.b0P],t.I)
A.OU=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Which sense do we use to hear sounds?",A.E,"Si Jabrik is confused. Can you help him? Which sense do we use to hear sounds? And which sense do we use to smell something nice?","Ask the children to answer while pointing to the sense. Praise every attempt.",null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aMm)
A.OL=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Your tongue has thousands of tiny taste buds!",A.a_,"Remember our question? Now you know: our nose smells the food first. Did you know? Your tongue has thousands of tiny taste buds that help you taste sweet, salty and sour. That's why ice cream tastes sweet and oranges taste sour. Tongues are amazing! Tomorrow morning, close your eyes for one minute and count: how many different sounds can you hear?",u.G,null,null,!1,null,"Long ago, nutmeg grew only in the Banda Islands in Maluku. People loved its smell and taste so much that traders from Europe sailed halfway around the world to buy it.","If the cochlea (a snail-shaped tube) deep inside the ear is damaged, doctors can fit a cochlear implant that turns sound into electrical signals for the hearing nerve. Hundreds of thousands of people around the world already use one.",null)
A.aTJ=s([A.Qe,A.Mw,A.MR,A.PN,A.OU,A.OL],t.n)
A.aKY=s(["A few safe objects from the classroom (pencil, leaf, stone, chalk)","A cloth or handkerchief to cover the eyes"],t.s)
A.aLO=s(["Cover one child's eyes with the cloth.","Give them an object to hold, smell, or listen to.","Ask the child to guess what the object is without looking.","Uncover their eyes. Was the guess right? Which sense did they use?"],t.s)
A.a_D=new B.J("Blindfold Guess-the-Object","We get to know the world through five senses, not just our eyes.",A.aKY,A.aLO,"We have five senses: eyes (seeing), ears (hearing), nose (smelling), tongue (tasting), and skin (touching). When one sense is covered, the other senses work harder to help us recognise things.",A.D,"Do not let children put small objects in their mouths. Use safe, clean objects.",10)
A.aUI=s(["Safe kitchen ingredients: sugar, salt, coffee/tea, orange slices","A cloth blindfold","Drinking water to rinse the mouth between tries"],t.s)
A.aKS=s(["Blindfold one child.","Ask them to smell and then taste a little of each item and guess it.","Try again while holding the NOSE \u2014 the taste is harder to guess.","Discuss: the nose also helps us taste our food."],t.s)
A.a_r=new B.J("Guess the Taste & Smell","The senses of taste (tongue) and smell (nose) work together.",A.aUI,A.aKS,"The tongue tastes the basic tastes (sweet, salty, bitter, sour), but the nose adds lots of flavour information through smell. With the nose closed, food tastes bland \u2014 that is why food tastes less nice when we have a cold.",A.D,"Use clean, food-safe ingredients. Do not share used spoons. Ask the parents first \u2014 not for children with food allergies.",10)
A.aQ1=s(["Things that make sounds: paper being scrunched, keys, water being poured, a spoon being tapped","A cloth blindfold"],t.s)
A.aL2=s(["Blindfold one child.","Make a sound with one object (scrunch paper, pour water, tap a spoon).","Ask them to guess the object/activity from the sound alone.","Take off the blindfold and check."],t.s)
A.a06=new B.J("Guess from the Sound","Our ears recognise things from their own special sound.",A.aQ1,A.aL2,"Without looking, our ears can still recognise many things from their special sound. The sense of hearing helps us understand what is around us \u2014 even when we are not using our eyes.",A.D,null,10)
A.aS2=s([A.a_D,A.a_r,A.a06],t.T)
A.b6U=new B.aD("Names fewer than 3 senses, or names them without knowing what they are for.","Names 3\u20134 senses and what they are for, but not all five.",'Names ALL FIVE senses AND what they are for, with examples, e.g. "skin is for feeling heat".')
A.b2r=new B.aC(u.iB,u.pd,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Have the children close their eyes and guess the sounds around them.","Main activity (20 minutes): Introduce the five senses and what they do through simple games: smelling, touching, listening. Play the blindfold guess-the-object game.","Closing (5 minutes): A short, light Q&A. End with the fact: the tongue has thousands of tiny taste buds for tasting flavours.","Pupils can name all five senses and what they are for, and give an example for each sense.",A.l8,u.T,A.b6U)
A.blP=new B.aw("panca_indra","The Five Senses","\ud83d\udc40",A.aM,A.bd,u.iB,u.pd,30,"The senses are easiest to understand through the body and games. Get the children touching, smelling and listening, not memorising. For the tasting experiment, ask parents first, and leave out any child with a food allergy.",A.aTJ,A.aS2,A.b2r,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to match the 5 senses to their jobs and give 1 example of each sense inside the classroom.","Play a guessing game: close your eyes, then listen to, smell or touch an object. Name the sense being used each time, together with the children.",u.F)
A.T1=new B.f(A.Z,"Oh no, why did it wilt?","Si Jabrik pulls a plant out of its pot \u2014 it wilts at once!",A.oq,"Everyone, look! Si Jabrik pulled a plant out of the soil. Oh dear, all of a sudden it wilted! Why do you think that happened?","Let a few children guess. There are no right or wrong answers yet \u2014 we will find out together.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.PW=new B.f(A.Y,"Look at the parts","Every plant has roots, a stem and leaves.",A.v,"Every plant is made of parts. Which parts of a plant can you name?","If there is a plant or some grass near the classroom, touch it and observe it together.","Draw a simple plant with 3 parts \u2014 roots (lines going down), stem (an upright line), leaves (oval shapes). Simple lines are enough.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bd5=new B.m("akar","\ud83c\udf31","Roots","ROOTS: take in water & minerals from the soil, and grip the soil so landslides happen less easily. Without roots, a plant wilts \u2014 just like before!")
A.bet=new B.m("batang","\ud83c\udf3f","Stem","STEM: the path water takes up from the roots to the leaves. Like a pipe or a straw!")
A.ba4=new B.m("daun","\ud83c\udf43","Leaves","LEAVES: where food is cooked using sunlight. Leaves are the plant's kitchen!")
A.aSY=s([A.bd5,A.bet,A.ba4],t.w)
A.Sn=new B.f(A.W,"What does each part do?","Tap each part \u2014 Si Jabrik explains.",A.v,"Let's find out what job each part does. I'll point, and you guess what it does!",null,"Write each part's job next to it on your blackboard drawing.",A.aSY,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Py=new B.f(A.X,"Try it yourself!","Pull up a small weed \u2014 look at its roots.",A.v,"Now it's your turn to be scientists!","Have the children pull up a small weed in the schoolyard and count/look at its roots. If you cannot go outside: have them draw a plant in their books and label each part.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.aYK=new B.D("The leaves take in water from the soil.",'Praise first: "well spotted, the leaves!" Then guide them \u2014 the leaves cook food; the part that sucks up water from the soil is the roots. Remember how the plant wilted when its roots were pulled out?')
A.b_j=new B.D("If all the leaves are picked off, the plant will be fine.",'Without leaves, a plant cannot cook its food, and it slowly grows weak. Leaves are the plant\'s "kitchen".')
A.b0q=new B.D("A plant will always die if it isn't planted in soil.","Not always. Water hyacinth in rivers and swamps floats with its roots in the water. Hydroponic farmers also grow vegetables with their roots in water with minerals added, with no soil. What roots need is water, minerals and air.")
A.aT0=s([A.aYK,A.b_j,A.b0q],t.I)
A.My=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Which part takes in water?",A.E,"Si Jabrik is confused \u2014 let's help him! Which part takes in water from the soil? And\u2026 if we picked off all the leaves, what would happen to the plant?","Ask the children to raise their hands to answer. Praise every attempt to answer.",null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aT0)
A.T9=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","One big tree drinks 400 litres of water a day!",A.a_,"Remember our question at the start? Now you know: without roots, a plant can't stand up strong and can't drink. Did you know? One big tree soaks up as much as 400 litres of water in ONE DAY through its roots \u2014 more than a whole bathtub full! Those tiny roots are amazing, aren't they? And here's something nobody knows yet: scientists are still trying to find out whether trees can share food with each other through fine threads of fungus in the soil.","A round of applause for Si Jabrik \u2014 and for all of you!",null,null,!1,null,"Farmers usually press the soil with a finger to tell whether it is dry or wet. Now some farmers in Indonesia use soil-moisture sensors, so watering can switch on by itself just when the roots need water.","The roots of a wild fig tree in South Africa are estimated to reach about 120 metres down into the ground in search of water \u2014 roughly five tall coconut palms stacked on top of each other!",null)
A.aV0=s([A.T1,A.PW,A.Sn,A.Py,A.My,A.T9],t.n)
A.aqx=s(["1 leafy vegetable stalk (mustard greens/celery/spinach) or a white-petalled flower","1 glass of water","Food colouring OR turmeric water/strong tea (if you have no colouring)"],t.s)
A.aUn=s(["Fill the glass with water and add colouring until the colour is strong.","Put the vegetable stalk/flower into the coloured water.","Place it somewhere bright. WAIT a few hours, up to a day.","Observe: the colour climbs up the stem and into the leaves/petals!"],t.s)
A.ZZ=new B.J("The Plant That Drinks Coloured Water","Proves that the stem carries water from bottom to top (Discover beat).",A.aqx,A.aUn,'The stem has tiny tubes that pull water upwards \u2014 all the way to the leaves. That is why the colour climbs up too. This is how plants "drink" through their roots and stems, just like we learned!',A.D,null,360)
A.aRF=s(["A living, leafy plant (in a pot or the garden)","A clear plastic bag","String/rubber band to tie it"],t.s)
A.aLb=s(["Wrap a few leaves, still on the plant, in the clear plastic bag.","Tie the end of the bag tightly around the stem.","Put the plant in a bright place for a few hours.","Observe: drops of water appear inside the bag."],t.s)
A.a_5=new B.J("Sweating Leaves","Plants release water through their leaves (transpiration).",A.aRF,A.aLb,'Plants take in water through their roots, then release some of it as water vapour through their leaves \u2014 this is called transpiration. The vapour condenses into water drops inside the bag. So leaves are not just a "kitchen" \u2014 they also "sweat".',A.D,"Do not cover the whole plant for too long, or it will wilt.",180)
A.aRw=s(["Stem cuttings of cassava or sweet potato (CUT BY THE TEACHER with a knife), or a Kalanchoe (life-plant) leaf","Damp soil in a pot/polybag (or a glass of water)"],t.s)
A.aMS=s(["Push the stem cutting into the damp soil (or stand its base in water).","Put it in a bright but shady spot and keep it damp.","Observe for a few days, up to a week or more.","Look: roots grow from the stem, then new shoots and leaves appear."],t.s)
A.a0d=new B.J("Growing Stem Cuttings","A stem can grow new roots \u2014 evidence of what stems & roots do.",A.aRw,A.aMS,"The stems of some plants can grow new roots and become a whole new plant \u2014 this is called vegetative reproduction. It shows that the stem carries water and the roots take it in, exactly the jobs we learned about.",A.D,"The teacher cuts the stems with a knife. Wash hands after handling the soil and the stem sap.",10080)
A.aKq=s([A.ZZ,A.a_5,A.a0d],t.T)
A.b6N=new B.aD("Cannot name the parts of a plant yet, or guesses names without knowing what they are for.","Names roots/stem/leaves, but is still unsure how to explain what each part does.",'Names 3 parts AND explains what they do in their own words \u2014 e.g. "roots are for drinking water".')
A.b2x=new B.aC(u.m,u.mI,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Show the Si Jabrik story where his plant wilts after he pulls it up \u2014 let the children guess why.","Main activity (20 minutes): Observe and identify the parts of a plant (roots, stem, leaves). Discuss what each part does. Have the children pull up a small weed, or draw a plant in their exercise books and label each part.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers to check understanding. End with a fun fact: one big tree can soak up as much as 400 litres of water in a day.","Pupils can name at least 3 parts of a plant and correctly explain what they do.",A.b2,u.T,A.b6N)
A.aPF=s(["Exercise book & pencil for notes and drawing","If possible: a plant or small weed from home/the schoolyard (with its roots still on)"],t.s)
A.bl3=new B.aw("bagian_tubuh_tumbuhan","Parts of a Plant","\ud83c\udf3f",A.aM,A.bd,u.m,u.mI,30,"When you explain roots, take the children outside to touch a real plant. Abstract ideas are easier to grasp through touch.",A.aV0,A.aKq,A.b2x,A.aPF,!1,!1,"Ask the children to draw one whole plant, label each part with what it does, then explain it to a friend.","Hold up a real plant. Point to the roots, stem and leaves one by one; have the children repeat each name while touching it.",u.j)
A.QU=new B.f(A.Z,"Why are they all different?","Fish have fins, birds have wings \u2014 why?",A.E,"Everyone, have a think: fish have fins, birds have wings, chickens have claws. Why are animal bodies all so different?",u.R,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Od=new B.f(A.Y,"Look at their bodies","Every animal has special body parts.",A.v,"Every animal has body parts that suit the place where it lives. Tell me an animal and a body part it has!","If there are small animals nearby (ants, chickens), observe them together.","Draw 1 simple fish and 1 simple bird. Point to the fins and wings.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bas=new B.m("sirip","\ud83d\udc1f","Fins","FINS: help a fish swim and turn in the water. Plastic rubbish in rivers and the sea can tangle round fins \u2014 so put rubbish in the bin!")
A.bb9=new B.m("sayap","\ud83d\udc26","Wings","WINGS: let a bird fly. Its feathers are light so it can glide easily through the air.")
A.bax=new B.m("cakar","\ud83d\udc14","Claws","CLAWS: for scratching the ground for food and for perching. Just right for life on land.")
A.bcS=new B.m("ekor","\ud83d\udc31","Tail","TAIL: keeps balance when moving and jumping. A cat uses its tail so it doesn't fall.")
A.aQQ=s([A.bas,A.bb9,A.bax,A.bcS],t.w)
A.RD=new B.f(A.W,"What does each part do?","Tap each part \u2014 find out what it does.",A.v,"Let's find out what each animal body part is for!",null,"Write each part's job next to it on your drawing.",A.aQQ,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Nd=new B.f(A.X,"Move like an animal!","Copy how your favourite animal moves.",A.v,"Now it's your turn to be animals!","Have the children copy how animals move: swim like a fish, flap like a bird, hop like a rabbit. Ask which body part they are using.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b_9=new B.D("All animals have legs for walking.","Ask back: do fish have legs? Snakes? Many animals move without legs \u2014 using fins, their belly, or wings, to suit where they live.")
A.b0Q=new B.D("Birds cannot live in water because they are bad swimmers.","Ducks are actually good swimmers. What stops birds living UNDER the water: they breathe with lungs and need air, whereas fish have gills for breathing in water.")
A.aPJ=s([A.b_9,A.b0Q],t.I)
A.PU=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Which part does a fish use to swim?",A.E,"Now it's your turn to explain to Si Jabrik. Which body part does a fish use to swim? And why don't birds live under the water?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aPJ)
A.Pf=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","An ant can lift 50x its body weight!",A.a_,"Now we can answer our question from the start: fish have gills to breathe in water, but chickens have lungs. Here's the amazing bit: a tiny ant can lift things up to 50 times its own body weight \u2014 because its muscles are very strong for its size! Animal bodies are amazing, aren't they? Tonight, look for a house gecko on a wall or ceiling at home. Watch closely: how can it walk upside down without falling?",u.pb,null,null,!1,null,"The Komodo dragons of Komodo and Rinca islands in East Nusa Tenggara are the largest lizards in the world. Scientists have found that the tips of their teeth are coated with orange iron, which helps keep them sharp.","A gecko's toes are covered in super-fine hairs that cling to walls with no glue. Engineers in many countries copy this \u2014 it's called biomimicry \u2014 to make dry adhesives and wall-climbing robots.",null)
A.aP5=s([A.QU,A.Od,A.RD,A.Nd,A.PU,A.Pf],t.n)
A.aUE=s(["Sharp eyes","Exercise book & pencil for notes","Animals nearby: ants, chickens, cats, fish, or house geckos"],t.s)
A.aW1=s(["Choose one animal near the classroom or at home.","Observe: what body parts does it have?","Look closely: which parts does it use to move? To eat?","Draw the animal and label each of its body parts."],t.s)
A.a0l=new B.J("Animal Detectives Around Us","An animal's body parts match where it lives and how it eats.",A.aUE,A.aW1,"Each part of an animal's body has a job that suits where it lives: fins for water, wings for the air, legs for land. This is how animals survive in their surroundings.",A.D,"Only watch \u2014 do not disturb or touch wild animals.",15)
A.aVy=s(['"Beak" tools: tongs/tweezers, a spoon, chopsticks','"Food": peanuts, rice, bits of paper, water in a bowl'],t.s)
A.aNN=s(['Try to pick up each "food" using only the tweezers.',"Repeat with the spoon, then the chopsticks.","Record which tool works best for each food.","Make the link: birds' beaks differ to suit their food."],t.s)
A.ZD=new B.J("Bird Beak Simulation","The shape of an animal's body parts suits the way it eats.",A.aVy,A.aNN,"Each tool suits a certain food \u2014 tweezers for small seeds, a spoon for scooping water. Birds' beaks are the same: pointed beaks for seeds, wide beaks for filtering water. An animal's body shape suits the way it eats.",A.D,"Peanuts & seeds are not for eating \u2014 they can cause choking. Check for peanut allergies first; if a child is allergic, use other seeds instead (e.g. dried corn).",15)
A.aVY=s([A.a0l,A.ZD],t.T)
A.b6s=new B.aD("Cannot name an animal's body parts yet, or names them without linking them to what they do.","Names body parts (fins/wings/legs), but does not yet link them to where the animal lives.",'Names 3 parts AND explains why they suit where the animal lives \u2014 e.g. "fins for swimming in water".')
A.b24=new B.aC(u.o5,u.gh,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question \u2014 why animal bodies are so different. Let the children guess with no right or wrong answers.","Main activity (20 minutes): Observe and identify the body parts of several animals (fins, wings, legs, tail) and what they do. Have the children copy how the animals move and name the body part they used.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers to check understanding. End with a fun fact: an ant can lift up to 50 times its own body weight.","Pupils can name at least 3 animal body parts and explain how they suit the place where the animal lives.",A.b2,u.T,A.b6s)
A.bm8=new B.aw("bagian_tubuh_hewan","Parts of an Animal's Body","\ud83d\udc3e",A.aM,A.bd,u.o5,u.gh,30,"Use animals the children know \u2014 chickens, cats, fish, ants. Have them copy how the animals move; moving their own bodies makes the idea more real.",A.aP5,A.aVY,A.b24,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to choose one animal and guess which body part helps it survive where it lives.",'Compare two very different animals (fish vs chicken). Point to one body part and ask: "what is this for?"',u.j)
A.Tp=new B.f(A.Z,"It used to be a caterpillar?!","This beautiful butterfly was once a caterpillar.",A.E,"Everyone, look at this butterfly. Would you believe it was once a caterpillar crawling on a leaf? How could it change like that?","Let the children react and guess. Many of them will be surprised!",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.SO=new B.f(A.Y,"The stages","Egg \u2192 caterpillar \u2192 chrysalis \u2192 butterfly.",A.v,"Look at this butterfly. Did it have wings when it was little? Has anyone ever seen a caterpillar on a leaf, or tiny mosquito wrigglers swimming in a bucket? What do you think caterpillars have to do with butterflies \u2014 and wrigglers with mosquitoes?",null,"Draw 4 circles in order: egg \u2192 caterpillar \u2192 chrysalis \u2192 butterfly. Join them with arrows in a circle (a cycle).",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bf7=new B.m("telur","\ud83e\udd5a","Egg","EGG: the mother butterfly lays tiny eggs under a leaf. This is where it all begins.")
A.beS=new B.m("ulat","\ud83d\udc1b","Caterpillar","CATERPILLAR: the egg hatches into a caterpillar. Its job is to eat leaves non-stop so it grows big.")
A.bcb=new B.m("kepompong","\ud83d\udfe4","Chrysalis","CHRYSALIS: the caterpillar wraps itself up and keeps still. Inside, its body changes completely.")
A.baY=new B.m("kupukupu","\ud83e\udd8b","Butterfly","BUTTERFLY: a winged butterfly comes out of the chrysalis. Then it lays eggs again \u2014 the cycle repeats!")
A.aVw=s([A.bf7,A.beS,A.bcb,A.baY],t.w)
A.O5=new B.f(A.W,"What happens at each stage?","Tap each stage \u2014 find out.",A.v,"Let's follow each stage of a butterfly's life!",null,"Write the features of each stage next to its drawing.",A.aVw,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Pt=new B.f(A.X,"Be a butterfly!","Act out the life cycle with your body.",A.v,"Now let's act it out and become butterflies!",'Have the children act out each stage: curl up like an egg, crawl like a caterpillar, keep still and wrapped up like a chrysalis, then "fly" with arms spread wide like a butterfly. Or draw the sequence in their books.',null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.aZM=new B.D("A chrysalis is just a caterpillar that is sleeping.","Inside the chrysalis the caterpillar is not just sleeping \u2014 its body changes completely into a butterfly. It is not an ordinary rest.")
A.b_0=new B.D("All animals change shape like butterflies.","Not all of them. Chicks and kittens already look like their mothers when they are little and just grow bigger. Only some animals, like butterflies and frogs, change completely.")
A.b_K=new B.D("The wrigglers in the bathroom water tank are little worms.","They are baby mosquitoes! Mosquitoes go through metamorphosis too: egg, wriggler (larva), pupa, then adult mosquito \u2014 in about a week. That's why we drain and scrub the water tank once a week and cover water containers, so the wrigglers never get the chance to become mosquitoes.")
A.aPg=s([A.aZM,A.b_0,A.b_K],t.I)
A.OQ=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Which stage comes after the caterpillar?",A.E,"Si Jabrik still isn't sure. Can you help him? Which stage comes after the caterpillar? And do all animals change like this? What about chickens?","Ask the children to raise their hands. (Hint: chickens do not go through metamorphosis \u2014 a chick looks like its mother and just grows bigger.)",null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aPg)
A.Pc=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Butterflies taste with their feet!",A.a_,"Remember our question \u2014 does a butterfly have wings from the moment it's born? Now you know the answer! Listen carefully to this fact. Butterflies taste their food with their FEET, not their tongues! As soon as their feet touch a flower, they know straight away whether it's sweet or not. Amazing, isn't it? Tomorrow, check the buckets or water containers at home: are there wrigglers going up and down to the surface to breathe? If there are, ask a grown-up to tip the water away.","A round of applause for the butterfly's journey!",null,null,!1,null,"The black soldier fly also goes through complete metamorphosis. In many places in Indonesia, its larvae (maggots) are raised to eat up food scraps, then become feed for fish and chickens \u2014 yet the adult flies don't eat at all!","Monarch butterflies in North America fly up to about 4,000 km to the mountains of Mexico to get through the winter. The ones that fly back north are their children, grandchildren and great-grandchildren \u2014 they have never made the trip before, yet they still know the way!",null)
A.aOm=s([A.Tp,A.SO,A.O5,A.Pt,A.OQ,A.Pc],t.n)
A.aW4=s(["1 caterpillar (one found on a leaf)","A clean jar + a lid with air holes","Fresh leaves from where the caterpillar was found (that is its food)"],t.s)
A.aRK=s(["Put the caterpillar and the leaves it was found on into the jar.","Make air holes in the lid. Change to fresh leaves every day.","Place it in the shade. Observe it every day.","Wait: the caterpillar forms a chrysalis, then becomes a butterfly. Release it outdoors!"],t.s)
A.a_w=new B.J("A Caterpillar House: Watch It Become a Butterfly","Watch metamorphosis happen: caterpillar \u2192 chrysalis \u2192 butterfly.",A.aW4,A.aRK,"A caterpillar eats a lot to store up energy, then changes completely inside the chrysalis into a butterfly. This is complete metamorphosis \u2014 one animal, four different forms.",A.D,"Do not touch hairy caterpillars \u2014 they can cause itching or a rash. Move the caterpillar on a leaf or twig. Wash hands after handling. Release the butterfly once it has come out.",20160)
A.awA=s(["Paper & pencils/crayons to make the cards","Scissors"],t.s)
A.aW5=s(["Draw the stages of a butterfly's life cycle on separate cards: egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, butterfly.","Shuffle the cards, then ask the children to put them in the right order.","Also make chicken cards: egg \u2192 chick \u2192 adult chicken.","Compare: the butterfly changes shape, the chicken just gets bigger."],t.s)
A.Z_=new B.J("Life Cycle Order Cards","Comparing life cycles with and without metamorphosis.",A.awA,A.aW5,"Some animals go through metamorphosis (completely changing shape, like a butterfly), others do not (a chick looks like its mother and just grows bigger). Ordering the cards helps children understand the stages and compare them.",A.D,"Take care with scissors; use blunt children's scissors.",20)
A.aMO=s(["A few tadpoles from a rice field/pond (COLLECTED BY THE TEACHER)","A container of pond water + a few water plants"],t.s)
A.aKF=s(["Put the tadpoles in the container of pond water and keep it in a shady spot.","Change the water regularly to keep it clean.","Observe every few days: the back legs grow, then the front legs, and the tail shrinks.","Once they are little frogs, RELEASE them back into the wild."],t.s)
A.Zr=new B.J("Tadpole to Frog","Frogs go through metamorphosis: egg \u2192 tadpole \u2192 frog.",A.aMO,A.aKF,"Frogs go through metamorphosis too: eggs hatch into tadpoles that live in water and breathe with gills, then change into frogs that can live on land. You can watch these changes for yourself in the rice fields.",A.D,"The teacher collects the tadpoles & water from the rice field/pond. Pond water is not for drinking. Wash hands after handling pond water. Release the frogs into the wild.",43200)
A.aAv=s([A.a_w,A.Z_,A.Zr],t.T)
A.b6K=new B.aD("Cannot order the stages yet, or thinks a butterfly is born with wings.","Orders egg\u2192caterpillar\u2192chrysalis\u2192butterfly, but does not yet name a feature of each stage.","Orders the 4 stages AND explains a feature of each stage in their own words.")
A.b1m=new B.aC(u.n5,u.cl,"Opening (5 minutes): Show a butterfly and reveal that it used to be a caterpillar. Let the children be surprised and guess how it could change.","Main activity (20 minutes): Trace the stages egg \u2192 caterpillar \u2192 chrysalis \u2192 butterfly and the features of each stage. Have the children act out the life cycle with their bodies or draw the sequence.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers \u2014 compare with an animal that does not go through metamorphosis (a chicken). End with the fact that butterflies taste with their feet.","Pupils can put the stages of butterfly metamorphosis in the correct order and name a feature of each stage.",A.b2,u.T,A.b6K)
A.bm1=new B.aw("daur_hidup_hewan","Animal Life Cycles","\ud83e\udd8b",A.aM,A.bd,u.n5,u.cl,30,'If possible, show a real caterpillar or tadpole. Many children do not know a butterfly used to be a caterpillar \u2014 this "wow!" moment really sticks.',A.aOm,A.aAv,A.b1m,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to compare the life cycle of a butterfly with a chicken \u2014 which one changes shape completely, and which one just grows bigger.","Use 4 picture cards (egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, butterfly). Have the children put them in order again and again until it comes easily.",u.j)
A.QM=new B.f(A.Z,"Plants never eat?","We eat for energy. Where do plants get theirs?",A.E,"Everyone, we have to eat so we have energy. But we never see plants eating, do we? So where do they get the energy to grow big?",u.n,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.MP=new B.f(A.Y,"The leaf: a plant's kitchen","Leaves make food from sunlight.",A.v,"We never feed that plant in the pot, and it never goes out looking for food. But it keeps on growing. Where do you think it gets its food? And why do its leaves almost always face the light?",null,'Draw a leaf, with an arrow for sunlight coming in, an arrow for water rising from the roots, and an arrow for air coming in. Write "food + oxygen" as the result.',null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bar=new B.m("cahaya","\u2600\ufe0f","Light","SUNLIGHT: the energy for cooking food in the leaf. Without light, photosynthesis stops.")
A.bcK=new B.m("air_daun","\ud83d\udca7","Water","WATER: taken in by the roots, it travels up the stem to the leaves to be used for making food.")
A.bes=new B.m("udara_co2","\ud83d\udca8","Air","AIR: leaves take in carbon dioxide \u2014 the gas that also comes out in our breath and from motorbike exhausts \u2014 to use in photosynthesis.")
A.bbi=new B.m("oksigen","\ud83e\udee7","Oxygen","OXYGEN: a by-product of photosynthesis, released into the air \u2014 it's what we breathe in!")
A.aPE=s([A.bar,A.bcK,A.bes,A.bbi],t.w)
A.Sw=new B.f(A.W,"What goes in & what comes out","Tap each card to find out.",A.v,"Let's find out what photosynthesis uses and what it makes!",null,"Label the inputs and products on the leaf drawing.",A.aPE,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Mc=new B.f(A.X,"Cover part of a leaf!","Cover a leaf with dark card. Check 3 days later.",A.v,"Now let's prove that leaves need light!","Cover part of one living leaf with dark card or a banana leaf, clipped tightly. After 3\u20134 days in the sun, uncover it. Ask the children to compare the covered part (pale) with the uncovered part (green).",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b_F=new B.D("Plants get ready-made food from the soil through their roots.","Plants make their own food in their leaves using sunlight (photosynthesis). Roots take in water, not ready-made food.")
A.aZ3=new B.D("Plants only give off carbon dioxide; they don't make oxygen.","Plants do breathe too, just like us. But in sunlight, plants photosynthesise and make much more oxygen than they use. That's why trees make the air feel fresher.")
A.aPh=s([A.b_F,A.aZ3],t.I)
A.Oc=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","What does a leaf need to make food?",A.E,"Si Jabrik isn't sure yet. Can you help him? What does a leaf need to make food? And which gas do plants give off that is useful for us?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aPh)
A.N3=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","The oxygen you breathe is made by plants!",A.a_,"We've answered our question from the start: plants make their own food from light. Now listen carefully to this fact. Every time you breathe in, most of the oxygen you take in was made by plants through photosynthesis \u2014 especially big trees and algae in the sea. So looking after plants means looking after the air we breathe! Tomorrow, with a grown-up, look at some grass that has been covered by a board, bucket or mat for a long time. What colour is it \u2014 and why?","A round of applause for the plants that keep us breathing!",null,null,!1,null,"Indonesia has the largest area of mangrove forest in the world \u2014 more than a fifth of all the mangroves on Earth. Mangroves take in carbon dioxide through photosynthesis and store lots of carbon in the coastal mud.","Rice and maize both photosynthesise, but maize has a way of doing it that uses less water and works faster in strong sunshine. Scientists from many countries are still trying to give rice maize's way of doing it \u2014 if they succeed, the same rice field could grow more rice.",null)
A.aPk=s([A.QM,A.MP,A.Sw,A.Mc,A.Oc,A.N3],t.n)
A.aww=s(["A living plant with wide leaves, in a pot or garden","A dark cover: dark card/thick dark paper or a banana leaf folded in two","Paper clips, safety pins or string to fasten it"],t.s)
A.aKU=s(["Choose one leaf that is still attached to the plant.","Cover part of the leaf (e.g. the middle) with the dark cover, clipped tightly so no light gets in.","Leave the plant in the sun for 3\u20134 days.","Remove the cover: the covered part looks paler/more yellow than the part that got light.","\ud83d\udca1 If the difference is faint: use a leaf from a fast-growing plant (e.g. spinach or water spinach), cover it really tightly, and compare it up close with the uncovered part."],t.s)
A.a_e=new B.J("The Leaf Kept from the Light","Leaves need sunlight to make food (photosynthesis).",A.aww,A.aKU,"Leaves use sunlight, water and air (carbon dioxide) to make their own food \u2014 this process is called photosynthesis. The covered part of the leaf gets no light, so it cannot make its green pigment and food properly, and it turns pale.",A.D,"Do not damage the whole plant; cover just one leaf.",5760)
A.aTR=s(["A water plant from a clean pond/rice field/ditch (COLLECTED BY THE TEACHER) \u2014 Hydrilla, pondweed, young water hyacinth, or water spinach also work","A clear glass/container of water","A sunny spot"],t.s)
A.aS5=s(["Put a few stems of the water plant into the glass of water.","Place it in direct sunlight.","Observe for a few minutes: tiny bubbles appear from the leaves.","Compare with a plant placed somewhere dark (few or no bubbles).","\ud83d\udca1 If no bubbles appear: do it in the middle of the day when the sun is strong, and use a fresh, healthy water plant \u2014 any pond/aquarium plant works, it does not have to be Hydrilla."],t.s)
A.Z3=new B.J("Bubbles from Water Plants","Water plants give off oxygen bubbles during photosynthesis.",A.aTR,A.aS5,"In light, water plants photosynthesise and give off oxygen as bubbles. In the dark, photosynthesis stops, so there are fewer bubbles \u2014 evidence that light is needed and that oxygen is produced.",A.D,"The teacher collects the plants & water from the pond/rice field/ditch. This water is not for drinking. Wash hands after handling the plants & pond water.",30)
A.aMK=s([A.a_e,A.Z3],t.T)
A.l9=s(["Critical reasoning","Caring for the environment"],t.s)
A.b76=new B.aD("Does not yet know that plants make their own food.","Knows plants need light/water, but does not yet give all the inputs or the products.","Names the inputs of photosynthesis (light, water, air) AND its products (food + oxygen).")
A.b1K=new B.aC("Pupils can explain that plants make their own food with the help of sunlight (photosynthesis).",u.fm,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Compare plants with people, who have to eat to get energy.","Main activity (20 minutes): Explain photosynthesis \u2014 leaves use light + water + air to make food, and give off oxygen. Start the experiment of covering a leaf with a dark cover to prove the role of light.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: plants make the oxygen we breathe \u2014 we and plants need each other.","Pupils can name what photosynthesis needs (light, water, air) and what it produces (food + oxygen).",A.l9,u.T,A.b76)
A.blI=new B.aw("fotosintesis","Photosynthesis","\ud83c\udf43",A.aM,A.bd,"Pupils can explain that plants make their own food with the help of sunlight.",u.fm,30,'Link this to the lesson on parts of a plant: the leaf is the "kitchen". The covered-leaf experiment takes several days \u2014 start it early.',A.aPk,A.aMK,A.b1K,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to explain why leaves are green (chlorophyll) and what happens to a plant kept in a dark place.","Compare two plants: one in the light, one in a dark cupboard for a few days. See how the dark one turns pale \u2014 leaves need light to make food.",u.j)
A.SY=new B.f(A.Z,"Tiny seed, big plant?","A bean this tiny \u2014 can it become a plant?",A.E,"Everyone, look at this tiny mung bean. Would you believe a whole plant can grow from something this small? What do you think it needs to start growing?","Let the children guess what it needs. There are no wrong answers yet.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.O_=new B.f(A.Y,"Inside a seed","Inside a seed: a baby plant + its packed lunch.",A.v,"Look at this little seed. It's hard, it's dry, and it just sits there. But if we plant it, a few days later a green shoot comes out. The bean sprouts sold at the market are mung beans that have just germinated, too! What do you think makes this sleepy seed wake up and start to grow?",null,"Draw a seed cut in half: inside are a baby root and baby leaves. Draw an arrow to a picture of a small shoot.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bea=new B.m("air","\ud83d\udca7","Water","WATER: wakes the seed up and softens its skin so the shoot can get out.")
A.bcG=new B.m("udara","\ud83d\udca8","Air","AIR: a living seed still breathes. That's why a seed completely under water can rot.")
A.beM=new B.m("hangat","\ud83c\udf21\ufe0f","Warmth","WARMTH: the right temperature helps a seed grow. Too cold, and the seed sleeps longer.")
A.bc_=new B.m("tunas","\ud83c\udf3f","Shoot","SHOOT: after the root grows down, the shoot grows up towards the light and becomes leaves.")
A.aP9=s([A.bea,A.bcG,A.beM,A.bc_],t.w)
A.QL=new B.f(A.W,"What does a seed need?","Tap each card to find out.",A.v,"Let's find out everything a seed needs to grow!",null,"Write what a seed needs on the blackboard.",A.aP9,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Sg=new B.f(A.X,"Plant it on cotton wool!","Put beans on wet cotton wool, look every day.",A.v,"Now we're going to be mini farmers!","Wet the cotton wool in a clear cup and put mung beans on top. Keep it damp. Ask the children to observe and draw the changes every day for several days.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.aYE=new B.D("The leaves come out of the seed first.","The root comes out first to look for water, then the shoot and leaves appear. Have the children watch the sprouts day by day.")
A.b_y=new B.D("A seed needs soil to grow.","Seeds can germinate on wet cotton wool with no soil; what they need is water, air and warmth. Soil gives support and nutrients later.")
A.aTN=s([A.aYE,A.b_y],t.I)
A.PP=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Which part comes out of the seed first?",A.E,"Who wants to help Si Jabrik? When a seed starts to grow, which part comes out first \u2014 the root or the leaves? And what does a seed need to start growing?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aTN)
A.Q7=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Roots always grow down, even if the seed is flipped!",A.a_,"We've answered our question from the start: a seed needs water, air and the right place. Now listen carefully to this fact. Even if a seed is put in upside down, its root still grows DOWN and its shoot grows UP. Plants can \"feel\" which way is down (where the water is) and which way is up (where the light is). So clever \u2014 and they don't even have eyes! Tonight, soak a few seeds from the kitchen (beans or corn) in water. Tomorrow morning, compare them with dry seeds: which are bigger?","A round of applause for the amazing little seed!",null,null,!1,null,"Bogor Botanic Gardens, founded in 1817, look after thousands of kinds of plants. Researchers at Indonesia's botanic gardens also sow the seeds of rare plants so that those kinds do not die out.","On Svalbard, icy islands near the North Pole that belong to Norway, there is a seed vault inside a mountain. More than a million seed samples from all over the world are kept frozen there at about \u221218 \xb0C, so they can still be planted decades from now.",null)
A.aR0=s([A.SY,A.O_,A.QL,A.Sg,A.PP,A.Q7],t.n)
A.aWk=s(["A few mung beans","Cotton wool or wet tissue","A clear plastic cup"],t.s)
A.aPf=s(["Wet the cotton wool and put it at the bottom of the clear cup.","Place a few mung beans on top of the wet cotton wool.","Put the cup in a bright place and keep the cotton wool damp (not flooded).","Observe every day: on days 1\u20132 a root comes out, then a shoot and small leaves."],t.s)
A.ZI=new B.J("Mung Beans on Cotton Wool","A seed grows into a plant if it has water, air and a warm place.",A.aWk,A.aPf,"Inside a seed there is a baby plant and its food store. With water, air and warmth, the seed germinates: first a root comes out, then a shoot and leaves. This is the start of a plant's life \u2014 and you can watch it without any soil.",A.D,"Wash hands after handling the wet cotton wool and the seeds.",2880)
A.aVD=s(["2 cups + cotton wool","Mung bean seeds (the same number in each cup)"],t.s)
A.aUx=s(["Cup A: wet the cotton wool. Cup B: leave the cotton wool DRY.","Put the same number of seeds in both cups and place them side by side in the same spot.","Keep cotton wool A damp; keep B dry.","Observe for a few days: only the seeds on the wet cotton wool grow."],t.s)
A.ZG=new B.J("Do Seeds Need Water?","A fair test: the only difference is the water.",A.aVD,A.aUx,"Because everything was kept the same except the water, any difference in the results must be caused by the water. The seeds on wet cotton wool sprout; the dry ones do not. This is how scientists test one factor \u2014 it is called a fair test.",A.D,"Wash hands after handling the cotton wool & seeds.",2880)
A.aKs=s([A.ZI,A.ZG],t.T)
A.b75=new B.aD("Does not yet know what a seed needs to grow.","Names one thing a seed needs (water/air/warmth), but cannot yet put the growth stages in order.","Names 2 things a seed needs AND puts seed \u2192 root \u2192 shoot in order.")
A.b1x=new B.aC(u.cf,u.aA,"Opening (5 minutes): Show some dry mung beans. Ask the children whether this tiny thing can become a plant, and what it needs.","Main activity (20 minutes): Discuss what a seed needs (water, air, warmth). Start the mung-beans-on-cotton-wool experiment. Observe and record the changes every day for several days.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: roots always grow down and shoots grow up, even if the seed is placed upside down.","Pupils can name at least 2 things a seed needs to grow and put seed \u2192 root \u2192 shoot in order.",A.b2,u.Z,A.b75)
A.bm5=new B.aw("perkecambahan_biji","Seed Germination","\ud83c\udf31",A.aM,A.bd,u.cf,u.aA,30,"Start the experiment early in the week so the children can see the root and shoot appear a few days later. Daily changes get them excited.",A.aR0,A.aKs,A.b1x,null,!0,!1,"Ask the children to record the height of the sprout every day for 5 days and make a simple bar chart of it.",'Put mung beans on wet cotton wool; observe them together every day \u2014 root first, then shoot. Say "roots first, leaves later".',u.j)
A.PF=new B.f(A.Z,"There, but invisible?","Wave your hand \u2014 feel the breeze. What is that?",A.E,"Everyone, wave your hand in front of your face. You can feel something touching you, can't you? But you can't see it. What is it?","Have all the children wave their hands and feel the breeze together.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Sl=new B.f(A.Y,"Three states","Things can be solid, liquid or gas.",A.v,"Hold a stone in your hand, then pour a little water into your palm. What's the difference? The stone keeps its shape, but the water takes the shape of your palm. Now blow on your hand \u2014 you can feel something, but you can't see it. How many different \"forms\" of things do you think there are in the world?","Ask the children to touch a table (solid) and drinking water (liquid).","Make 3 columns on the blackboard: SOLID | LIQUID | GAS. Fill them in together with the children.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bdQ=new B.m("padat","\ud83e\uddca","Solid","SOLID: keeps its shape even when moved. Examples: stone, table, ice. Its pieces are packed tight and can't move easily.")
A.bcJ=new B.m("cair","\ud83d\udca7","Liquid","LIQUID: flows and takes the shape of its container. Examples: water, oil, milk. It can be poured from a glass onto a plate.")
A.be5=new B.m("gas","\ud83d\udca8","Gas","GAS: fills the whole space and is usually invisible. Examples: the air inside a ball, water vapour. It's what we feel when we wave our hands.")
A.aPu=s([A.bdQ,A.bcJ,A.be5],t.w)
A.OW=new B.f(A.W,"What is each state like?","Tap each state \u2014 find out its features.",A.v,"Let's find out what makes each state special!",null,"Write the features of each state in its own column.",A.aPu,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.QE=new B.f(A.X,"Sort them out!","Sort things in the class: solid / liquid / gas.",A.v,"Now it's your turn to be scientists who sort things!","Name a few things (a book, water, a balloon, a stone, sweet soy sauce) and have the children guess the state by raising their hands for each state.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.aZB=new B.D("Air is empty \u2014 it isn't anything.","Remember feeling the breeze when you waved your hand? Air is a gas \u2014 it is real and has weight, it is just invisible.")
A.aZE=new B.D("Ice is a different substance from water.","Ice and water are the SAME substance, just in a different state. Cool water \u2192 it becomes ice; melt ice \u2192 it turns back into water.")
A.aYF=new B.D("Smoke from a cooking fire is a gas, just like air.","Smoke isn't only gas. It has tiny solid bits (soot) floating in the air \u2014 that's why kitchen walls can turn black. When forests or fields burn, those bits make the sky look blurry: that is smoke haze.")
A.aKD=s([A.aZB,A.aZE,A.aYF],t.I)
A.Qi=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","What state is water? And when it freezes?",A.E,"Who wants to help Si Jabrik? What state is water? And if we put water in the freezer until it freezes, what state does it become?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aKD)
A.Rh=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Air has weight \u2014 a whole classroom of it is \xb1250 kg!",A.a_,'Now we can answer our question from the start: air really is there, even though we can\'t see it. Get ready for a surprise. That "empty" air actually has weight! The air inside one classroom can weigh more than 250 kilograms \u2014 as much as several grown-ups. Gas is real!',u.D,null,null,!1,null,"Inside the LPG cylinders used in many kitchens in Indonesia, the gas is squeezed until it turns into a LIQUID. When the stove tap is opened, that liquid turns back into a gas that can burn.",'There is a fourth state: plasma \u2014 a gas so hot that it glows, like lightning and the Sun. In several countries, scientists have built giant machines to make a "mini Sun" out of plasma to produce electricity, but it is not yet used in everyday life.',null)
A.aPe=s([A.PF,A.Sl,A.OW,A.QE,A.Qi,A.Rh],t.n)
A.aQi=s(["A few ice cubes","A glass/small pan","A stove or hot water (HANDLED BY THE TEACHER)"],t.s)
A.aLZ=s(["Put the ice cubes in the glass. This is the SOLID state \u2014 you can hold it, and its shape stays the same.","Leave it. The ice melts into water. This is the LIQUID state \u2014 it flows and takes the shape of the glass.",'Heat the water until it boils (the teacher handles this). The water turns into water vapour \u2014 the GAS state, which is invisible. The white "steam" we see is really tiny water droplets, where the vapour has cooled and condensed again.',"Point out: the same thing (water) changes into three states just because of heat."],t.s)
A.a_i=new B.J("The Three States of Water","The same substance can be a solid, a liquid and a gas (Discover beat).",A.aQi,A.aLZ,"Water can be ice (solid), water (liquid) and water vapour (gas) \u2014 depending on the temperature. Everything around us is in one of these three states. The difference is how tightly packed its tiny pieces are and how freely they move.",A.ct,"ONLY the teacher heats the water. Children watch from a safe distance.",30)
A.aVx=s(["An empty plastic bottle","1\u20132 teaspoons of baking soda","Some vinegar + 1 balloon"],t.s)
A.aDT=s(["Put the baking soda into the balloon (use a funnel).","Pour vinegar into the bottle (about 1/4 full).","Stretch the balloon's mouth over the bottle's mouth without spilling what is inside.","Lift the balloon so the baking soda drops into the vinegar \u2014 the balloon blows itself up!"],t.s)
A.a_Z=new B.J("The Self-Inflating Balloon","A reaction makes a gas; the gas takes up space & inflates the balloon.",A.aVx,A.aDT,"Baking soda (a base) and vinegar (an acid) react to make carbon dioxide gas. Gas is a state of matter that takes up space: the more gas is made, the more it fills the bottle and the balloon, so the balloon inflates \u2014 proof that gas is real even though we cannot see it.",A.D,"The vinegar & the mixture are not for drinking. Keep them away from the eyes. Throw away burst or flat balloons straight away (they can be swallowed); watch for children with a latex allergy.",10)
A.aQK=s([A.a_i,A.a_Z],t.T)
A.b6i=new B.aD('Cannot tell the states apart yet, or thinks gas/air "isn\'t there".',"Sorts solids and liquids, but is still unsure about gases or the features.","Sorts 3 things correctly AND names the features of each state.")
A.b1t=new B.aC("Pupils can tell apart the three states of matter (solid, liquid, gas) and give an example of each from their surroundings.",u.jY,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Wave your hand so the children feel the breeze \u2014 proof that air (a gas) is real even though it is invisible.","Main activity (20 minutes): Sort things in the classroom into solid, liquid and gas. Discuss their features: solids keep their shape, liquids take the shape of their container, gases fill the whole space. Show water as an example that can be all three.",'Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers to check understanding. End with a fact: the water vapour from cooking is the same water, it has just changed state into an invisible gas; the white "steam" we see is tiny water droplets that have condensed again.',"Pupils can name the features of each state and sort at least 3 things into the correct state.",A.b2,u.T,A.b6i)
A.blx=new B.aw("wujud_benda","States of Matter","\ud83d\udca7",A.aM,A.ci,"Pupils can tell apart the three states of matter (solid, liquid, gas) and give an example of each.",u.jY,30,"Use things right in front of them: a table (solid), drinking water (liquid), air you wave at them (gas). Concrete first, then the names of the states.",A.aPe,A.aQK,A.b1t,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to find 5 things in the classroom and sort them into solid/liquid/gas, then explain their features.","Hold up three real examples: a stone (solid), water (liquid), a wave of the hand (gas). Repeat the name of each state while touching/feeling it.",u.u)
A.Oa=new B.f(A.Z,"Where did the water go?","Wet clothes on the line get dry. Where did the water go?",A.E,"Everyone, when we hang wet clothes out to dry, after a while they get dry. But nobody wrung them out. So where did the water in the clothes go?",u.R,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.QB=new B.f(A.Y,"States can change","Heat and cold change the state of things.",A.v,"Have a think: if you leave ice cubes in a glass for a while, what do they turn into? And the water in the washing \u2014 where does it go? Things around us can change their form \u2014 let's take a closer look!",null,"Draw a triangle with SOLID \u2013 LIQUID \u2013 GAS at the corners. Add two-way arrows between the corners.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bam=new B.m("mencair","\ud83e\udee0","Melting","MELTING: solid turns into liquid because of heat. Examples: ice cubes turning into water, chocolate melting in your hand.")
A.bc8=new B.m("membeku","\ud83e\uddca","Freezing","FREEZING: liquid turns into solid because of cold. Examples: water turning into ice cubes in the freezer, syrup turning into ice lollies.")
A.beB=new B.m("menguap","\ud83d\udca8","Evaporating","EVAPORATING: liquid turns into gas because of heat. Examples: washing drying, boiling water turning into steam.")
A.bcM=new B.m("mengembun","\ud83d\udca7","Condensing","CONDENSING: gas turns into liquid because of cold. Examples: droplets on an iced glass, morning dew on leaves, a helmet visor misting up from your breath.")
A.aWd=s([A.bam,A.bc8,A.beB,A.bcM],t.w)
A.N6=new B.f(A.W,"The name of each change","Tap each change \u2014 find out.",A.v,"Let's find out the name of each change of state!",null,"Write the name of each change next to its arrow.",A.aWd,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Tg=new B.f(A.X,"Find examples!","Name an example of each change from home.",A.v,"Now it's your turn to be example hunters!","Ask the children for examples from home: the ice in iced tea melting, water evaporating from a cooking pot, morning dew on the window. Write them on the blackboard together.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b_U=new B.D("The water in the washing disappears or gets used up.","The water does not disappear \u2014 it evaporates into a gas (water vapour) and rises into the air. We just can't see it.")
A.b17=new B.D("The droplets on the iced glass leaked through from inside.","The glass is not leaking. The droplets are water vapour from the air that condensed because it touched the cold side of the glass.")
A.b_M=new B.D("The drops on a cooking-pot lid are splashes from the water being cooked.","They aren't splashes. The water being cooked evaporates into water vapour, then the vapour touches the cooler lid and condenses \u2014 just like the droplets on the iced glass.")
A.aUD=s([A.b_U,A.b17,A.b_M],t.I)
A.OI=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Washing drying \u2014 which change is that?",A.E,"Si Jabrik is confused \u2014 let's help him! The water that disappears from the washing on the line \u2014 what is that change called? And the droplets on the iced glass earlier \u2014 what was that?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aUD)
A.NE=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Evaporated water comes back down as rain!",A.a_,"Remember our question \u2014 where does the water go when the washing dries? Now you know the answer! Did you know? The water that evaporates from the washing rises up into the sky, gathers into clouds, and then falls again as RAIN. So the same water has been used over and over since the time of the dinosaurs. Maybe you have drunk water that a dinosaur once drank!",'A round of applause for water\'s amazing journey! Then set a challenge: at home, breathe a warm "haaah" onto a metal spoon or a mirror. A thin mist appears \u2014 count how many seconds it takes to disappear. Where does that water go?',null,null,!1,null,"Fishers on many Indonesian coasts take blocks of ice on their boats: as it melts, the ice takes in heat from the fish, so the fish stay fresh all the way to the harbour. Fridges and cold stores use a special liquid (a refrigerant) that evaporates inside pipes and takes in heat.","When a comet \u2014 a ball of ice and dust in space \u2014 comes close to the Sun, its ice turns straight into gas without melting first, making a glowing tail millions of kilometres long. Halley's Comet will be visible from Earth again around 2061 \u2014 maybe you will see it!",null)
A.aTg=s([A.Oa,A.QB,A.N6,A.Tg,A.OI,A.NE],t.n)
A.aQI=s(["1 glass","Ice cubes","A little water"],t.s)
A.aes=s(["Fill the glass with ice cubes and a little water.","Leave it for a few minutes in an ordinary spot.","Observe the outside of the glass: water droplets appear!","Ask the children: where did this water come from? The glass isn't leaking."],t.s)
A.a_x=new B.J("Droplets on a Cold Glass","Water vapour in the air condenses into droplets when it touches something cold.",A.aQI,A.aes,"The air around us contains invisible water vapour. When that vapour touches the cold glass, it condenses into droplets of water. This is the same process as morning dew on the grass.",A.D,null,10)
A.aV1=s(["Juice/syrup/sweet water","Small moulds or plastic cups + sticks","A freezer \u2014 if there is none: ice cubes from a small shop + salt + a big container"],t.s)
A.aep=s(["Pour the juice into the moulds/cups and stand a stick in the middle.","Put them in the freezer for a few hours.","\ud83d\udca1 No freezer? Sink the cup of juice into a container of ice cubes sprinkled with lots of salt \u2014 the salt makes the ice much colder, so the juice freezes too (this is how hand-churned ice cream is made).","Observe: the liquid turns into a solid (an ice lolly).","Leave it out \u2014 slowly it melts back into a liquid."],t.s)
A.a01=new B.J("Making Ice Lollies","A liquid turns solid when it is cooled (freezing).",A.aV1,A.aep,"When cooled in the freezer, a liquid loses heat and FREEZES into a solid. When left somewhere warm, it MELTS again. This change of state can go back and forth depending on temperature \u2014 and the result is tasty to eat!",A.D,"Use clean containers & ingredients if you want to eat them; check for food allergies or foods a child must avoid first. Ice + salt gets very cold \u2014 do not hold the container with bare hands for long (use a cloth).",240)
A.aNu=s(["3 ice cubes of the same size","Different places: held in a hand, on a table, in ordinary water"],t.s)
A.aWf=s(["Get 3 ice cubes of about the same size.","At the same moment: hold one in your hand, put one on the table, and drop one into a glass of water.","Observe which one melts fastest.","Discuss why they are different."],t.s)
A.a0i=new B.J("Melting Race","Heat makes ice melt faster.",A.aNu,A.aWf,"Ice melts faster in a warmer place. A hand and water give more heat than the air around the table, so that ice melts first. The more heat, the faster the change of state happens.",A.D,null,15)
A.aVm=s([A.a_x,A.a01,A.a0i],t.T)
A.b5Y=new B.aD('Cannot name changes of state yet, or thinks the water in the washing "just disappears".',"Names some changes (melting/freezing), but mixes up evaporating and condensing.","Names 3 changes AND gives a correct real example for each.")
A.b1o=new B.aC(u.j5,u.gj,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question about wet clothes that dry. Let the children guess where the water went.","Main activity (20 minutes): Go through the four changes of state \u2014 melting, freezing, evaporating, condensing \u2014 with everyday examples (ice melting, water freezing into ice, washing drying, droplets on a glass). Link each change to heating or cooling.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers to check understanding. End with a fact: the water in the washing does not disappear \u2014 it evaporates into a gas and rises into the air.","Pupils can name at least 3 changes of state and give one real example of each.",A.b2,u.T,A.b5Y)
A.bm9=new B.aw("perubahan_wujud","Changes of State","\ud83c\udf2b\ufe0f",A.aM,A.ci,u.j5,u.gj,30,"Link it to everyday life: the ice in iced tea melting, washing drying on the line. The children have already seen these \u2014 they just need the names.",A.aTg,A.aVm,A.b1o,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to make up the story of a drop of water's journey: freezing \u2192 melting \u2192 evaporating \u2192 condensing.","Focus first on the two most visible ones: ice melting and water evaporating. Add the other two changes once the children understand.",u.u)
A.NS=new B.f(A.Z,"An empty glass, really?","This glass looks empty. But is there really nothing in it?",A.E,"Everyone, look at this glass. It looks empty \u2014 nothing in it at all. But is it really empty, or is there something invisible inside?",u.n,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Sy=new B.f(A.Y,"Air fills space",'An "empty" glass is really full of air.',A.v,"Hold this glass \u2014 it looks empty, doesn't it? Now turn it upside down and push it straight down into a bucket of water. Watch carefully: the water won't go all the way in. What do you think is inside that \"empty\" glass?",null,"Draw a glass with little dots inside to show the air filling the space.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bev=new B.m("menempati","\ud83e\udd64","Takes up space","TAKES UP SPACE: air in an upside-down glass keeps the water out, so the tissue inside stays dry.")
A.bd8=new B.m("balon","\ud83c\udf88","Fills balloons","FILLS: balloons and balls puff up because air is blown or pumped into them. Air takes the shape of its container.")
A.bbV=new B.m("tekanan","\ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f","Has pressure","PRESSURE: air pushes in every direction. That's why air can hold back water or make a tyre feel hard.")
A.baA=new B.m("angin","\ud83c\udf43","Can move","MOVES: moving air is wind. It can push kites along and turn windmills.")
A.aMM=s([A.bev,A.bd8,A.bbV,A.baA],t.w)
A.QV=new B.f(A.W,"Proof that air is real","Tap each card to find out.",A.v,"Let's find proof that air is real and takes up space!",null,"Write the evidence for air on the blackboard.",A.aMM,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Ss=new B.f(A.X,"Magic tissue!","Dunk an upside-down glass with tissue \u2014 it stays dry!",A.v,"Now let's prove that air takes up space!","Scrunch up the tissue and push it into the bottom of the glass. Turn the glass over, push it straight down into the basin of water until it is under, lift it straight out. Take the tissue out \u2014 it's still dry! Ask the children to guess why.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.aYB=new B.D("A glass that looks empty really is empty.",'An "empty" glass is actually full of air. Air takes up space, and that is what keeps the water out so the tissue stays dry.')
A.b_h=new B.D("Air has no substance and no weight.","Air is real: it takes up space and has weight. A balloon that gets fuller as you blow it up proves air is there.")
A.aZr=new B.D("A motorbike tyre is hard because its rubber is thick.","The tyre is hard because it is full of air pressing from inside. If the tyre gets a puncture and the air escapes, it goes flat, even though the rubber is the same. That is why tyres get pumped up at the tyre-repair stall.")
A.aQn=s([A.aYB,A.b_h,A.aZr],t.I)
A.Tf=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Why does the tissue in the glass stay dry?",A.E,"Si Jabrik is confused \u2014 help him out! Why does the tissue inside the upside-down glass stay dry, even when it goes into the water? What is stopping the water?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aQn)
A.Rf=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Air presses on your body with tonnes of force!",A.a_,"Now we've answered our question from the start: the \"empty\" glass was full of air. Get ready for a surprise. The air all around us presses on our bodies from every direction with a huge force \u2014 added up, it's several TONNES! We don't get squashed flat because our bodies push back just as strongly, and we're used to it. That invisible air is really powerful!","A round of applause for the air all around us!",null,null,!1,null,"Weather forecasters at Indonesia's weather agency (BMKG) read barometers \u2014 instruments that measure air pressure \u2014 at weather stations all over Indonesia. The higher up you go, the lower the air pressure: a sealed bag of snacks can puff up when it is carried up into the mountains.","At the top of Mount Everest in the Himalayas, the highest mountain in the world, the air is so thin that most climbers carry bottled oxygen to breathe.",null)
A.aVP=s([A.NS,A.Sy,A.QV,A.Ss,A.Tf,A.Rf],t.n)
A.aQo=s(["A clear glass","Tissue/paper that can be scrunched up","A basin of water"],t.s)
A.aPP=s(["Scrunch up the tissue and push it into the bottom of the glass so it won't fall out.","Turn the glass over (mouth down) and check the tissue stays at the bottom.","Push the upside-down glass straight down into the water until it is under, then lift it straight out again.","Check the tissue: it is still DRY! The water could not get in."],t.s)
A.a_4=new B.J("Dry Tissue Under Water","Air takes up space \u2014 it stops water getting into an upside-down glass.",A.aQo,A.aPP,"Even though it looks empty, the glass is full of air. Air takes up space, so when the upside-down glass goes into the water, the air inside stops the water rising and wetting the tissue. This proves air is real and takes up space.",A.D,"Push the glass in and lift it out straight \u2014 do not tilt it \u2014 so it works.",10)
A.aNr=s(["A plastic bottle","1 balloon","A nail/needle (for the variation, used by the teacher)"],t.s)
A.aVE=s(["Push the balloon into the neck of the bottle and stretch the balloon's mouth back over the rim of the bottle.","Try to blow up the balloon inside the bottle \u2014 it hardly inflates!","As a variation, the teacher makes a hole in the bottom of the bottle; then try blowing again \u2014 now the balloon inflates.","Discuss what changed."],t.s)
A.YB=new B.J("The Balloon That Won't Blow Up","The air inside the bottle takes up space and stops the balloon inflating.",A.aNr,A.aVE,'The "empty" bottle is full of air. When we blow up the balloon inside it, the air in the bottle has nowhere to go, so it holds the balloon back. Once there is a hole, the air can escape and the balloon inflates \u2014 evidence that air takes up space.',A.D,"The teacher makes the hole in the bottle. Watch out for sharp edges. Throw away burst balloons straight away (they can be swallowed); watch for children with a latex allergy.",10)
A.aDV=s(["A glass filled to the brim with water","A stiff card/piece of cardboard to cover the mouth of the glass","A basin (just in case)"],t.s)
A.aLh=s(["Fill the glass right up with water and cover its mouth with the card.","Holding the card in place, turn the glass upside down over the basin.","Slowly take your hand away from the card.","Observe: the card & water stay put and do not spill!","\ud83d\udca1 If it spills on the first try: make sure the glass is completely FULL and the card covers the whole mouth with no bubbles \u2014 it usually works on the second try."],t.s)
A.ZX=new B.J("Water That Won't Spill","Air pressure from below holds the card & water in an upside-down glass.",A.aDV,A.aLh,"The air around us pushes in every direction. Air pressure from below pushes the card up more strongly than the weight of the water in the glass, so the card & water stay put \u2014 evidence that air has pressure.",A.D,"Do it over a basin; practise once first so it does not spill.",10)
A.aUu=s([A.a_4,A.YB,A.ZX],t.T)
A.b5P=new B.aD('Still thinks air is "nothing" and does not take up space.',"Knows air takes up space, but cannot yet point to evidence from the experiment.","Explains that air takes up space AND gives one piece of evidence from the experiment.")
A.b2p=new B.aC(u.gC,'Is an "empty" glass really empty?',"Opening (5 minutes): Show an empty glass. Ask the children whether there is anything in it. Let them give their opinions.","Main activity (20 minutes): Show that air takes up space with the glass-and-tissue experiment. Discuss how air has pressure that can hold back water. Link this to tyres and balloons filled with air.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: the air around us presses on us from every direction, but our bodies are used to it.","Pupils can explain that air takes up space and give one piece of evidence from the experiment.",A.b2,u.Z,A.b5P)
A.bl1=new B.aw("udara_tempat_tekanan","Air Takes Up Space","\ud83c\udf88",A.aM,A.ci,u.gC,'Is an "empty" glass really empty?',30,"The glass-and-tissue experiment really amazes children. Practise it once yourself first so the glass goes in straight and the tissue truly stays dry.",A.aVP,A.aUu,A.b2p,null,!0,!1,"Ask the children to design another way to prove that air fills space (for example a balloon in a bottle, or bubbles when the glass is tilted).",'Repeat the glass + tissue experiment slowly; push the glass straight down into the water, lift it out, the tissue is dry. Say "air holds back the water".',u.u)
A.Q0=new B.f(A.Z,"How does it move?","An object sits still on the table \u2014 how do we move it?",A.E,"Everyone, look at this. It's sitting still on the table. No magic allowed! So how can we make it move?","Let the children guess and act it out. No answer is wrong yet.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.LA=new B.f(A.Y,"Push and pull","Force = a push or a pull.",A.v,"To make something move, we give it a FORCE. There are two basic kinds of force: a push, away from us, and a pull, towards us. Try pushing your table, then pulling your chair!","Have the children push a wall and pull a friend's hand.","Draw a box. Add an arrow to the right (PUSH) and an arrow to the left (PULL).",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bd2=new B.m("gerak","\ud83c\udfc3","Changes motion","FORCE changes MOTION: a still object starts moving (a kicked ball), or a moving one stops (a caught ball, a bicycle braking).")
A.baP=new B.m("arah","\u21aa\ufe0f","Changes direction","FORCE changes DIRECTION: tap a rolling ball from the side and it turns. Steering a bicycle changes direction too.")
A.ba7=new B.m("bentuk","\ud83d\udfe1","Changes shape","FORCE changes SHAPE: press plasticine and it goes flat; scrunch paper and it becomes a ball. Pulling and pressing change shape.")
A.aOQ=s([A.bd2,A.baP,A.ba7],t.w)
A.MD=new B.f(A.W,"What can a force change?","Tap each card to find out.",A.v,"Let's find out all the things a force can change!",null,"Write the 3 things a force can change on the blackboard.",A.aOQ,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.RH=new B.f(A.X,"Feel the force!","Push, pull, scrunch \u2014 feel the force.",A.v,"Now it's your turn to feel a force!","Have the children push a table in pairs, pull a rope together (a mini tug-of-war) and scrunch paper. Ask: which force did you use?",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b0D=new B.D("Heavy things can't be moved; there's no force on them.","They can, they just need a bigger force. Have two children push a table together \u2014 it shows a bigger force moves a heavy object.")
A.aYC=new B.D("Kicking a ball is pulling the ball.","Kicking = pushing (away from the foot). Pulling moves an object towards you, like pulling open a drawer.")
A.aZk=new B.D("A motorbike can move by itself, without a force.","A motorbike needs a force too. Its engine turns the wheel; the wheel pushes the road backwards, and the road pushes the motorbike forwards. Without that force, the motorbike stays still.")
A.aKo=s([A.b0D,A.aYC,A.aZk],t.I)
A.PS=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Kicking a ball \u2014 is that a push or a pull?",A.E,"Now it's your turn to explain to Si Jabrik. When we kick a ball, is that a push or a pull? And what changes about the ball?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aKo)
A.Pe=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Without friction, we couldn't walk!",A.a_,"Remember our question at the start? Now you know: a still object only moves when it's pushed or pulled. And here's the surprise: we can walk because of friction between our feet and the floor. If the floor were as slippery as ice, the friction would be gone and we'd slip over! So friction can be annoying, but it's really our helper.","A round of applause for Si Jabrik and for all of you! A challenge for home: push a sandal across the floor, the ground and a mat \u2014 where does it slide most easily? Scientists guess first, then test.",null,null,!1,null,"A rocket works like a balloon rocket: gas shoots downwards and the rocket is pushed upwards. That is how SATRIA-1, an Indonesian satellite, was launched in 2023; from about 36,000 km above the equator, it helps schools and community health centres in remote villages connect to the internet.","Rockets used to be used only once. Now some rockets fire their engines again on the way down, so the push works like a brake, then land upright on their legs and can fly again.",null)
A.aLz=s([A.Q0,A.LA,A.MD,A.RH,A.PS,A.Pe],t.n)
A.aLr=s(["1 balloon","Long thread/string, about 3 metres","1 straw","Sticky tape"],t.s)
A.aVu=s(["Thread the string through the straw. Tie each end of the string to one of two chairs set far apart, and pull it straight.","Blow up the balloon and pinch the neck closed (do not tie it).","Tape the balloon to the straw.","Let go of the neck \u2014 the balloon zooms along the string!"],t.s)
A.a0N=new B.J("Balloon Rocket","A pushing force (air rushing out) makes the balloon shoot forward.",A.aLr,A.aVu,"When air shoots backwards out of the balloon, a pushing force sends the balloon the opposite way \u2014 forwards. Every push always has a push back. This is how real rockets fly.",A.D,"Watch out for bursting balloons: pick up the pieces straight away \u2014 pieces of a burst or flat balloon can be swallowed and block breathing. Young children must not put balloons in their mouths; for young children, the teacher blows up the balloons. Watch for children with a latex allergy.",15)
A.aRX=s(["A small object that can slide (toy car, small box)","A board or thick book as a ramp","Different surfaces: smooth floor, carpet/towel, sandpaper/cardboard"],t.s)
A.aAx=s(["Lean the board as a ramp on the smooth floor, then let go of the object at the top of the ramp \u2014 do not push it, so the energy is always the same.","Mark where the object stops.","Repeat on the carpet, then on the rough surface \u2014 the ramp & its height stay the same, only the surface changes (a fair test).","Compare how far it slides.","Discuss which surface makes the object stop quickly."],t.s)
A.Zw=new B.J("Sliding Race (Friction)","Rough surfaces hold back movement more strongly (more friction).",A.aRX,A.aAx,"Friction is a force that holds back movement when two surfaces rub together. Rough surfaces have more friction, so the object stops quickly. On a smooth surface there is little friction, so the object slides further.",A.D,null,15)
A.aJc=s([A.a0N,A.Zw],t.T)
A.zK=s(["Critical reasoning","Mutual cooperation"],t.s)
A.b72=new B.aD("Cannot yet tell a push from a pull, or thinks objects move by themselves without a force.","Tells a push from a pull, but does not yet say what the force changes.","Tells push from pull AND gives an example of a force changing motion, direction or shape.")
A.b2j=new B.aC(u.fa,u.fU,"Opening (5 minutes): Put an object on the table. Ask the children how to make it move. Show a push and a pull.","Main activity (20 minutes): Demonstrate pushing and pulling forces on objects. Show that a force can change motion (still to moving), direction (turning) and shape (pressing plasticine). Let the children try pushing and pulling.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with a fun fact: without friction between our feet and the floor, we could not walk \u2014 we would slip instead.","Pupils can tell a push from a pull and give one example of a force changing motion/direction/shape.",A.zK,u.T,A.b72)
A.bl_=new B.aw("gaya_dorong_tarik","Forces: Push & Pull","\u26a1",A.aM,A.bv,u.fa,u.fU,30,"Use the children's bodies: pushing a wall, pulling a friend's hand, scrunching paper. Force is abstract, but the body feels it straight away.",A.aLz,A.aJc,A.b2j,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to find 3 examples of forces around them and say whether each one is a push or a pull, and what changes.",'Use the body: push a wall, pull a friend\'s hand, scrunch paper. Say "push" or "pull" together with the children each time.',u.s)
A.Pm=new B.f(A.Z,"Where does the energy come from?","We can run & play \u2014 where does the energy come from?",A.E,"Everyone, we can run, jump and play all day long. So where does our body get the energy for all that?",'Let the children guess. Steer them towards "eating" if needed.',null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.P6=new B.f(A.Y,"Sources of energy","The sun, food and electricity give us energy.",A.v,"Let's jump on the spot ten times. Feeling tired, right? Now look at the fan spinning and the lamp shining. You need something to keep you moving, and so do the fan and the lamp. Where do you think it all comes from?",null,"Draw the sun, a plate of food and an electric plug.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.baE=new B.m("panas","\ud83d\udd25","Heat","HEAT ENERGY: makes things warm. Examples: the sun, a stove flame, an iron. Cooking needs heat energy.")
A.beF=new B.m("cahaya","\ud83d\udca1","Light","LIGHT ENERGY: lets us see. Examples: the sun, a candle flame, a torch, a solar lamp.")
A.bc0=new B.m("bunyi","\ud83d\udd0a","Sound","SOUND ENERGY: comes from vibrations. Examples: our voices, a drum being beaten, a car horn. Our ears pick it up as sound.")
A.bec=new B.m("gerak","\ud83c\udf00","Motion","MOTION ENERGY: things that move have it. Examples: wind turning a windmill, flowing water, us running.")
A.aMb=s([A.baE,A.beF,A.bc0,A.bec],t.w)
A.PE=new B.f(A.W,"Forms of energy","Tap each form to find out.",A.v,"Energy comes in lots of forms. Let's find out!",null,"Write the 4 forms of energy on the blackboard, with examples.",A.aMb,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Sm=new B.f(A.X,"Make sound & motion!","Clap, run on the spot \u2014 feel the energy.",A.v,"Now let's make some energy ourselves!","Have the children clap their hands (sound energy), run on the spot (motion energy) and rub their palms together (heat energy). Ask which form of energy they made.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b0y=new B.D("Electricity is the only source of energy.","Electricity is one source, but there are also the sun and food. Electricity itself is usually made from another source.")
A.aYO=new B.D("The sun only gives light, not heat.","The sun gives two forms of energy at once: light (bright) and heat (warm). Remember how it feels to stand in the midday sun.")
A.b0z=new B.D("A torch battery makes its own energy.","A battery only STORES energy. When we use it, the energy comes out as light from a torch or sound from a radio. When it runs out, the battery has to be replaced or recharged.")
A.aQ9=s([A.b0y,A.aYO,A.b0z],t.I)
A.Td=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","A lamp is on \u2014 what form of energy?",A.E,"Si Jabrik still isn't sure. Can you help him? When a lamp is on, what form of energy does it make? And where does our body get its energy from?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aQ9)
A.Sa=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Almost all the energy on Earth comes from the Sun!",A.a_,"We've answered our question from the start: the energy to play comes from our food. Now listen carefully to this. The energy in the rice you eat really comes from the SUN. Rice plants soak up sunlight to grow, and then we eat the rice. So when you run, you're using the sun's energy, stored in your food!","A round of applause for the Sun and for all of you!",null,null,!1,null,"Heat from deep inside volcanoes can become electricity too. At Kamojang in West Java, hot steam from deep underground has turned turbines since the 1980s \u2014 Indonesia's first geothermal power station.","An ordinary solar panel turns only about a fifth of sunlight into electricity. In laboratories in several countries, special multi-layer solar cells can already turn almost half of it into electricity \u2014 but they are still too expensive for house roofs.",null)
A.aHk=s([A.Pm,A.P6,A.PE,A.Sm,A.Td,A.Sa],t.n)
A.aOH=s(["2 used plastic cups","Thread/string, about 3\u20135 metres","A toothpick or matchstick (to tie off inside the cup)"],t.s)
A.aUO=s(["Make a hole in the bottom of each cup and push the string end through from the outside to the inside.","Inside the cup, tie the string end to a piece of toothpick so it can't slip out.","Two children hold the cups and walk apart until the string is tight and straight.","One speaks into their cup, the other holds their cup to their ear \u2014 they can hear it!"],t.s)
A.a0w=new B.J("Cup Telephone","Sound energy travels along a tight string.",A.aOH,A.aUO,"Sound is vibration. When we speak, the cup vibrates too; the vibration travels along the tight string to the other cup and goes into the ear as sound. Sound needs a material (matter) to travel through \u2014 here, the string.",A.D,"Take care with the sharp toothpick ends; the teacher should tie the toothpick to the string.",15)
A.aHl=s(["2 glasses with the same amount of water","A hot, sunny spot & a shady spot"],t.s)
A.aP6=s(["Put one glass in direct sunlight.","Put the second glass in the shade.","Wait about 1 hour.","Feel/touch both glasses \u2014 the one in the sun is warmer."],t.s)
A.a0n=new B.J("Warm Water from the Sun","The Sun gives heat energy that can warm things up.",A.aHl,A.aP6,"The Sun gives off heat energy. Water in the sunlight absorbs that energy and becomes warmer than water in the shade. This is free solar energy, used for example for water heaters and for drying things in the sun.",A.D,"Use ordinary water, not hot water. Do not look straight at the Sun.",60)
A.aSK=s(["A cork or a bottle cap as the centre of the wheel","Blades cut from bottle caps/thick cardboard","A thin palm-leaf stick or a chopstick as the axle + water from a tap/water scoop"],t.s)
A.aKN=s(["Push several blades into the cork/bottle cap all the way round to make a wheel.","Push the stick through the centre as the axle; hold both ends of the stick.","Hold the wheel under a running tap (or pour water over it from above).","Observe the wheel turning when the water hits it.","\ud83d\udca1 If the wheel sticks or the blades fall out: make the axle hole in the cork a little bigger so the stick turns freely, and push the blades in deeper \u2014 you can strengthen them with a little plasticine."],t.s)
A.Zg=new B.J("Water Wheel","Flowing/falling water has movement energy that can turn a wheel.",A.aSK,A.aKN,"Flowing or falling water carries movement energy. When it hits the blades of the wheel, that energy is passed on and turns the wheel. The same idea is used in hydroelectric power stations to make electricity.",A.D,"Wipe up spilt water so the floor is not slippery.",15)
A.aM2=s([A.a0w,A.a0n,A.Zg],t.T)
A.b6f=new B.aD("Cannot yet name a source or a form of energy.","Names sources/forms of energy, but mixes up a source (the sun) and a form (light).","Names 2 sources AND 2 forms of energy, each with a correct example.")
A.b2o=new B.aC("Pupils can name sources of energy and recognise forms of energy (heat, light, sound, motion) in everyday life.",u.o0,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Link feeling hungry to needing energy from food.","Main activity (20 minutes): Discuss sources of energy (the sun, food, electricity) and forms of energy (heat, light, sound, motion). Show examples: the sun gives heat & light, food gives energy to move, clapping = sound.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with a fact: almost all the energy on Earth comes from the Sun \u2014 even the energy in our food.","Pupils can name at least 2 sources of energy and 2 forms of energy, with examples.",A.b2,u.T,A.b6f)
A.bly=new B.aw("sumber_bentuk_energi","Energy Sources & Forms","\ud83d\udd06",A.aM,A.bv,"Pupils can name sources of energy and recognise forms of energy (heat, light, sound, motion).",u.o0,30,"Energy is invisible, but you can feel what it does: the sun's heat, the sound of a clap, the motion of running. Start with what the children feel in their own bodies.",A.aHk,A.aM2,A.b2o,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to trace one object (a lamp/a fan) from its energy source to the form of energy it produces.","Start with their own bodies: clap (sound), run (motion), rub palms together (heat). Name the form of energy each time.",u.b)
A.ON=new B.f(A.Z,"Can hands be fooled?","Can the same water feel warm AND cold?",A.E,"Everyone, we usually use our hands to feel if something is hot or cold. But are our hands always right? Could they be fooled? Let's test it in a moment!",u.n,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.QR=new B.f(A.Y,"Temperature = how hot or cold","A thermometer measures temperature accurately.",A.v,"Try putting one hand in warm water and the other in cold water, then move them both into plain water. Strange, isn't it \u2014 the same water feels different to each hand! So what do you think \u2014 if even our hands can be fooled, how can we measure hot and cold accurately?",null,"Draw a simple thermometer with a number scale and liquid rising. Show small numbers (cold) and big numbers (hot).",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.beg=new B.m("suhu","\ud83c\udf21\ufe0f","Temperature","TEMPERATURE: how hot or cold something is, written in degrees (\xb0C). The hotter it is, the higher the number.")
A.bb1=new B.m("termometer","\ud83d\udccf","Thermometer","THERMOMETER: a tool for measuring temperature accurately. There are body, room and weather thermometers. Some are digital: the number shows on a small screen.")
A.bdO=new B.m("tertipu","\ud83e\udd1a","Fooled hands","FOOLED HANDS: hands only feel a difference, not the real temperature. The same water can feel warm or cold.")
A.bcY=new B.m("demam","\ud83e\udd12","Fever","FEVER: when we're ill, body temperature rises above normal. A body thermometer helps the doctor know for sure.")
A.aLJ=s([A.beg,A.bb1,A.bdO,A.bcY],t.w)
A.TB=new B.f(A.W,"Meet temperature & thermometers","Tap each card to find out.",A.v,"Let's get to know temperature and the tool that measures it!",null,"Write the temperature facts on the blackboard.",A.aLJ,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Rc=new B.f(A.X,"Three-bowl test!","Cold hand & warm hand, then both into plain water.",A.v,"Now let's prove that our hands can be fooled!","Prepare 3 bowls: cold, plain, warm. Dip one hand in the cold and one in the warm for about 30 seconds, then dip BOTH into the middle bowl. Ask the children to describe the strange feeling.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.aZn=new B.D("Hands can measure temperature accurately.","Hands only feel; they aren't accurate and are easily fooled (plain water feels cold after touching warm water). A thermometer gives an exact number.")
A.aZZ=new B.D("Something that feels cold must be colder than something that feels warm.","We can be fooled: metal feels colder than wood even at the same temperature, because metal takes heat from our hand faster.")
A.b0_=new B.D("A fan (a hand fan or an electric fan) makes the air colder.","A fan doesn't lower the air temperature: a thermometer in front of the fan shows almost the same number. We FEEL cooler because the breeze helps our sweat evaporate and carries heat away from our skin.")
A.aDW=s([A.aZn,A.aZZ,A.b0_],t.I)
A.TF=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Which tool measures temperature accurately?",A.E,"Si Jabrik still isn't sure. Can you help him? What tool do we use to measure temperature accurately? And why isn't it enough to just use our hands?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aDW)
A.Pj=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Your body stays at about 37\xb0C, whether it's hot or cold outside!",A.a_,"Now we can answer our question from the start: yes, our hands can be fooled, and that's what thermometers are for. And here's the surprising part: whether the weather is boiling hot or freezing cold, our body keeps itself at about 36 to 37 degrees Celsius. Our body has its own automatic \"temperature control\": we sweat when it's hot and shiver when it's cold, so our temperature stays just right. Amazing, isn't it!","A round of applause for our temperature-controlling bodies! A challenge for home: put two identical glasses of water outside, one in the sun and one in the shade. An hour later, dip a finger in each. Guess first: which one will be warmer?",null,null,!1,null,"Weather satellites can measure the temperature of cloud tops from space with infrared \u2014 invisible rays, like the warmth of a fire you feel on your skin. Very cold cloud tops usually mean heavy rain, so forecasters at Indonesia's weather agency (BMKG) can give warnings earlier.","In 1983, at the Vostok research station in Antarctica \u2014 the icy continent around the South Pole \u2014 a thermometer recorded about \u221289 \xb0C. That is the coldest air temperature ever measured directly on the Earth's surface.",null)
A.aNs=s([A.ON,A.QR,A.TB,A.Rc,A.TF,A.Pj],t.n)
A.aV8=s(["3 bowls","Cold water, plain water (room temperature), warm water (not hot, PREPARED BY THE TEACHER)"],t.s)
A.aSE=s(["Fill bowl 1 with cold water, bowl 2 with plain water, bowl 3 with warm water.","Dip your left hand in the cold water and your right hand in the warm water for about 30 seconds.","Then dip BOTH hands into the middle bowl (plain water) at the same time.","Strange! The same water feels different \u2014 warm to one hand, cold to the other."],t.s)
A.a0j=new B.J("Three Bowls of Water","Temperature measures how hot or cold something is; hands can be fooled, a tool is more accurate.",A.aV8,A.aSE,"Temperature is a measure of how hot or cold something is. Our hands can be fooled because they only feel a difference, not the real temperature. That's why we need a thermometer \u2014 a tool that measures temperature accurately, in degrees.",A.D,"The teacher prepares the warm water and checks it first with their own hand \u2014 only lukewarm, NEVER hot or boiling water \u2014 so the children's hands stay safe.",10)
A.aQw=s(["A small bottle of water + a little colouring (or turmeric water/tea so it is easy to see)","A clear straw + plasticine/modelling clay/wax to seal the bottle's mouth"],t.s)
A.aSO=s(["Fill the bottle to the top with coloured water. Push in the straw and seal all round it with plasticine so it is airtight (the water rises a little in the straw).","Hold the bottle in warm hands \u2014 watch the water RISE in the straw.","Put the bottle in cold water \u2014 the water goes DOWN.",'Mark the water levels as "warm" and "cold".',"\ud83d\udca1 If the water does not seem to rise: use a narrower straw and make sure the plasticine seal is completely tight so the water can only go up the straw."],t.s)
A.a0f=new B.J("Simple Thermometer","Liquids expand when hot and contract when cold.",A.aQw,A.aSO,"When warmed, the water expands (gets bigger) so it rises in the straw; when cooled, it contracts and goes down. Real thermometers work in exactly this way \u2014 they use a liquid expanding to show the temperature.",A.D,"Use the warmth of hands, not hot water.",20)
A.aL3=s([A.a0j,A.a0f],t.T)
A.b6M=new B.aD("Still thinks a hand can measure temperature as accurately as a thermometer.","Knows a thermometer measures temperature, but cannot yet explain why it is more accurate than a hand.","Explains that a thermometer is more accurate than a hand AND names one of its uses.")
A.b1P=new B.aC(u.oj,u.bY,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Ask how a doctor knows we have a fever.","Main activity (20 minutes): Explain temperature as a measure of hot and cold. Do the three-bowl experiment to show that hands can be fooled. Introduce the thermometer as the accurate tool for measuring temperature.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: a healthy human body temperature is about 36\u201337 degrees Celsius.","Pupils can explain that a thermometer measures temperature more accurately than a hand, and name one of its uses.",A.b2,u.Z,A.b6M)
A.bll=new B.aw("suhu_termometer","Temperature & Thermometers","\ud83c\udf21\ufe0f",A.aM,A.bv,u.oj,u.bY,30,'The three-bowl experiment amazes children because their own hands get "fooled". If you have a thermometer (body or room), show them straight away how to read it.',A.aNs,A.aL3,A.b1P,null,!0,!1,"Ask the children to measure the temperature of 3 different waters (ice, plain, warm) with a thermometer and order the numbers from smallest to largest.","Dip a hand in warm water, then in plain water \u2014 it feels cold even though it is just plain water. Show that the thermometer still gives the same number.",u.b)
A.SX=new B.f(A.Z,"Umbrella or not?","The sky is grey this morning. What should we do?",A.E,"Everyone, imagine the sky this morning looks dark and cloudy. Should we take an umbrella or not? How can we guess? Farmers and fishers have read the signs in the sky for a long time. There are also weather forecasts from Indonesia's weather agency (BMKG), shared on the radio, on TV and on phones.","Let the children guess by looking at the sky outside.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Qf=new B.f(A.Y,"Kinds of weather","Sunny, cloudy, rainy, windy.",A.v,"Have a quick look out of the window. What can you see in the sky today? Was yesterday the same as today? The sky and the air can be different every day, and each kind has its own name. How many kinds do you think we usually see?",null,"Draw 4 symbols: a sun (sunny), a cloud (cloudy), a cloud + drops (rainy), slanting lines (windy).",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bbF=new B.m("cerah","\u2600\ufe0f","Sunny","SUNNY: blue sky, the sun is shining. Good for drying clothes and playing outside. On very hot days, drink water and find some shade.")
A.bdP=new B.m("berawan","\u2601\ufe0f","Cloudy","CLOUDY: lots of clouds cover the sun, and the air is cooler. It can be a sign that rain is coming.")
A.baq=new B.m("hujan","\ud83c\udf27\ufe0f","Rainy","RAINY: drops of water fall from the clouds. Best take an umbrella or a raincoat. Plants are happy to get water.")
A.be_=new B.m("berangin","\ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f","Windy","WINDY: the air moves fast. Kites fly easily, but watch out for light things blowing about.")
A.aVU=s([A.bbF,A.bdP,A.baq,A.be_],t.w)
A.RM=new B.f(A.W,"Signs of each weather","Tap each weather to find out.",A.v,"Let's find out the signs of each kind of weather, and what it's good for!",null,"Write a suitable activity next to each weather symbol.",A.aVU,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.N_=new B.f(A.X,"Be a weather forecaster!","Look at the sky & record today's weather.",A.v,"Now you're all little weather forecasters!","Have the children look at the sky and decide what today's weather is. Start a weather calendar: stick on a weather symbol every morning for a week.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b_c=new B.D("Weather is the same as a season.","Weather is what the sky is like TODAY (it can change from hour to hour); a season lasts for months. Rain = weather; the rainy season = a season.")
A.b_f=new B.D("Dark clouds always mean heavy rain.","Dark clouds are a sign that rain MIGHT come, not that it definitely will. But be sensible: get an umbrella ready, just in case.")
A.b0X=new B.D("The weather forecast is always right.","A forecast is the experts' best estimate from satellite, radar and weather-station data, so it is sometimes wrong. Encourage the children to keep watching the sky themselves and to have an umbrella ready, just in case.")
A.aMa=s([A.b_c,A.b_f,A.b0X],t.I)
A.MQ=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Dark, cloudy sky \u2014 what does it tell us?",A.E,"Now it's your turn to explain to Si Jabrik. If the sky is full of dark clouds, what weather might be coming? And what should we get ready?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aMa)
A.Qo=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Rainwater was once sea water & vapour!",A.a_,"Remember our question from the start? Now you can read the signs of the weather yourselves. Did you know? The rain that falls today used to be water in the sea or a river. The sun's heat made it evaporate, it rose up into the clouds, and then it fell again. The same water goes round and round and never runs out. Isn't nature amazing? But forecasting rain in a tropical country like Indonesia is still very hard \u2014 scientists are still looking for ways to forecast heavy rain more accurately. Maybe one day you'll help crack it! Tomorrow morning, look at the sky and guess: will it rain today? Write down your guess, then check in the afternoon.","A round of applause for the amazing water cycle!",null,null,!1,null,"Weather satellites, machines made by people that circle far above the Earth, photograph the clouds over the whole of Indonesia every 10 minutes, and forecasters at Indonesia's weather agency (BMKG) read them to predict rain. Early warnings of heavy rain, and of high waves for fishers, are then shared on the radio, on TV and on phones.","In the United States, some pilots and weather scientists fly special aircraft right through giant hurricanes over the sea on purpose. Instruments on board measure the wind and air pressure inside the storm, so people on the coast can be warned earlier.",null)
A.aVO=s([A.SX,A.Qf,A.RM,A.N_,A.MQ,A.Qo],t.n)
A.aKJ=s(["Hot water (HANDLED BY THE TEACHER)","A clear, heatproof glass/jar","A small plate + a few ice cubes"],t.s)
A.aVZ=s(["The teacher pours hot water into the jar (not full).","Cover the jar with the plate and put the ice cubes on top of it.","Wait a few minutes. Look under the plate.","Drops of water form and then drip down \u2014 mini rain!"],t.s)
A.ZF=new B.J("Rain in a Jar","A mini water cycle: vapour rises, condenses, then falls like rain.",A.aKJ,A.aVZ,"The hot water evaporates and rises. When the vapour touches the plate, made cold by the ice, it condenses into water drops, which then fall like rain. This is the same water cycle that makes rain fall from the clouds.",A.ct,"Hot water is handled ONLY by the teacher. Children watch from a safe distance.",15)
A.aPQ=s(["A clear plastic bottle (cut off the top and turn it upside down as a funnel)","A ruler + marker pen","An open space when it rains"],t.s)
A.aW2=s(["Stick a ruler or draw a measuring scale on the side of the bottle.","Put the bottle in an open space when it rains (away from roofs/trees).","After the rain, read the height of the water collected.","Record it every rainy day and compare."],t.s)
A.ZT=new B.J("Simple Rain Gauge","Rainfall can be measured and recorded like a weather expert.",A.aPQ,A.aW2,"The rainwater collected shows how much rain fell (rainfall). By recording it every day, we can compare which days had heavy rain \u2014 exactly how weather experts at Indonesia's weather agency (BMKG) work.",A.D,"The teacher cuts the bottle \u2014 the edges can be sharp. Put out & collect the bottle only when it is safe; do not go outside when there is lightning.",60)
A.aQa=s([A.ZF,A.ZT],t.T)
A.b6A=new B.aD("Cannot yet name kinds of weather, or mixes up weather and seasons.","Names some kinds of weather, but does not yet link them to suitable activities.","Names 3 kinds of weather AND one suitable activity for each.")
A.b1p=new B.aC("Pupils can recognise kinds of weather (sunny, cloudy, rainy, windy) and link them to everyday activities.",u.hc,"Opening (5 minutes): Have the children look out of the window. Ask what the weather is like today and how they can tell.","Main activity (20 minutes): Introduce kinds of weather (sunny, cloudy, rainy, windy) and their signs. Discuss how weather affects what we wear and do. Make a simple weather calendar.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with a fact: rain comes from water that evaporates, gathers into clouds, then falls again.","Pupils can name at least 3 kinds of weather and one suitable activity for each.",A.b2,u.T,A.b6A)
A.bm_=new B.aw("cuaca","Weather","\u26c5",A.aM,A.bw,"Pupils can recognise kinds of weather and link them to everyday activities.",u.hc,30,"Today's weather outside the window is a free teaching aid. Start with what the children can see right now.",A.aVO,A.aQa,A.b1p,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to make a weather forecast for tomorrow from today's signs in the sky, then check it the next day.","Look at the sky outside now and match it to one weather symbol. Repeat every morning until the children get used to it.",u.I)
A.SM=new B.f(A.Z,"Why do rivers flow?","River water keeps on flowing. Why is it never still?",A.E,"Everyone, the water in a river is always flowing. It never stays still in one place. Where is it going, and why does it keep on flowing?",u.R,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Su=new B.f(A.Y,"The Earth goes up and down","There are high mountains, and low valleys & seas.",A.v,"Think about your journey to school \u2014 on foot, by boat, or on the back of a motorbike \u2014 or to your grandma's village. Is the way flat all along, or does it go uphill and downhill? Has anyone seen a mountain far away? Or a river? What shapes do you think the Earth's surface has?",null,"Draw a landscape: a tall mountain, a river flowing down, a flat plain, and the sea at the bottom.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.baj=new B.m("gunung","\u26f0\ufe0f","Mountain","MOUNTAIN: a very high part of the Earth. The air is cold, and rivers often start from springs up there.")
A.b9X=new B.m("dataran","\ud83c\udf3e","Plain","PLAIN: wide, flat land. Good for rice fields, farms and homes. Most cities are on plains.")
A.bey=new B.m("sungai","\ud83c\udfde\ufe0f","River","RIVER: water flowing from a high place (a mountain) to a low place (the sea). That's why river water always flows. Indonesia's longest river, the Kapuas in Kalimantan, is about 1,100 km long.")
A.bdd=new B.m("laut","\ud83c\udf0a","Sea","SEA: a huge, low body of salty water. It's where river water finally ends up.")
A.aNn=s([A.baj,A.b9X,A.bey,A.bdd],t.w)
A.OS=new B.f(A.W,"Get to know each landform","Tap each landform to find out.",A.v,"Let's get to know the shapes of the Earth's surface!",null,"Label each landform on your landscape drawing.",A.aNn,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Mi=new B.f(A.X,"Make a sand map!","Shape mountains & valleys, pour water.",A.v,"Now we're going to make our own mini map of the Earth!","Use a tray + wet sand/soil. Shape a mountain and a valley, then pour water on the top. Ask the children to watch where the water flows.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.aZD=new B.D("River water can flow up a mountain.","Water always flows down to a lower place because the Earth's gravity pulls it. Remember the sand map \u2014 the water went down into the valley, not up.")
A.b0Z=new B.D("A river flows because something pushes it.","It doesn't need a push \u2014 water flows by itself from a high place (a mountain) to a low place (the sea) because of the Earth's pull.")
A.aLw=s([A.aZD,A.b0Z],t.I)
A.NQ=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Which way does water flow from a mountain?",A.E,"Si Jabrik still isn't sure. Can you help him? Does water from a mountain flow to a higher place or a lower place? And where does it all end up?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aLw)
A.QK=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","A mountain on Indonesia's equator once had snow all year!",A.a_,"We've answered our question from the start: water always flows from high places to low places. And here's the surprise: in Papua there's a mountain called Puncak Jaya, about 4,884 metres high \u2014 and its top used to have SNOW and glaciers (ice) all year round, even though Indonesia is a hot, tropical country! The higher you go, the colder the air. Sadly, as the Earth warms, those glaciers have now almost disappeared. After the next rain, watch where the water in the yard flows and where it collects. That's a mini river and lake \u2014 and you're the researchers!","A round of applause for Indonesia's mountains!",null,null,!1,null,"Satellites, machines made by people that circle the Earth, can measure how high mountains are and how deep valleys are by bouncing radar waves off the ground. Indonesia's mapping agency uses data like this to map the whole country, which has more than 17,000 islands.","The deepest place in the ocean, the Mariana Trench in the Pacific, is almost 11 km deep \u2014 Puncak Jaya would sink right in, with its top still about 6 km under water. People have travelled down to the bottom several times in special submarines.",null)
A.aNQ=s([A.SM,A.Su,A.OS,A.Mi,A.NQ,A.QK],t.n)
A.aVV=s(["A tray","Wet sand or soil","A little water in a glass"],t.s)
A.aVl=s(['Shape the sand on the tray into a tall "mountain" and a low "valley".',"Slowly pour water onto the top of the sand mountain.","Watch: the water flows down towards the low part.",'The water collects in the valley, making a little "lake".'],t.s)
A.a0B=new B.J("Sand Map","Water always flows from high places to low places.",A.aVV,A.aVl,"The Earth's surface has high parts (mountains, hills) and low parts (valleys, rivers, the sea). Water always flows from high to low because of the Earth's pull. That's why rivers flow from the mountains to the sea.",A.D,null,15)
A.aOD=s(["2 trays/containers: one with bare soil, one with grassy soil","A glass of water","A container to catch the water at the lower end"],t.s)
A.a2j=s(["Tilt both trays at the same angle.","Pour the same amount of water onto the top of each tray.","Catch the water that runs out at the lower end.","Compare: the water from the bare soil is muddier & carries more soil."],t.s)
A.a0m=new B.J("Soil Erosion Test","Plants hold soil in place; bare soil is easily washed away.",A.aOD,A.a2j,"Plant roots bind the soil, so grassy soil stands up better to being washed away by water. Bare soil is easily carried off by water (erosion). That is why planting trees on slopes prevents landslides and keeps the soil fertile.",A.D,"Do this outside or on a covered surface; wash hands afterwards.",20)
A.awt=s([A.a0B,A.a0m],t.T)
A.b5X=new B.aD("Cannot yet name landforms, or thinks water can flow uphill.","Names some landforms, but is not yet sure which way water flows.","Names 3 landforms AND explains that water flows from high to low.")
A.b20=new B.aC(u.n6,u.bT,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Link it to a river or a drain the children have seen.","Main activity (20 minutes): Introduce the landforms of the Earth's surface \u2014 mountains, plains, valleys, rivers, the sea. Discuss their features. Use the sand map to show water flowing from high to low.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with a fact: Puncak Jaya in Papua is about 4,884 m high and once had permanent snow and glaciers, now almost gone, even though it lies on the hot equator.","Pupils can name at least 3 landforms and explain that water flows to low places.",A.b2,u.T,A.b5X)
A.bm2=new B.aw("permukaan_bumi","Landforms of the Earth","\ud83c\udfd4\ufe0f",A.aM,A.bw,u.n6,u.bT,30,"Link it to the local area: a mountain you can see, a river/drain, flat rice fields. Children understand landforms better through places they know.",A.aNQ,A.awt,A.b20,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to draw the journey of water from the top of a mountain to the sea, through rivers and valleys.","Use the sand map: shape a mountain and a valley, pour water on the top, and watch together as the water always goes down to the low place.",u.q)
A.SZ=new B.f(A.Z,"Who looks after nature?","Water, soil, forests, air \u2014 all from nature. Who looks after them?",A.E,"Everyone, look around you. The water we drink, paper from trees, the air we breathe \u2014 they all come from nature! But if all the trees around us keep getting cut down, what will happen to the air and the temperature around us?","Ask the children to name one thing in the classroom that comes from nature (paper, drinking water, a wooden table).",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Qs=new B.f(A.Y,"Nature's four friends","Water, soil, forests, air \u2014 four natural resources around us.",A.v,"Look at these four things: flowing water, the ground we stand on, shady forests and trees, and the air we breathe. We use all of them every day. What do you think happens if one of them gets dirty or runs out?",null,"Draw four circles: \ud83d\udca7 water, \ud83d\udfe4 soil, \ud83c\udf33 forest, \ud83d\udca8 air, all joined to each other with arrows.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.baK=new B.m("air","\ud83d\udca7","Water","WATER: we use it to drink, wash, and water plants. Rain renews water, but clean water can run out if we waste it or if rubbish makes it dirty.")
A.beD=new B.m("tanah","\ud83d\udfe4","Soil","SOIL: where plants grow and where we stand. Rich soil takes a very long time to form, so we must protect it from damage and from rubbish.")
A.bdp=new B.m("hutan","\ud83c\udf33","Forest","FORESTS/TREES: give us clean air and shade. Trees can be planted again, but a forest that is cut down completely takes decades to grow back.")
A.bcE=new B.m("udara","\ud83d\udca8","Air","AIR: we breathe it in all the time. Clean air comes from plants, but smoke from vehicles, forest fires and burning rubbish can make it dirty and make the Earth warmer.")
A.aOV=s([A.baK,A.beD,A.bdp,A.bcE],t.w)
A.NT=new B.f(A.W,"Natural resources around us","Tap each part \u2014 find out more.",A.v,"Let's explore the natural resources around us, one by one!",null,"Label each natural resource on the drawing.",A.aOV,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.OX=new B.f(A.X,"Filter some muddy water!","Pour muddy water into the filter \u2014 see it come out clearer.",A.v,"Now let's try it ourselves: making dirty water clear again. It's harder than you think!","Do the Simple Water Filter experiment: layer gravel, sand, and charcoal/cotton wool in an old bottle, then pour in muddy water and catch what drips out. Compare its colour with the water before filtering. Remember: this filtered water is NOT for drinking.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b0g=new B.D("Natural resources like water and trees will never run out, so we can use as much as we like.","Clean water and trees can be renewed, but it takes time. If we use them or cut them down faster than nature can replace them, those resources can still run out or be damaged.")
A.aYM=new B.D("Climate change is just ordinary rainy or hot weather, nothing serious.","Climate change means the Earth is getting warmer because too much smoke and gas from burning builds up in the air. It is not the ordinary weather that changes from day to day, but the Earth's temperature slowly rising \u2014 that is why we need to protect trees and keep the air clean.")
A.aZY=new B.D("Burning plastic rubbish makes it disappear, so burning rubbish is safe.","Burnt plastic does not disappear \u2014 it turns into ash and smoke that dirties the air and is bad to breathe in. It is better to use less plastic, sort rubbish, and collect the plastic for a community waste bank or a scrap collector where there is one.")
A.aTZ=s([A.b0g,A.aYM,A.aZY],t.I)
A.Qm=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","What is one thing you can do to look after nature? Why?",A.E,"Now it's your turn to explain to Si Jabrik. Name one natural resource we need to look after, and one thing YOU can do to look after it. Why does that matter?","Ask the children to raise their hands. Praise every attempt to answer, and write their solutions on the blackboard.",null,null,!0,"Ask the children to name ONE practical action (for example saving water, putting rubbish in the bin, planting a tree, bringing their own water bottle, collecting plastic rubbish for a community waste bank or a scrap collector where there is one) AND the reason for it.",null,null,A.aTZ)
A.RQ=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Satellites in space watch the Earth's forests every day!",A.a_,"Remember our question at the start? Now you know: if trees keep being cut down, there's less clean air and the Earth can get warmer and warmer. But here's a surprise! Far out in space, there are SATELLITES going round the Earth. With help from AI (that's artificial intelligence), they watch the forests and the Earth's temperature EVERY DAY! So if someone starts cutting down a forest, or the Earth's temperature changes, scientists know straight away. It's not just us looking after nature down here \u2014 clever technology up in the sky is helping too!","Give a round of applause \u2014 then ask the children to promise to do one thing to look after nature, starting today. Home challenge: count how many plastic wrappers the family throws away in one day, and bring the number in tomorrow to compare in class.",null,null,!1,null,"The sunshine that dries rice and salted fish can also be turned into electricity by solar panels, with no smoke. In many villages and small islands far from the power grid, solar panels charge batteries during the day to light homes, schools and community health centres at night.","Costa Rica, a small country in Central America, once lost much of its forest. After decades of protecting and replanting, its forest grew back from about a quarter to more than half of the country.",null)
A.aLL=s([A.SZ,A.Qs,A.NT,A.OX,A.Qm,A.RQ],t.n)
A.aUH=s(["An old plastic bottle, cut in two","Small gravel","Fine sand and coarse sand","Cooking charcoal + cotton wool or a clean cloth (for the plug)","Muddy water (water mixed with a little soil)"],t.s)
A.a2t=s(["Cut the old bottle in two. Turn the top half upside down like a funnel and put it into the bottom half.","Build the layers starting at the bottle neck (the bottom): a plug of cotton wool/cloth, then charcoal, then fine sand, then coarse sand, and gravel on top.","Make muddy water by mixing clean water with a little soil.","Slowly pour the muddy water onto the filter and catch the water that drips into the container below.","Compare the colour of the water before and after filtering."],t.s)
A.a0A=new B.J("Simple Water Filter","Soil/sand can filter water, but dirty water is hard to clean \u2014 it is easier to keep water clean than to fix it once it is dirty.",A.aUH,A.a2t,"The gravel, sand, and charcoal filter dirt and soil particles out of the water, so the water that drips through looks clearer. But water from this simple filter is NOT necessarily free of germs \u2014 it only filters out big particles, it does not kill germs. This shows that cleaning dirty water is hard and takes many steps; it is much easier to keep water clean in the first place.",A.D,"This filtered water is NOT for drinking \u2014 it is only for the experiment. The teacher should cut the bottle, or supervise the cutting, because the edges can be sharp.",15)
A.asZ=s(["2 clear glasses or jars","Some soil","A little water","Clear plastic (plastic wrap/clear plastic bag)","Rubber bands"],t.s)
A.aOa=s(["Put a little soil in both glasses/jars, then sprinkle a little water into each.","Cover one glass with clear plastic and hold it in place with a rubber band. Leave the other glass open.","Put both glasses in direct sunlight for 30 minutes.","After 30 minutes, feel the inside of both glasses \u2014 which one feels warmer?"],t.s)
A.ZP=new B.J("Mini Greenhouse","A clear plastic cover holds in the sun's heat, like the gases in the air that hold in the Earth's heat \u2014 a simple picture of climate change.",A.asZ,A.aOa,"The glass covered with plastic feels warmer because the plastic holds the sun's heat inside. This is like certain gases that build up in the Earth's air and hold in the sun's heat \u2014 that is what makes the Earth warmer and warmer (climate change). The more smoke and gas from burning, the more heat is trapped.",A.D,"Glasses left in the sun can feel warm \u2014 hold them carefully when checking how warm they are. Do not look directly at the sun.",35)
A.aNR=s([A.a0A,A.ZP],t.T)
A.b6u=new B.aD("Cannot yet name the natural resources around them.","Names natural resources (e.g. water or forests), but does not yet suggest a practical solution for protecting them.","Names natural resources AND suggests one practical solution with a reason (e.g. save water because clean water is limited).")
A.b1X=new B.aC(u.j0,u.c7,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the children to name things from nature they have used today (drinking water, paper, air to breathe). Ask the hook question.","Main activity (20 minutes): Explore four natural resources around us: water, soil, forests, and air \u2014 which can be renewed and which must be protected. Do the Simple Water Filter experiment to show how hard it is to clean water once it is dirty. Ask the children to suggest one practical solution they can carry out themselves.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. Finish with a fact: satellites and AI now watch the forests and the Earth's temperature every day from space.","Pupils can name at least 2 natural resources around them AND suggest one practical solution with a reason.",A.l9,u.T,A.b6u)
A.blt=new B.aw("pelestarian_sda","Caring for Nature & Climate","\ud83c\udf0d",A.aM,A.bw,u.j0,u.c7,30,"Keep the mood relaxed and do not frighten the children \u2014 explain climate change as a simple science fact, not a threat. Focus on small solutions the children can carry out every day, and praise every solution they suggest.",A.aLL,A.aNR,A.b1X,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to make a list of 3 habits for saving water or electricity that they can do at home, then share it in front of the class.",'Show a picture of a tree and ask: "If this tree is cut down, where will we get clean air and shade?" Say: "trees keep the air clean and cool".',u.H)
A.P3=new B.f(A.Z,"Where does food go?","A mouthful of rice is swallowed. Then where does it go?",A.E,"Everyone, we eat every single day \u2014 rice, corn, sweet potato or sago, with fish or vegetables. But have you ever wondered where food goes after we swallow it? What kind of journey does it take inside our bodies?","Let the children guess the order. No answer is wrong yet.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Qy=new B.f(A.Y,"The food path","Mouth \u2192 oesophagus \u2192 stomach \u2192 intestines.",A.v,"Try swallowing your saliva slowly. Where do you feel it go? When you eat rice, it doesn't stop in your mouth. So where does it stop along the way before it turns into energy? And what job does each stop do? What do you think?",null,"Draw a simple body: the mouth at the top, a line down to the stomach (a pouch), then the intestines (a winding tube). Add arrows pointing down.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.baW=new B.m("mulut","\ud83d\udc44","Mouth","MOUTH: teeth chew food until it's fine, and saliva starts breaking it down. Digestion starts here!")
A.bfa=new B.m("kerongkongan","\ud83d\udd3d","Oesophagus","OESOPHAGUS: a tube that pushes food down from the mouth to the stomach with a squeezing movement.")
A.beE=new B.m("lambung","\ud83e\uded9","Stomach","STOMACH: a pouch that churns food with acid juices until it's soft, like porridge.")
A.bdC=new B.m("usus","\ud83e\uddf5","Intestines","INTESTINES: soak up the goodness from food to give the body energy. Leftovers the body can't use leave as poo.")
A.aPZ=s([A.baW,A.bfa,A.beE,A.bdC],t.w)
A.MG=new B.f(A.W,"Each organ's job","Tap each organ to find out its job.",A.v,"Let's find out what each digestive organ does!",null,"Write each organ's job next to its drawing.",A.aPZ,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Rd=new B.f(A.X,"Bread turns sweet!","Chew bread for a long time and taste it turn sweet.",A.v,"Now let's prove that digestion starts in the mouth!","Hand out small pieces of plain bread. Ask the children to chew slowly without swallowing for about 1 minute and notice a sweet taste appear. Ask where the sweetness comes from.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.aZp=new B.D("Food is only digested in the belly (the stomach).","Digestion starts in the mouth (chewing + saliva), then the stomach, and the goodness from food is absorbed in the small intestine. Have the children trace the path of food from the mouth downwards.")
A.b15=new B.D("The goodness from food is absorbed in the stomach.","The stomach turns food into a smooth mush; absorbing the goodness happens in the small intestine, which is long and has lots of folds.")
A.b_E=new B.D("If my hands don't look dirty, I don't need to wash them before eating.","Germs are too small to see and can be swallowed with our food, then cause a stomach ache or diarrhoea. Wash hands with soap and running water (it can be poured from a scoop) before eating.")
A.aQS=s([A.aZp,A.b15,A.b_E],t.I)
A.Q2=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Which organ soaks up the goodness from food?",A.E,"Si Jabrik is confused \u2014 help him out! Which organ soaks up the goodness from food to give our bodies energy? And which organ starts digesting food first?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aQS)
A.Lu=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Your small intestine is 6\u20137 metres long!",A.a_,"Remember our question at the start? Now you know food's journey from the mouth all the way to the intestines. Did you know? If you stretched out the small intestine inside our belly, it would be about 6 to 7 METRES long \u2014 longer than a car! But it's coiled up neatly in our small belly. That's why it takes a long time to soak up all the goodness from our food. Trillions of friendly bacteria live in our gut too \u2014 roughly as many as the cells of our own body. Scientists are still finding out all the jobs they do \u2014 many of their secrets are still unknown!",u.G,null,null,!1,null,'Tempeh, a food that comes from Indonesia, is made with the help of a mould that "digests" part of the soya beans first, so tempeh is easier for our bodies to digest. Scientists from many countries study tempeh too.','In hospitals in many countries, doctors can use a capsule-sized "camera pill" that is swallowed. As it passes through the gut, it takes tens of thousands of photos, so doctors can see inside the intestines with no operation.',null)
A.aQA=s([A.P3,A.Qy,A.MG,A.Rd,A.Q2,A.Lu],t.n)
A.aPr=s(["A small piece of plain white bread (or plain white rice)","Your own mouth and saliva","Patience, about 1 minute"],t.s)
A.awp=s(["Take a small piece of plain bread and put it on your tongue.","Chew it slowly without swallowing, and keep going for about 1 minute.","Notice: little by little, the bread tastes SWEETER in your mouth.","Ask the children: where does the sweet taste come from, when plain bread isn't sweet?"],t.s)
A.Z9=new B.J("The Bread That Turns Sweet","Enzymes in saliva start digesting (breaking down) food in the mouth.",A.aPr,A.awp,"Saliva contains an enzyme that breaks the starch in bread down into simple sugars, which taste sweet. So digestion already starts in the mouth, not just in the stomach!",A.D,"Use clean bread/rice. Do not share food that has already been chewed. Check for food allergies first (e.g. wheat in bread \u2014 use rice instead). Children who are fasting can just watch and listen to their friends describe it, without tasting.",5)
A.aQV=s(["A long sock/stocking or a long plastic bag","A ball about the size of a tennis ball or a small orange (for young children, NOT marbles or grapes \u2014 choking risk)"],t.s)
A.aKH=s(["Put the ball into the top end of the stocking.","Squeeze the stocking from top to bottom, one squeeze after another, just behind the ball.","Observe: the ball is pushed forward even though the stocking is not tilted.","Link this to how food moves through the oesophagus (food pipe) & intestines."],t.s)
A.Zk=new B.J("Peristalsis","Gut muscles squeeze to push food along (peristalsis).",A.aQV,A.aKH,"Food does not just fall down by gravity \u2014 the muscles of the gut squeeze one after another (peristalsis) to push food forward. That is why we can still swallow while lying down or even upside down.",A.D,"Marbles & grapes are a choking risk. For young children, use objects bigger than their mouths, and nothing goes in anyone's mouth.",15)
A.anM=s([A.Z9,A.Zk],t.T)
A.b6e=new B.aD("Cannot yet put the path of food in order, or thinks food goes straight to the stomach and nowhere else.","Puts part of the path in order, but does not yet name the job of each organ.","Puts the digestive path in order AND correctly names the job of at least 3 organs.")
A.b1F=new B.aC("Pupils can name the main digestive organs (mouth, stomach, intestines) and explain what each one does, in order.","Where does food go after we swallow it?","Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Have the children swallow their saliva and imagine food's journey inside the body.","Main activity (20 minutes): Trace the path of food: mouth \u2192 oesophagus \u2192 stomach \u2192 intestines. Explain the job of each organ. Do the bread-and-saliva experiment to show that digestion starts in the mouth.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: an adult's small intestine is about 6\u20137 metres long, neatly coiled up inside the belly.","Pupils can put the digestive path in order and correctly name the job of at least 3 organs.",A.b2,u.d,A.b6e)
A.blw=new B.aw("sistem_pencernaan","Digestive System","\ud83c\udf4e",A.ao,A.bd,"Pupils can name the main digestive organs and explain what each one does, in order.","Where does food go after we swallow it?",30,"Use the children's own bodies as a map: point to the mouth, the chest (oesophagus), the belly (stomach). It makes hidden organs feel real.",A.aQA,A.anM,A.b1F,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to put the path of food in order (mouth \u2192 oesophagus \u2192 stomach \u2192 small intestine \u2192 large intestine) and name the job of each organ.","Use an analogy: mouth = grinder, stomach = blender, small intestine = a filter that takes out the goodness. Trace it slowly while pointing to your belly.",u.K)
A.PC=new B.f(A.Z,"Where does the air go?","Breathe in \u2014 air comes in. Then where does it go?",A.E,"Everyone, take a really deep breath. The air comes in through your nose, but then where does it go inside your body? And why do we have to keep on breathing?","Have the children take a breath and feel their chest expand.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.OV=new B.f(A.Y,"The air's path","Nose \u2192 windpipe \u2192 lungs.",A.v,"Take a big deep breath, hold it for a moment, then breathe out. Put your hand on your chest \u2014 what can you feel? The air went in, but where did it go? Where do you think it stops along the way, and what does your body take from that air?",null,"Draw a body: the nose, a line for the windpipe going down, two lungs in the chest. Add arrows for air going in and out.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.beY=new B.m("hidung","\ud83d\udc43","Nose","NOSE: the door where air comes in. It filters out dust and warms the air before it goes further inside. When the air is smoky, a mask helps filter out more.")
A.bbI=new B.m("tenggorokan","\ud83d\udd3d","Windpipe","WINDPIPE (trachea): the tube that carries air down from the nose to the lungs.")
A.baU=new B.m("paru","\ud83e\udec1","Lungs","LUNGS: two bags where the body takes oxygen from the air and gets rid of used air.")
A.bce=new B.m("diafragma","\u2b07\ufe0f","Diaphragm","DIAPHRAGM: a muscle below the lungs. It pulls down when we breathe in and pushes up when we breathe out.")
A.aQk=s([A.beY,A.bbI,A.baU,A.bce],t.w)
A.MI=new B.f(A.W,"Breathing organs","Tap each part to find out.",A.v,"Let's explore the organs we breathe with!",null,"Label each organ on the body drawing.",A.aQk,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.O0=new B.f(A.X,"Make a lung model!","Pull the base balloon \u2014 the balloon lung inflates.",A.v,"Now let's make our own lung model!","Use a bottle with a balloon inside (the lung) and a balloon over the base (the diaphragm). Pull the base balloon down \u2014 the inside balloon inflates (breathing in). Push it up \u2014 it deflates. (The teacher cuts the bottle.)",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b_P=new B.D("The air we breathe in goes into the tummy or stomach.","Air goes into the lungs through the windpipe (the airway), not into the stomach. The stomach is the path for food, not air.")
A.aYV=new B.D("We breathe in oxygen and breathe out oxygen too.","We breathe in air rich in oxygen and breathe out air rich in carbon dioxide. The lungs swap one for the other.")
A.b_l=new B.D("Vape smoke (from e-cigarettes) is just water vapour, so it's harmless.","Vape smoke is not just water vapour: it contains chemicals and usually nicotine, which are bad for the lungs \u2014 especially children's lungs, which are still growing. Lungs are healthiest breathing clean air.")
A.aPC=s([A.b_P,A.aYV,A.b_l],t.I)
A.Mx=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Which organ does the air finally reach?",A.E,"Now it's your turn to explain to Si Jabrik. Which organ does the air we breathe in finally reach? And which muscle helps us breathe in?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aPC)
A.SF=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","You breathe about 20,000 times every day!",A.a_,"Now we can answer our question from the start: the air goes into the lungs through the airway. This fact might surprise you. Without even thinking about it, we breathe about 20,000 TIMES every day \u2014 day and night, even while we sleep! Our body takes care of breathing all by itself, so we never forget to breathe. What an amazing gift! Tonight before bed, count how many breaths you take while someone at home slowly counts to 60. Tomorrow we'll compare everyone's numbers!","A round of applause \u2014 and a big deep breath together!",null,null,!1,null,"The Bajau, seafaring people who live in the waters around Sulawesi, can dive for a long time on a single breath. In 2018, scientists working in Central Sulawesi found that their spleens are about 50% bigger \u2014 the spleen stores oxygen-carrying red blood cells.","Sperm whales in oceans around the world can dive more than 1 km down into the pitch-dark deep sea and hold their breath for over an hour \u2014 their blood and muscles store lots of oxygen.",null)
A.aU5=s([A.PC,A.OV,A.MI,A.O0,A.Mx,A.SF],t.n)
A.aTc=s(["A plastic bottle with the bottom cut off","2 balloons (1 inside as the lung, 1 cut to make the base)","A straw + rubber band/sticky tape"],t.s)
A.aU3=s(["Fix a small balloon to the end of the straw, put it into the bottle's neck and seal it tightly (this is the lung).","Cover the cut bottom of the bottle with the stretched balloon (this is the diaphragm).","Pull the base balloon down \u2014 the balloon inside inflates (breathing in).","Push the base balloon up \u2014 the balloon inside deflates (breathing out)."],t.s)
A.a_C=new B.J("Bottle Lung Model","The diaphragm pulls down to draw air into the lungs.",A.aTc,A.aU3,"When we breathe, the diaphragm muscle below the lungs pulls down, so the lungs expand and air comes in. When the diaphragm rises, air is pushed out. The bottle model copies this movement: base balloon = diaphragm, inside balloon = lung.",A.D,"The teacher cuts the bottle \u2014 the edges can be sharp. Throw away leftover balloon pieces or burst balloons straight away (they can be swallowed); watch for children with a latex allergy.",20)
A.aM6=s(["1 balloon per child","A ruler or tape measure"],t.s)
A.aUM=s(["Take one deep breath, then blow into the balloon as hard as you can in ONE breath.","Pinch the balloon closed and measure around it with the tape/ruler.","Compare with friends: usually TALLER children make bigger balloons \u2014 their lungs really are bigger. This is about body size, not about who is better.","Write down the result. Measure again next year when you have grown taller, and compare with today's result."],t.s)
A.Z5=new B.J("Measure Your Breath","The lungs hold air; their capacity can be compared.",A.aM6,A.aUM,'One breath out pushes air out of the lungs; a bigger balloon means more air was breathed out. Everyone\'s lung capacity is different, and what matters most is BODY SIZE \u2014 especially height. So lung capacity grows as children grow. Swimmers and runners breathe more strongly not because their lungs have been "trained to get bigger", but because their heart, muscles and way of breathing become more efficient.',A.D,"Do not share balloons. Stop if you feel dizzy; breathe normally first. Children with asthma or a heart condition may blow gently or just watch \u2014 never force them. Throw away burst balloons straight away (they can be swallowed); watch for children with a latex allergy.",15)
A.aMo=s([A.a_C,A.Z5],t.T)
A.b6b=new B.aD("Cannot yet name a breathing organ.","Names a breathing organ (nose/lungs), but does not yet explain that air comes in when we breathe in.","Names 2 breathing organs AND explains that air comes in when we breathe in.")
A.b1W=new B.aC(u.eX,u.in,"Opening (5 minutes): Have the children take a deep breath and feel their chest expand. Ask the hook question.","Main activity (20 minutes): Trace the path of air: nose \u2192 windpipe \u2192 lungs. Explain the role of the diaphragm. Make a lung model from a bottle and balloons to show how breathing works.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. Close with the fact: we breathe about 20,000 times every day without having to think about it.","Pupils can name at least 2 breathing organs and explain that air comes in when we breathe in.",A.b2,u.d,A.b6b)
A.blq=new B.aw("sistem_pernapasan","The Respiratory System","\ud83e\udec1",A.ao,A.bd,u.eX,u.in,30,"Link to the Photosynthesis lesson (oxygen from plants) and to Digestion. The bottle-and-balloon model makes the diaphragm's movement easy to see.",A.aU5,A.aMo,A.b1W,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to count their breaths per minute while sitting and then after jumping, and to explain why they differ.",'Put a hand on the chest and take a deep breath \u2014 feel the chest expand as the lungs fill. Say "the air goes to the lungs".',u.K)
A.LO=new B.f(A.Z,"Why does the heart beat?","Put your hand on your chest \u2014 something's beating, isn't it?",A.E,"Everyone, put your hand on your chest. Can you feel something going thump-thump inside? That's your heart! What do you think your heart's job is inside your body?","Ask all the children to put a hand on their chest and feel their own heartbeat for a few seconds.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.RP=new B.f(A.Y,"The heart, our pump","The heart beats to push blood all around the body.",A.v,"Your heart is like a pump that never stops working. It has a left side and a right side, a top part and a bottom part, and every time it beats, it pushes blood out. Then the blood flows through tubes called blood vessels, some as thin as a hair, all around your body \u2014 right to the tips of your fingers and toes \u2014 and then back to the heart again. What do you think the blood carries around your body?",null,"Draw a simple heart in the middle of a chest, with lines for blood vessels spreading out to the arms, legs, and head. Add arrows going out of the heart and arrows coming back to the heart.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bcf=new B.m("jantung","\u2764\ufe0f","Heart","HEART: a muscle that pumps blood. It has left and right, top and bottom parts, and it keeps beating to pump blood all around the body.")
A.baX=new B.m("pembuluh_nadi","\u27a1\ufe0f","Arteries","ARTERIES: carry blood AWAY FROM the heart to the whole body, bringing oxygen and nutrients.")
A.bbK=new B.m("pembuluh_balik","\u2b05\ufe0f","Veins","VEINS: carry blood BACK to the heart, bringing carbon dioxide and waste from the body.")
A.bdV=new B.m("darah","\ud83e\ude78","Blood","BLOOD: a red liquid that carries oxygen and nutrients all around the body, then carries carbon dioxide back.")
A.aVW=s([A.bcf,A.baX,A.bbK,A.bdV],t.w)
A.Qa=new B.f(A.W,"Parts of the circulatory system","Tap each part \u2014 find out its job.",A.v,"Let's explore the parts that work together to carry blood all around our bodies!",null,"Label the heart and blood vessel drawing: heart, arteries, veins.",A.aVW,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.M2=new B.f(A.X,"Count your pulse!","Feel your pulse before and after jumping.",A.v,"Nurses at the community health centre count a pulse with just two fingers and a watch. Now let's be nurses: let's feel our hearts working for ourselves, by finding our pulse!","Do the Count Your Pulse experiment: while the children sit quietly, they put two fingers on the wrist or neck, count the beats for 15 seconds, then multiply by 4. Then ask all the children to jump up and down on the spot for 1 minute, and count their pulse again. Compare the two numbers.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b_V=new B.D("Blood is only inside the heart.","Blood flows all around the body through the blood vessels \u2014 the heart is just the pump, not the place where blood stays.")
A.b0v=new B.D("Arteries and veins are the same thing.","Arteries carry blood AWAY FROM the heart to the body, while veins carry blood BACK to the heart. They go in different directions.")
A.b_a=new B.D("The blood in veins is blue, because the veins in my arm look blue.","Blood is always red: bright red when it is full of oxygen, dark red on its way back to the heart. Veins look bluish because of the way light passes through the skin.")
A.aQE=s([A.b_V,A.b0v,A.b_a],t.I)
A.Tr=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Why does your pulse get faster after moving?",A.E,"Now it's your turn to explain to Si Jabrik. Just now, we felt our pulse get faster after jumping. Why do you think that happened? What was your heart doing then?","Ask the children to raise their hands and explain their reason, not just give a short answer. Praise every attempt to reason it out.",null,null,!0,'Ask the children to explain the REASON the heart beats faster, not just answer "yes, it\'s faster".',null,null,A.aQE)
A.Pb=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Your heart beats about 100,000 times every day!",A.a_,"Remember our question? Now you know: the heart beats faster when the body needs more oxygen, like after we've been running and jumping. And here's a surprise: your heart beats about 100,000 times every day! It pumps blood all around your body and never takes a rest. So look after your heart \u2014 move lots, eat your vegetables and fruit, never smoke, and get enough sleep. For hundreds of years, people thought blood was made all the time and then used up by the body. About 400 years ago, a doctor called William Harvey did careful sums and proved that blood goes round and round. Scientists get things wrong too \u2014 then they fix them with evidence!","Clap softly together to the rhythm of a heartbeat!",null,null,!1,null,"Donated blood can be separated into parts, such as red blood cells and plasma, so one bag of blood can help more than one patient. In Indonesia, healthy adults can give blood at the Indonesian Red Cross (PMI).","In 1967, a team of doctors in Cape Town, South Africa, moved a heart from a donor into another patient for the very first time. Today operations like this have helped many people in many countries.",null)
A.aOt=s([A.LO,A.RP,A.Qa,A.M2,A.Tr,A.Pb],t.n)
A.aV2=s(["The teacher's watch or phone with a timer (stopwatch)","Your own fingers"],t.s)
A.aPt=s(["Sit quietly for a moment, then put two fingers (not your thumb) on your wrist or on the side of your neck.","Feel the beat, then count how many beats there are in 15 seconds.","Multiply the number by 4 to get the number of beats per minute. Write it down.","Jump up and down on the spot for 1 minute, then straight away count your pulse again for 15 seconds and multiply by 4.","Compare the two numbers \u2014 which is bigger, and why do you think that is?"],t.s)
A.a_I=new B.J("Count Your Pulse","The heart pumps faster when the body needs more oxygen.",A.aV2,A.aPt,"Your pulse is the throb you feel when the heart pushes blood through the arteries. After moving (like jumping), the body's muscles need more oxygen, so the heart beats faster to send more blood, more quickly, all around the body. That is why your pulse feels faster after moving than when you are sitting quietly.",A.D,"Children with asthma or a heart condition may jump gently, or just sit and count their pulse \u2014 never force them.",10)
A.aLo=s(["A soft plastic bottle/old sauce bottle (one that can be squeezed)","Water mixed with red food colouring or a little red syrup","A plastic straw or a small tube","A container/basin to catch the water that comes out"],t.s)
A.aUC=s(["Fill the soft bottle with water coloured red (with food colouring or red syrup).","Fit the straw into the bottle cap or into a small hole in the cap, and seal it so it does not leak around the gaps.","Hold the bottle upright with the end of the straw pointing into the container.","Squeeze the bottle hard \u2014 the red water is pushed out through the straw, like blood pumped by the heart.","Let go, refill if needed, and repeat a few times."],t.s)
A.Zl=new B.J("Heart Pump Model","The muscular heart squeezes and pushes blood into the blood vessels.",A.aLo,A.aUC,"The heart is a muscle that keeps squeezing and relaxing. When it squeezes (contracts), the blood inside is pushed out into the blood vessels. Squeezing the bottle copies that movement: every time we squeeze the bottle, the red water is pushed out through the straw, like blood being pumped by the heart all around the body.",A.D,"Wipe up any spilled coloured water straight away, so the floor or table does not get slippery or stained.",15)
A.aRH=s([A.a_I,A.Zl],t.T)
A.b71=new B.aD("Cannot yet state the role of the heart.","States that the heart is a pump, but does not yet explain why the pulse is faster after moving.","States that the heart is a pump AND explains why the pulse is faster after moving.")
A.b1z=new B.aC(u.f0,u.d6,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the children to put a hand on their chest and feel their own heartbeat. Ask the hook question.","Main activity (20 minutes): Explain the heart as a pump, and the blood vessels (arteries & veins) that carry blood around the body. Do the Count Your Pulse experiment (sitting and after jumping) so the children feel the heart at work, then show the Heart Pump Model.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. Finish with a fact: the heart beats about 100,000 times every day without ever resting.","Pupils can state the heart's role as a pump and explain why the pulse gets faster after moving.",A.b2,u.d,A.b71)
A.blM=new B.aw("sistem_peredaran_darah","The Circulatory System","\u2764\ufe0f",A.ao,A.bd,u.f0,u.d6,30,"Link this to the Respiratory System lesson (oxygen from the lungs) and the Digestive System lesson (nutrients from food) \u2014 the blood carries both of these around the whole body.",A.aOt,A.aRH,A.b1z,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to explain why the blood leaving the heart (through the arteries) is different from the blood coming back to the heart (through the veins).",'Put a hand on your chest and feel the beat \u2014 say: "the heart pumps blood all around the body, and then the blood comes back again".',u.K)
A.Pa=new B.f(A.Z,"Why don't we flop?","Our bodies can stand up straight. What holds them up?",A.E,"Everyone, our bodies can stand up straight. They don't flop down like jelly. So what makes our bodies strong enough to stand?","Let the children feel their bodies and guess. No answer is wrong yet.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.QJ=new B.f(A.Y,"Skeleton & muscles","Bones hold us up, muscles move us.",A.v,"Feel your arm. There's something hard inside, and something soft around it. What could the hard part be? Now hold your forearm and wiggle your fingers up and down quickly \u2014 what moves in your arm? And how can hard bones move?",null,"Draw a simple person, showing the bones (lines inside) and the muscles (rounded shapes around the bones).",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.beH=new B.m("penopang","\ud83e\uddb4","Support","The SKELETON is a SUPPORT: it gives the body its shape and lets us stand up straight, like the posts of a house.")
A.bbR=new B.m("pelindung","\ud83d\udee1\ufe0f","Protection","The SKELETON also PROTECTS: the skull protects the brain, and the ribs protect the heart and lungs.")
A.bex=new B.m("otot","\ud83d\udcaa","Muscles","MUSCLES are MOVERS: by tightening and relaxing, muscles pull on bones so the body can move. Many of the muscles that move your fingers are in your forearm, pulling on them through tendons (tough cords).")
A.ba1=new B.m("sendi","\ud83e\uddb5","Joints","JOINTS: where two bones meet so they can bend, like the elbow and knee. Without joints, the body would be stiff.")
A.aSW=s([A.beH,A.bbR,A.bex,A.ba1],t.w)
A.LF=new B.f(A.W,"What does each one do?","Tap each card to find out.",A.v,"Let's find out what the skeleton and muscles do!",null,"Write the jobs of the skeleton and muscles on the blackboard.",A.aSW,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.LQ=new B.f(A.X,"Feel your muscles!","Bend your arm and feel the muscle bulge.",A.v,"Now feel your muscles at work!","Ask the children to feel their upper arm, then bend the arm as if lifting a weight. They feel the biceps bulge and harden as it tightens, then go soft when the arm is straightened.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b07=new B.D("Bones can move by themselves.","Bones don't move by themselves \u2014 muscles pull them. Have the children feel their arm muscle as they bend the elbow; it goes hard.")
A.b0L=new B.D("Bones are dead things, like stones.","Bones are alive: they can grow and join back together if they break, and inside them the body makes blood cells.")
A.aLV=s([A.b07,A.b0L],t.I)
A.QA=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","What moves our bones?",A.E,"Now it's your turn to explain to Si Jabrik. What makes our bones move? And which bone protects our brain?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aLV)
A.S0=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Babies are born with \xb1300 bones, adults have just 206!",A.a_,"Remember our question \u2014 why don't our bodies flop down like jelly? Now you know the answer! Did you know? When babies are born, they have about 300 bones. But grown-ups have only 206 bones. Where did the rest go? Some bones fuse (join together) as we grow up. So your bones are working hard at growing right now! Test it yourself: tomorrow morning and evening, ask someone at home to mark your height on a door frame with a pencil (ask permission first). Many people are about 1 cm taller in the morning, because the soft discs between the bones of the spine get squashed during the day and spring back while we sleep!",u.G,null,null,!1,null,"In 1985, Pratiwi Sudarmono, an Indonesian woman scientist, was chosen as an astronaut candidate for a Space Shuttle mission. The mission was later cancelled, but she is remembered as Indonesia's first astronaut candidate.","Astronauts who live for months on the International Space Station (a big home that circles the Earth) can lose some of their bone and muscle strength, because their bodies don't have to work against gravity. That's why they exercise for about 2 hours every day.",null)
A.aQL=s([A.Pa,A.QJ,A.LF,A.LQ,A.QA,A.S0],t.n)
A.aWq=s(["Your own arm","The palm of your other hand, to feel with"],t.s)
A.al1=s(["Straighten one arm and feel the top of it with your other hand.","Bend the arm upwards (as if lifting a weight).","Notice: the muscle in your upper arm (biceps) BULGES and gets hard.","Straighten it again \u2014 the muscle goes soft. Muscles work by tightening & relaxing."],t.s)
A.a_7=new B.J("Muscles That Tighten","Muscles move bones by tightening (contracting).",A.aWq,A.al1,"Muscles are attached to bones. When a muscle tightens (gets shorter), it pulls the bone so the arm bends. Bones give the body its shape and hold it up; muscles move them. They work together as the movement system.",A.D,null,5)
A.akU=s(["A hand shape cut from card","Straws cut into short pieces","Thread/string + glue"],t.s)
A.aRU=s(["Draw & cut out a hand shape from card.","Glue straw pieces along each finger as the finger sections.","Thread string through the straw pieces on each finger and tie it at the fingertip.","Pull the strings at the wrist end \u2014 the fingers bend as if gripping!"],t.s)
A.a0Q=new B.J("Straw Robot Hand",'Muscles pull "tendons" to bend the finger joints.',A.akU,A.aRU,"The string works like a tendon being pulled by a muscle. When it is pulled, the card finger bends at the joints; when it is let go, the finger straightens again. That is how the muscles in our arm pull tendons to move our fingers.",A.D,"Take care with scissors; use child-safe scissors.",30)
A.aLA=s(["A bone from cooked chicken (e.g. a thigh bone), cleaned & washed by the teacher","Enough vinegar + a jar with a lid"],t.s)
A.aR8=s(["Try to bend the chicken bone (not yet soaked) \u2014 it is hard and will not bend.","Soak the bone in vinegar in the closed jar for 2\u20133 days.","Change the vinegar if needed. Observe bubbles coming from the bone.","Take out & rinse the bone, then try bending it again \u2014 now it bends like rubber!"],t.s)
A.ZO=new B.J("Bendy Bone","A hard substance (calcium) makes bones stiff; without it, bones bend.",A.aLA,A.aR8,"Bones are hard because they contain calcium. The acid in vinegar slowly dissolves that calcium, so the bone that is left becomes bendy. That is why we need foods with calcium to keep our bones strong.",A.D,"Use a bone from cooked chicken, prepared by the teacher \u2014 not a raw chicken bone. This bone & vinegar are not for eating. Wash hands after handling it.",4320)
A.aPS=s([A.a_7,A.a0Q,A.ZO],t.T)
A.b6X=new B.aD("Cannot yet name a job of the skeleton, or thinks bones move by themselves.","Names jobs of the skeleton, but does not yet explain what muscles do.","Names 2 jobs of the skeleton AND explains that muscles move bones by tightening.")
A.b2v=new B.aC("Pupils can explain what the skeleton (bones) and muscles do and how they work together to move the body.",u.fQ,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Have the children imagine a body with no bones.","Main activity (20 minutes): Explain the skeleton as support & protection, and muscles as movers. Do the experiment of feeling the biceps tighten. Discuss how bones and muscles work together.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: an adult human body has 206 bones, but babies are born with about 300 bones.","Pupils can name at least 2 jobs of the skeleton and explain that muscles move bones by tightening.",A.b2,u.d,A.b6X)
A.blR=new B.aw("rangka_otot","Skeleton & Muscles","\ud83e\uddb4",A.ao,A.bd,"Pupils can explain what the skeleton and muscles do and how they work together to move the body.",u.fQ,30,"The children's own bodies are the best teaching aid. Lots of feeling and bending makes skeleton & muscles click straight away.",A.aQL,A.aPS,A.b2v,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to point to 3 joints (elbow, knee, finger) and act out which muscle pulls when each one bends.",'Demonstrate: bend your elbow and feel the arm muscle go hard \u2014 that\'s the muscle pulling the bone. Repeat together while saying "muscles pull bones".',u.K)
A.NZ=new B.f(A.Z,"What if the grass vanished?","Imagine all the grass on Earth disappeared. Then what?",A.E,"Everyone, imagine if suddenly all the grass and plants on Earth disappeared. What do you think would happen to the animals?","Let the children guess the knock-on effects. No answer is wrong yet.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.MJ=new B.f(A.Y,"Who eats whom","Grass \u2192 grasshopper \u2192 bird \u2192 ...",A.v,"Think about your breakfast this morning. Rice comes from rice plants, and rice plants grow with help from the Sun. If you eat chicken, what does the chicken eat? What do you think \u2014 if we keep tracing it back, where does all our food start?",null,'Draw: grass \u2192 grasshopper \u2192 bird \u2192 snake, linked with arrows (arrow direction = "is eaten by").',null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.baS=new B.m("produsen","\ud83c\udf31","Producers","PRODUCERS: plants that make their own food from sunlight. They are the start of every food chain.")
A.bcP=new B.m("konsumen1","\ud83e\udd97","Plant-eaters","PRIMARY CONSUMERS: plant-eating animals (herbivores), like grasshoppers, rats and cows. They eat producers.")
A.bb7=new B.m("konsumen2","\ud83e\udd85","Meat-eaters","SECONDARY CONSUMERS: meat-eating animals (carnivores), like birds, snakes and eagles. They eat other consumers.")
A.beh=new B.m("pengurai","\ud83c\udf44","Decomposers","DECOMPOSERS: fungi and bacteria that break down dead things into rich soil. They recycle so plants can grow again. But they can hardly break down plastic, so plastic lasts a very long time.")
A.awv=s([A.baS,A.bcP,A.bb7,A.beh],t.w)
A.MH=new B.f(A.W,"Each living thing's role","Tap each role to find out.",A.v,"Let's find out the role of each living thing in a food chain!",null,"Label the role of each living thing in the chain.",A.awv,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Q_=new B.f(A.X,"Build your chain!","Build a food chain from the garden.",A.v,"Now it's your turn to be food chain detectives!","Have the children build a food chain from their surroundings (rice plant \u2192 rat \u2192 snake \u2192 eagle). If possible, look around the garden/yard and find real examples.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b_C=new B.D("Food chain arrows point from the eater to the thing being eaten.",'The arrows show which way energy flows: from the thing eaten TO the eater (grass \u2192 rat \u2192 snake). Say "is eaten by" as you draw them.')
A.aZL=new B.D("If the predators (snakes) are all gone, the rats will surely do better.","At first the rats increase, but too many rats eat up all the grass, and then the rats go hungry. Everything in the chain is connected.")
A.aYU=new B.D("Plastic rubbish in the sea breaks down and disappears in the end.","Plastic only breaks into very tiny pieces (microplastics). These can be eaten by small sea animals, then by fish, and so get into the food chain. Using less single-use plastic helps.")
A.aSB=s([A.b_C,A.aZL,A.aYU],t.I)
A.Qh=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","What is the role of plants in a food chain?",A.E,"Si Jabrik isn't sure. Can you help him? What is the role of plants in a food chain? And if the snake in our chain disappeared, what would happen to the number of rats?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aSB)
A.MN=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","All the food on Earth starts with the Sun!",A.a_,"We've answered our question from the start: if the grass disappeared, the whole food chain would collapse. Here's the surprising part: every food chain on the whole Earth really starts with the SUN. Plants catch sunlight and turn it into food, then that energy flows to insects, to birds, all the way to us. So the energy in your body really comes from the Sun! And this web of life isn't fully mapped yet: researchers in Indonesia keep discovering new kinds of animals and plants in our forests and seas, from tiny frogs to orchids. Who knows \u2014 the next discoverer might be you!","A round of applause for the amazing web of life!",null,null,!1,null,"In a number of areas in Indonesia, farmers put up owl houses in the middle of the rice fields. Barn owls can find a rat in the dark just from the sound it makes, so the food chain helps protect the rice and less rat poison is needed.","In Yellowstone National Park in the United States, wolves were brought back in 1995 after about 70 years away. There were fewer elk eating young trees, and in some places willow trees grew back \u2014 one predator changed a lot!",null)
A.aL1=s([A.NZ,A.MJ,A.MH,A.Q_,A.Qh,A.MN],t.n)
A.aW9=s(["A garden, yard or park near the school","A book & pencil for taking notes","Sharp eyes"],t.s)
A.aUK=s(["Look around the garden: find plants (leaves, grass), then the insects that eat them.","Find animals that eat those insects (birds, house geckos, frogs).","Put them in order: grass \u2192 grasshopper \u2192 bird.","Discuss: what would happen if one of them disappeared?"],t.s)
A.ZJ=new B.J("Food Chain Detectives","Every living thing is connected through who-eats-whom.",A.aW9,A.aUK,"In an ecosystem, living things are connected by food chains: plants (producers) are eaten by plant-eating animals (consumers), which are eaten by meat-eating animals, and when they die they are broken down by decomposers. If one link in the chain disappears, the others are affected too.",A.D,"Only observe; do not disturb or hurt the animals.",20)
A.aMs=s(["Name cards for living things (sun, grass, grasshopper, bird, snake, fungus)","A ball of string/yarn"],t.s)
A.aeu=s(["Each child holds one card and they stand in a circle.",'Start with the sun: throw the ball of string to a living thing that "eats it" \u2014 each child holds on to their bit of string, making a web.',"Once the web is made, ask one living thing (e.g. the grass) to pull its string.","Any child who feels the pull pulls too \u2014 the effect spreads to everyone."],t.s)
A.a0c=new B.J("String Food Web","All living things are connected; lose one and the others are affected.",A.aMs,A.aeu,"In an ecosystem, living things are connected through food chains and food webs. When one part is disturbed (e.g. the grass runs out), the tug is felt across the whole web \u2014 a sign that all living things depend on each other.",A.D,null,20)
A.aSq=s([A.ZJ,A.a0c],t.T)
A.b6Y=new B.aD("Cannot yet put an eat-and-be-eaten sequence in order.","Builds a food chain, but does not yet tell producers and consumers apart.","Builds a food chain in the correct order AND names the roles of producers and consumers.")
A.b1h=new B.aC("Pupils can build a simple food chain and explain the roles of producers, consumers and decomposers in an ecosystem.",u.bS,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Let the children guess the knock-on effects of losing the plants.","Main activity (20 minutes): Introduce producers (plants), consumers (animals) and decomposers. Build a food chain together, e.g. rice plant \u2192 rat \u2192 snake \u2192 eagle. Discuss how living things depend on each other.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: the Sun is the original source of energy for every food chain on Earth.","Pupils can build one food chain in the correct order and name the roles of producers and consumers.",A.l9,u.d,A.b6Y)
A.bmc=new B.aw("rantai_makanan","Food Chains & Ecosystems","\ud83c\udf3e",A.ao,A.bd,"Pupils can build a simple food chain and explain the roles of producers, consumers and decomposers.",u.bS,30,"Use local animals and plants the children know (rice plants, rats, snakes, eagles). A food chain from their own surroundings feels more real.",A.aL1,A.aSq,A.b1h,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to make a 4-step food chain from a nearby garden and show which way the energy-flow arrows point.",'Lay out cards/pictures of grass\u2013rat\u2013snake and draw "is eaten by" arrows. Say "energy flows from the grass to the rat to the snake".',u.x)
A.R8=new B.f(A.Z,"Get the salt back?","Salt disappears into water. Can we get it back?",A.E,'Everyone, when we stir salt into water, it "disappears" \u2014 it dissolves. But has the salt really gone? Can we get it back again, do you think?',"Let the children guess how. There are no wrong answers yet.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.QF=new B.f(A.Y,"Mixtures can be separated","Two things mixed together can be separated again.",A.v,"When two or more things are mixed together, we call it a MIXTURE \u2014 like coffee or sandy water. The interesting thing is, we can separate a mixture again if we use the right method. When rice is tossed on a flat woven winnowing tray, it is being separated from its husks and dirt! Can you guess how to separate sand from water?",null,"Draw 2 methods: a funnel + cloth filtering muddy water, and a plate of salty water drying in the sun.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bcz=new B.m("menyaring","\ud83e\uddfd","Filtering","FILTERING: separates solids that do not dissolve (sand, pulp) from a liquid, using a cloth or a sieve.")
A.bf5=new B.m("menguapkan","\u2600\ufe0f","Evaporating","EVAPORATING: separates a dissolved substance (salt, sugar) by evaporating the water \u2014 the substance is left as crystals.")
A.bdS=new B.m("larut","\ud83e\uddc2","Dissolves","DISSOLVES: salt and sugar dissolve in water until you can't see them, but you can still taste them \u2014 proof they're there.")
A.bf6=new B.m("tak_larut","\ud83e\udea8","Doesn't dissolve","DOESN'T DISSOLVE: sand and oil do not dissolve in water. Sand is separated by filtering; oil by leaving it to stand until it floats, then skimming it off the top.")
A.aW_=s([A.bcz,A.bf5,A.bdS,A.bf6],t.w)
A.MV=new B.f(A.W,"Ways to separate mixtures","Tap each method to find out.",A.v,"Let's find out how to separate mixtures!",null,"Write an example of each method on the blackboard.",A.aW_,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Ni=new B.f(A.X,"Filter & dry!","Filter dirty water, dry salty water in the sun.",A.v,"Now we're going to be mixture-separating scientists!","Filter dirty water through a cloth \u2014 watch the water become clear and the dirt get caught. Leave a little salty water on a plate in the sun until the water evaporates, and see the salt crystals appear. (This water is not for drinking.)",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.aYW=new B.D("Once salt has dissolved, it is gone forever.","The salt has not gone, it has only dissolved. When the water evaporates, the salt is left behind again. Dry a little salty water in the sun to prove it.")
A.b_Y=new B.D("All mixtures are separated in the same way.","The methods are different: sand + water is filtered, salty water is evaporated, iron filings are picked out with a magnet. Match the method to the properties of the materials.")
A.aQl=s([A.aYW,A.b_Y],t.I)
A.Rl=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","How do we get salt back out of salty water?",A.E,"Who can explain this for Si Jabrik? What method do we use to get the salt back out of salty water? And what method do we use to separate sand from water?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aQl)
A.T3=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","The salt in your kitchen comes from evaporated seawater!",A.a_,"Now we've answered our question from the start: the water evaporates and the salt is left behind. Did you know? Most of the table salt we use every day is made by EVAPORATING seawater. Seawater is let into shallow ponds called salt pans, dried by the sun until the water evaporates, and the white salt is left behind \u2014 just like our experiment! But one mixture is still a puzzle: tiny bits of plastic (microplastics) in rivers and the sea. Researchers are still looking for the best way to separate them out \u2014 perhaps one of you will help find it.","A round of applause for salt farmers and the science of separating!",null,null,!1,null,"Water treatment works make the mud in river water settle with the help of alum, then filter it through sand. In the Thousand Islands off Jakarta, seawater is even pushed hard through an ultra-fine membrane: the salt stays behind and fresh water gets through.","In France, the scientist Marie Curie spent years separating tonnes of rock to get a tiny amount of radium, a new element she discovered with her husband, Pierre. She became the first person to win two Nobel Prizes.",null)
A.aKI=s([A.R8,A.QF,A.MV,A.Ni,A.Rl,A.T3],t.n)
A.aUZ=s(["Dirty water (water + soil/sand) and salty water","Cloth/tissue + a funnel or cut-off bottle as a filter","A plate + sunshine (to evaporate the salty water)"],t.s)
A.aws=s(["Pour the dirty water through the cloth filter into another container \u2014 the dirt is caught.","Observe: the water is clearer, and the sand/soil is left on the cloth.","Pour a little salty water onto a plate and leave it in the sun for a few hours.","Once the water has evaporated, look at the white salt crystals left on the plate."],t.s)
A.a0t=new B.J("Filtering & Evaporating","Mixtures can be separated: filter out dirt, evaporate salty water.",A.aUZ,A.aws,"A mixture is two or more substances mixed together. We can separate them: filtering separates solids that do not dissolve (sand) from water; evaporating separates a dissolved substance (salt), which is left behind when the water evaporates.",A.D,"This filtered water is NOT for drinking \u2014 it is only for the experiment. If you use a cut-off bottle, the teacher cuts it.",180)
A.aTy=s(["Filter paper/tissue cut into long strips","Water-based marker pens (black/brown are the most exciting)","A glass with a little water"],t.s)
A.aOn=s(["Make a thick dot/line of marker about 2 cm from the bottom end of the paper.","Dip the bottom end of the paper in the water (the ink dot must NOT be under water).","Leave it: the water creeps up past the ink.","Observe: the ink splits into several colours that travel different distances."],t.s)
A.a_K=new B.J("Marker Pen Rainbow","Black/coloured ink is often a mixture of several colours.",A.aTy,A.aOn,"The water creeps up, carrying the ink. Each colour in the ink moves at a different speed, so they separate into bands of colour. Black ink turns out to be a mixture of many colours \u2014 this method is called chromatography.",A.D,"Use water-based markers, not permanent ones.",20)
A.aST=s(["A mixture of sand/rice with small iron things (safety pins, paper clips, iron filings)","A magnet","A sheet of paper"],t.s)
A.aRR=s(["Stir the sand/rice and the small iron things until mixed.","Wrap the magnet in paper (so it is easy to release what it picks up).","Bring the magnet close to the mixture.","Observe: the iron things are pulled out and separated; the sand is left behind."],t.s)
A.a04=new B.J("Separating with a Magnet","Mixtures can be separated by their magnetic properties.",A.aST,A.aRR,"A magnet only attracts things made of iron. By bringing a magnet close, the magnetic material is separated from the mixture while everything else is left behind. This is how mixtures are separated by their magnetic properties \u2014 it is also used in recycling plants.",A.D,"Take care with sharp things like safety pins; the teacher hands them out and collects them again. Keep iron filings away from the eyes \u2014 if possible, keep them in a closed bag/container. Small magnets must not go in the mouth.",15)
A.aUh=s([A.a0t,A.a_K,A.a04],t.T)
A.b5Z=new B.aD("Cannot yet name a way to separate a mixture.","Names one way to separate a mixture, but does not yet know when it is used.","Names 2 ways to separate mixtures AND gives an example of when each is used.")
A.b23=new B.aC(u.df,u.kz,"Opening (5 minutes): Show some muddy water and some salty water. Ask the hook question about separating mixtures.","Main activity (20 minutes): Introduce mixtures and ways to separate them \u2014 filtering for things that do not dissolve, evaporating for dissolved substances. Do the experiments of filtering dirty water and evaporating salty water.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: table salt is made by evaporating seawater in salt pans.","Pupils can name 2 ways to separate mixtures and give an example of when each is used.",A.b2,u.e,A.b5Z)
A.blh=new B.aw("campuran_memisahkan","Mixtures & Separating","\ud83e\uddea",A.ao,A.ci,u.df,u.kz,30,"Link it to everyday life: straining coconut milk, drying salted fish in the sun, salt pans. The science of separating is in the kitchen and all around the children.",A.aKI,A.aUh,A.b23,null,!0,!0,"Ask the children to choose the right way to separate 3 different mixtures (sand + water, salty water, rice + gravel) and explain why.",'Leave a little salty water in a shallow container in the sun; once the water has dried up, show the salt left behind. Say "the water goes, the salt stays".',null)
A.To=new B.f(A.Z,"Sour vs slippery?","Oranges taste sour, soap feels slippery. What's the difference?",A.E,"Everyone, orange juice tastes sour on your tongue, but soapy water feels slippery and tastes bitter. So what makes them so different?","Let the children name other things that taste sour. No wrong answers.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.P9=new B.f(A.Y,"Acid, base, neutral","Substances can be acidic, basic or neutral.",A.v,"Imagine the taste of a lime on your tongue. Now imagine holding a slippery bar of soap. They feel very different, don't they? Well, the substances around us can be sorted into groups by this difference. What do you think \u2014 how can we tell which group something belongs to, without tasting it?",null,"Make 3 columns: ACID (purple/red) | NEUTRAL (blue) | BASE (green). Leave space to write the test results.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bcc=new B.m("asam","\ud83c\udf4b","Acid","ACID: tastes sour, like oranges, vinegar and tomatoes. Turns butterfly-pea water purple to reddish.")
A.baJ=new B.m("basa","\ud83e\uddfc","Base","BASE: feels slippery/tastes bitter, like soap, slaked lime paste and baking soda. Turns butterfly-pea water green. Farmers spread farm lime on rice fields or gardens whose soil is too acidic.")
A.bbG=new B.m("netral","\ud83d\udca7","Neutral","NEUTRAL: neither acid nor base, like plain water. Hardly changes the indicator's colour.")
A.bdM=new B.m("indikator","\ud83c\udfa8","Indicator","INDICATOR: something that changes colour to show acid or base. Butterfly-pea flowers are a natural indicator.")
A.aOG=s([A.bcc,A.baJ,A.bbG,A.bdM],t.w)
A.RU=new B.f(A.W,"Meet acids & bases","Tap each card to find out.",A.v,"Let's get to know acids, bases and indicators!",null,"Write each material's test result in its column.",A.aOG,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.MX=new B.f(A.X,"Test with butterfly-pea water!","Drip things into blue butterfly-pea water. Watch the colour.",A.v,"Now let's test acids and bases with our magic liquid!","Pour the blue butterfly-pea water into several glasses. Add drops of lime juice (turns purple/red = acid) and slaked-lime water/baking soda water (turns green = base). Ask the children to guess, then watch the change. Not for drinking.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b04=new B.D("Anything acidic is safe to taste to find out what it is.","Not everything is safe to taste (some cleaning products are strong bases and dangerous). We identify acids and bases with the colour of the butterfly-pea indicator, not by tasting.")
A.b_n=new B.D("The butterfly-pea water changes colour because of magic or dirt.","It changes colour because the natural indicator reacts with acid or base \u2014 that's science, a simple way to recognise what a liquid is like.")
A.aVn=s([A.b04,A.b_n],t.I)
A.R5=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","What colour does lime juice turn butterfly-pea water?",A.E,"Si Jabrik is confused \u2014 help him out! What colour does sour lime juice turn the butterfly-pea water? And what colour does slaked lime paste, which is a base, turn it?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aVn)
A.N5=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Your stomach uses strong acid to digest food!",A.a_,"Now we can answer our question from the start: oranges are acidic, and soap is a base. And here's the surprising part: inside our stomach there's an ACID strong enough to help break down our food. But don't worry \u2014 the stomach wall has a special protective lining, so it doesn't get hurt by its own acid. Our bodies are amazingly clever! Try this at home: ask an adult to cut a small piece of turmeric, rub it on paper until it turns yellow, then add a few drops of soapy washing water. The yellow turns reddish-brown \u2014 turmeric is an indicator too!","A round of applause for colourful acid-and-base science!",null,null,!1,null,"Indonesia's seas are part of the Coral Triangle, home to about three-quarters of the world's kinds of coral. Researchers check how acidic the water around our reefs is, because more acidic water makes it harder for corals to build their limestone skeletons.","All over the world, the sea soaks up carbon dioxide from the air. As a result, seawater \u2014 which is actually slightly basic \u2014 is now about 30% more acidic than it was about 200 years ago.",null)
A.aAX=s([A.To,A.P9,A.RU,A.MX,A.R5,A.N5],t.n)
A.aKp=s(["A handful of butterfly-pea flowers \u2014 they often grow on fences and in gardens","Hot water for steeping (PREPARED BY THE TEACHER)","Small glasses + things to test: lime juice/vinegar, water with a little slaked lime paste stirred in/baking soda water, plain water"],t.s)
A.a2l=s(["The teacher steeps the butterfly-pea flowers in hot water until the water turns BLUE, then lets it cool. This is the indicator liquid.","Pour the blue butterfly-pea water into several glasses.","Add drops of lime juice/vinegar to one glass \u2014 the blue turns PURPLE to pink (acid).","Add drops of slaked-lime water/baking soda water to another glass \u2014 the blue turns GREEN (base)."],t.s)
A.a_u=new B.J("Magic Butterfly-Pea Water","A natural indicator changes colour in acids and bases.",A.aKp,A.a2l,"Butterfly-pea water is a blue natural indicator. Acids (lime, vinegar) turn it reddish-purple; bases (slaked lime paste, baking soda) turn it green; neutral substances (plain water) leave it blue. This is a simple way to test for acids and bases with cheap, easy-to-find materials.",A.ct,"The hot water (for steeping) is prepared by the TEACHER. The test liquids are NOT for drinking. Wash hands after the experiment; keep slaked lime paste away from the eyes.",30)
A.aTX=s(["Baking soda (base)","Vinegar or lime juice (acid)","A glass + a mat/tray"],t.s)
A.aR4=s(["Put 1 spoonful of baking soda in the glass.","Pour in a little vinegar/lime juice.","Observe: it fizzes and hisses straight away.","Discuss: what gas makes the foam?"],t.s)
A.a_L=new B.J("Acid Meets Base","Acids and bases react to make a gas (fizzing).",A.aTX,A.aR4,"When an acid (vinegar) meets a base (baking soda), they react and make carbon dioxide gas \u2014 that is the hissing foam. Acid-base reactions like this are also used in cakes to make them rise.",A.D,"Not for drinking. Keep out of eyes; do it over a tray.",10)
A.aRN=s(["1 raw egg","Enough vinegar + a glass"],t.s)
A.aVs=s(["Put the egg in the glass and pour in vinegar until the egg is covered.","Observe: tiny bubbles appear on the surface of the shell.","Leave it for 1\u20132 days (change the vinegar if needed).","Take the egg out: the shell has dissolved, leaving a rubbery egg in a skin."],t.s)
A.YF=new B.J("Egg Without a Shell","An acid (vinegar) reacts with and dissolves the eggshell, which is a base.",A.aRN,A.aVs,"Eggshell contains calcium, which is a base. The acid in vinegar reacts with it, making a gas (bubbles) and dissolving the shell, leaving the rubbery skin. The same acid-base reaction wears away teeth if they often meet acidic food.",A.D,"This egg is NOT for eating. Wash hands after handling it.",2880)
A.aOT=s([A.a_u,A.a_L,A.YF],t.T)
A.b5R=new B.aD("Cannot yet read the indicator's colour change.","Sees the colour change, but does not yet link it to acid or base.","Sorts 2 materials into acid or base based on the indicator's colour change.")
A.b25=new B.aC(u.eF,u.bL,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Collect sour-tasting examples the children know (citrus, vinegar).","Main activity (20 minutes): Introduce acids, bases and neutral substances. Show the blue butterfly-pea water as an indicator that changes colour. Test several materials and group the results.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: our body keeps its acids and bases in balance, and the stomach uses acid to digest food.","Pupils can sort at least 2 materials into acid or base based on the indicator's colour change.",A.b2,u.e,A.b5R)
A.blZ=new B.aw("asam_basa","Acids & Bases","\ud83e\uddeb",A.ao,A.ci,u.eF,u.bL,30,"The colour change of butterfly-pea water always amazes children. The flowers are easy to find (they often grow on fences). Steep the indicator before class so there is enough time to test many materials.",A.aAX,A.aOT,A.b25,null,!0,!0,"Ask the children to test 3 kitchen liquids (vinegar, soapy water, sugar water) with butterfly-pea water and sort them into acid, base or neutral.",'Drip lime juice and then slaked-lime water into butterfly-pea water; observe the two different colours. Say "acids and bases give different colours".',null)
A.QS=new B.f(A.Z,"Where do crystals come from?","Rock sugar & gems have beautiful shapes \u2014 how do they form?",A.E,"Everyone, have you ever seen rock sugar or a sparkling gemstone with neat, sharp corners? Where do you think such beautiful shapes come from?",u.n,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.R3=new B.f(A.Y,"Crystals from a solution","Water evaporates \u2192 the dissolved stuff becomes crystals.",A.v,"Look closely at these grains of rock sugar or coarse salt \u2014 use a magnifying glass if you have one. They're neat, with sharp corners, as if someone shaped them. But nobody carved them! What do you think \u2014 how could such neat shapes happen all by themselves?",null,"Draw a glass of solution with a thread hanging in it, then draw tiny crystals stuck to the thread.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bdk=new B.m("jenuh","\ud83e\uddc2","Saturated solution","SATURATED SOLUTION: water so full of salt it can't dissolve any more. This is what we use to make crystals.")
A.baZ=new B.m("menguap_k","\u2600\ufe0f","Evaporating","EVAPORATING: as the water slowly evaporates, the salt it can no longer hold starts to stick and pile up. The white lines on a hat or shirt that dries after sweating are salt crystals from sweat too!")
A.bbk=new B.m("tumbuh_k","\ud83d\udc8e","Growing","GROWING: crystals get bigger as more water evaporates. The slower it evaporates, the neater their shape.")
A.bb8=new B.m("sudut","\ud83d\udd37","Sharp corners","SHARP CORNERS: crystals have neat, angled shapes because their particles line up in a regular pattern.")
A.aUY=s([A.bdk,A.baZ,A.bbk,A.bb8],t.w)
A.Nh=new B.f(A.W,"Secrets of crystals","Tap each card to find out.",A.v,"Let's explore the secrets of how crystals grow!",null,"Write the crystal facts on the blackboard.",A.aUY,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Om=new B.f(A.X,"Hang a thread in the solution!","Dip a thread in salt solution and wait for crystals.",A.v,"Now let's start our crystal-growing project!","Dissolve salt in warm water until it is saturated. Hang a thread from a pencil so it dips into the solution. Put it somewhere still. Observe for a few days until crystals grow on the thread.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b0B=new B.D("Crystals grow like living things.","Crystals are not alive; they form when salt particles line up neatly as the water evaporates, not by growing like a plant.")
A.b_t=new B.D("Salt disappears when it dissolves, then new salt appears as crystals.","The same salt comes back: as the water evaporates, the salt particles line up into crystals. No new salt is made.")
A.aOA=s([A.b0B,A.b_t],t.I)
A.Ra=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","What makes crystals start to form?",A.E,"Who can explain this for Si Jabrik? What has to happen to the water for salt crystals to start forming? And why does the solution have to be saturated first?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aOA)
A.SB=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Gemstones in nature grow for millions of years!",A.a_,"Now we can answer our question from the start: crystals grow slowly from a solution. Did you know? Our crystals grow in just a few days. But quartz crystals deep in the Earth grow from hot liquids through a similar process over THOUSANDS or even MILLIONS of years! Diamonds are different: they form from carbon squeezed and heated very strongly deep inside the Earth. That's why gems are so precious \u2014 nature takes a very, very long time to make them. One of the biggest natural crystals ever found, in a cave in Mexico, is more than 10 metres long \u2014 as tall as a three-storey house! Our crystals are much smaller, but they are still real data: measure them and write it down every morning.","A round of applause for sparkling crystal science!",null,null,!1,null,"In the limestone caves of Maros in South Sulawesi, drips of limy water slowly build stalactites \u2014 crystal limestone that may grow only about 1 cm in a hundred years. On the walls of those caves there are also ancient hand stencils tens of thousands of years old!",'In chip factories in many countries, extremely pure silicon is grown into big crystal bars, then sliced very thinly to make the "brains" (chips) of phones and computers, and the cells of most solar panels.',null)
A.aTM=s([A.QS,A.R3,A.Nh,A.Om,A.Ra,A.SB],t.n)
A.aMA=s(["Plenty of table salt (or sugar)","Warm water (PREPARED BY THE TEACHER)","A clear glass, thread, and a pencil to hang the thread from"],t.s)
A.aSF=s(["The teacher dissolves salt in the warm water, stirring until no more will dissolve (a saturated solution).",'Dip the thread in water, then in dry salt so it gets a coating of crystal "seeds"; let it dry for a moment.',"Tie the thread to the pencil and lay the pencil across the top of the glass so the thread hangs down into the solution without touching the bottom.","Put it somewhere safe and don't shake it.","Wait a few days: salt crystals stick to the thread and grow!","\ud83d\udca1 If there are no crystals after 3 days, the solution is not saturated enough \u2014 the teacher reheats the water and dissolves MORE salt until some is left over that won't dissolve. Keep it in a warm, uncovered place where it won't be shaken; a covered glass stops the water from evaporating."],t.s)
A.ZN=new B.J("Growing Salt Crystals","A dissolved substance forms crystals when its water slowly evaporates.",A.aMA,A.aSF,'Warm water can dissolve a lot of salt. As the water slowly evaporates, the salt that the water can no longer hold "sticks together" and forms crystals on the thread. The longer the water takes to evaporate, the bigger the crystals grow. This is how crystals form in nature too, it just takes much, much longer.',A.D,"The teacher heats and pours the water and dissolves the salt; children only hold the glass once the teacher has checked the solution is lukewarm, not hot. The crystals are not for eating.",7200)
A.aM3=s(["Granulated sugar (lots) + warm water (PREPARED BY THE TEACHER)","A clear glass, a satay skewer/thread, a clothes peg"],t.s)
A.aT8=s(["The teacher dissolves sugar in the warm water until it is completely saturated (no more will dissolve).","Dip the skewer in water and then in dry sugar so it gets a coating of seed crystals; let it dry for a moment.","Hang the skewer in the solution without touching the bottom, and leave it somewhere undisturbed.","Wait a few days: sugar crystals grow on the skewer."],t.s)
A.a_M=new B.J("Rock Sugar","Sugar crystals grow from a very strong sugar solution.",A.aM3,A.aT8,"Warm water can dissolve a lot of sugar. As the water slowly evaporates, the sugar that no longer fits sticks to the seed crystals and grows into big crystals (rock sugar) \u2014 the same idea as salt crystals, with a different material.",A.D,"The teacher prepares and pours the warm water and dissolves the sugar; children only hold the glass once the teacher has checked the solution is lukewarm, not hot. Do not eat crystals that have sat open for days (dust/ants). Check for food allergies or foods a child must avoid.",7200)
A.aTB=s([A.ZN,A.a_M],t.T)
A.b6D=new B.aD("Does not yet know where crystals come from.","Knows crystals appear after being left to stand, but does not yet link this to water evaporating.","Explains that crystals form when the water in a solution evaporates and the dissolved substance is left behind.")
A.b1g=new B.aC(u.fy,"How do gemstones and rock sugar form?","Opening (5 minutes): Show granulated sugar/salt up close (crystal grains). Ask the hook question.","Main activity (20 minutes): Explain saturated solutions and how crystals form as water evaporates. Start the experiment of hanging a thread in salt solution. Observe the crystals growing over the next few days.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: gemstone crystals in nature form over thousands to millions of years.","Pupils can explain that crystals form when the water in a solution evaporates and the dissolved substance is left behind.",A.b2,u.e,A.b6D)
A.blQ=new B.aw("menumbuhkan_kristal","Growing Crystals","\ud83d\udc8e",A.ao,A.ci,u.fy,"How do gemstones and rock sugar form?",30,'This experiment needs a few days of patience \u2014 make it "this week\'s project". Children love watching the crystals grow little by little.',A.aTM,A.aTB,A.b1g,null,!0,!1,"Ask the children to predict what happens to the size of the crystals if the evaporation goes on longer, then check.",'Dissolve salt until no more will dissolve (saturated), then leave it in the sun; show the crystals that appear as the water goes down. Say "the water leaves, the crystals line up".',u.L)
A.Oh=new B.f(A.Z,"Why is there a shadow?","Stand in the sun \u2014 there's a dark shadow. Why?",A.E,"Everyone, when we stand in the sunshine, a dark shape follows us along the ground \u2014 a shadow! Why do you think a shadow appears?",u.R,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.OD=new B.f(A.Y,"Light goes straight","Light travels straight until something blocks it.",A.v,"Look at your shadow on the floor. Who does it look like? If you wave your hand, does your shadow wave too? Where do you think the shadow comes from? Can you point to something else in the classroom that has a shadow?",null,"Draw the sun, straight lines (light), a person, and a dark shadow behind them.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bem=new B.m("sumber","\u2600\ufe0f","Light source","There must be a LIGHT SOURCE, like the sun, a lamp or a torch. No light, no shadow \u2014 in the dark everything is black.")
A.bab=new B.m("penghalang","\ud83e\uddcd","Blocking object","There must be an OBJECT that blocks the light, like our body, a tree or a hand. Objects that light can't pass through make the clearest shadows. That's why solar panels (boards that catch sunlight) are put up away from the shadows of trees.")
A.bau=new B.m("layar","\ud83e\uddf1","Somewhere to land","A shadow falls on a SURFACE behind the object: the ground, a wall or the floor. That's where we see its dark shape.")
A.aQX=s([A.bem,A.bab,A.bau],t.w)
A.Pv=new B.f(A.W,"What does a shadow need?","Tap each card to find out.",A.v,"Let's find out everything it takes to make a shadow!",null,"Write the 3 things a shadow needs on the blackboard.",A.aQX,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Mv=new B.f(A.X,"Play hand shadows!","Make animal shadows with your hands.",A.v,"Now we're going to be shadow puppet masters!","Use a torch (or light from a window) and a wall. Have the children shape hand shadows into a bird or a dog. Try making them bigger and smaller by moving the hand.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b_R=new B.D("A shadow is still there even in total darkness.","Without light, everything is equally dark \u2014 there is no shadow. A shadow actually needs light that is being blocked.")
A.b0m=new B.D("A shadow comes out of our body.","A shadow doesn't come out of the body \u2014 it's the area that gets NO light because the body is blocking it. Move out of the light and the shadow moves too.")
A.awl=s([A.b_R,A.b0m],t.I)
A.Oy=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Can there be a shadow in total darkness?",A.E,"Who wants to help Si Jabrik? Can there be a shadow in a room that's completely dark, with no light at all? Why?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.awl)
A.NH=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Your shadow changes length all day long!",A.a_,"Remember our question \u2014 why do we have a shadow during the day? Now you know the answer! Get ready for a surprise. Our shadow is SHORTEST when the sun is right above our heads, at midday, and LONGEST in the morning and late afternoon. Because the sun's position changes, the length of our shadow changes too. Long ago, people used this as a sundial! In Indonesia there is even a \"day without shadows\" twice a year, when the midday sun is right above our heads and our shadow almost disappears; Indonesia's weather agency (BMKG) announces the dates for each city. When that day comes where you live, stand in an open space right at midday and look down at your feet: where has your shadow gone?",u.pb,null,null,!1,null,"Light can carry messages: inside glass cables as thin as a hair (optical fibre), flashes of light carry phone calls and the internet at about 200,000 km a second, even along the seabed between Indonesia's islands.","Astronauts who landed on the Moon left special mirrors there. Scientists on Earth fire laser beams at those mirrors and time the reflection \u2014 about 2.5 seconds there and back \u2014 to measure the distance to the Moon very precisely.",null)
A.aUl=s([A.Oh,A.OD,A.Pv,A.Mv,A.Oy,A.NH],t.n)
A.aL6=s(["A torch (or sunlight from a window)","A wall or a white cloth","Your own hands"],t.s)
A.aN2=s(["Make the room fairly dark. Switch on the torch, facing the wall.","Put a hand between the torch and the wall.","Shape your hand into a bird, a dog or a rabbit \u2014 look at the shadow!","Move your hand closer to and further from the torch. The shadow grows and shrinks."],t.s)
A.a0F=new B.J("Hand Shadow Puppets","An object that blocks light makes a shadow.",A.aL6,A.aN2,"Light travels in straight lines. When a hand blocks the light, the area behind it gets no light \u2014 and a dark, hand-shaped shadow appears. The closer the hand is to the light source, the bigger the shadow.",A.D,"Do not shine the torch straight into a friend's eyes.",15)
A.aRI=s(["A torch","Test objects: clear plastic, thin paper, printer paper, card, a book"],t.s)
A.aPm=s(["In a fairly dark place, shine the torch at each object from one side.","Look from the other side: does the light pass through?","Group them: clear/transparent (all light through), cloudy/translucent (some light through), opaque (no light through).","Link to shadows: opaque objects make the sharpest shadows."],t.s)
A.YJ=new B.J("Does Light Pass Through?","Objects can be grouped: let light through, let some through, or block it.",A.aRI,A.aPm,"Clear objects (glass, clear plastic) let almost all the light through; cloudy objects (thin paper) let only some through; opaque objects (card, books) block the light. Objects that block light make the sharpest shadows.",A.D,u.l,15)
A.aOJ=s(["A small mirror","A torch or sunlight from a window"],t.s)
A.aNa=s(["Hold the mirror and shine the torch/sunlight onto it.","Aim the reflection at a wall or the ceiling.","Move the mirror \u2014 the spot of light moves too.","Try reflecting light from one mirror to another."],t.s)
A.a0H=new B.J("Bouncing Light","Light reflects when it hits a smooth, shiny surface (a mirror).",A.aOJ,A.aNa,"When light hits a smooth, shiny surface like a mirror, it reflects in a new direction and we can aim it. This is how mirrors, periscopes and rear-view mirrors work.",A.D,"Never reflect light (especially sunlight) into a friend's eyes.",10)
A.aOM=s([A.a0F,A.YJ,A.a0H],t.T)
A.ph=s(["Critical reasoning","Creativity"],t.s)
A.b6W=new B.aD("Cannot yet explain why a shadow appears, or thinks the shadow comes out of the object itself.","Knows a shadow needs light, but does not yet name a blocking object as a condition.","Names both conditions (light + a blocking object) AND gives one real example.")
A.b1u=new B.aC(u.ki,"Why do we have a shadow during the day?","Opening (5 minutes): Have the children look at their own shadows. Ask why a shadow appears and when it disappears.","Main activity (20 minutes): Show that light travels in straight lines and that, when an object blocks it, a shadow forms. Demonstrate hand shadow puppets. Discuss how a shadow changes size with its distance from the light source.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with a fact: our shadow is shortest at midday and grows longer towards the afternoon.","Pupils can explain what a shadow needs to form (light + a blocking object) and give one example.",A.ph,u.d,A.b6W)
A.bl4=new B.aw("cahaya_bayangan","Light & Shadows","\ud83d\udd26",A.ao,A.bv,u.ki,"Why do we have a shadow during the day?",30,"If it's sunny, take the children outside briefly to look at their own shadows. First-hand experience sticks far better than a picture.",A.aUl,A.aOM,A.b1u,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to guess and then test: how can you make a shadow bigger or smaller?","Use a torch + one object + a wall. Show: light + something in the way = a shadow. Switch off the light \u2192 the shadow disappears.",u.V)
A.N9=new B.f(A.Z,"What is sound?","When we talk, what is really happening?",A.E,'Everyone, every day we hear sounds \u2014 talking, a rooster crowing, music from the radio, motorbike horns. But what is really happening so that we can hear those sounds? Put your hand on your throat and say "aaah".',"Have the children hold their throats and feel the vibration as they make a sound.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Rk=new B.f(A.Y,"Sound from vibration","Object vibrates \u2192 air vibrates \u2192 ear hears.",A.v,'Put your fingers on your throat and hum "mmmm". What do you feel in your fingers? Now I\'ll pluck this stretched rubber band \u2014 watch closely. What do you think always happens to a thing when it makes a sound?',null,"Draw a vibrating object (wavy lines), with wave arrows going to a picture of an ear.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.beK=new B.m("getaran","\u3030\ufe0f","Vibration","VIBRATION: the source of every sound. Things that vibrate (strings, drums, vocal cords, the thin cone inside a radio speaker) make sound.")
A.bf4=new B.m("merambat","\ud83d\udca8","Travels","TRAVELS: sound needs something like air to carry it to our ears. Without air, no sound is heard.")
A.beP=new B.m("tinggi_rendah","\ud83c\udfb6","Pitch","PITCH: fast vibrations make high notes, slow vibrations make low notes.")
A.bdj=new B.m("berhenti","\ud83e\udd2b","Stops","STOPS: if the vibration is stopped (the string is held), the sound stops straight away too.")
A.a2v=s([A.beK,A.bf4,A.beP,A.bdj],t.w)
A.M_=new B.f(A.W,"The secrets of sound","Tap each card to find out.",A.v,"Let's explore the secrets of sound!",null,"Write the facts about sound on the blackboard.",A.a2v,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.PB=new B.f(A.X,"Make a rubber-band guitar!","Pluck the bands on a box \u2014 hear & see them vibrate.",A.v,"Now let's make our own musical instrument!","Stretch rubber bands across the open top of the box. Have the children pluck them, watch them vibrate, then touch them to stop the sound. Pull a band tighter and listen to the note go up.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b_u=new B.D("Sound only comes from hard objects.","Every sound comes from something vibrating, whatever it is \u2014 a rubber band, a drum, even our own vocal cords. Hold your throat while talking and you can feel it vibrate.")
A.b_8=new B.D("Sound can travel through empty space, like outer space.","Sound needs a medium (air, water, solid objects) to travel. In the emptiness of outer space, no sound can be heard.")
A.b0k=new B.D("No sound, however loud, can damage our ears.","Very loud sounds that go on for a long time \u2014 like standing next to the loudspeakers at a party or a roaring engine \u2014 can damage our ears for ever. Move away and cover your ears.")
A.a2n=s([A.b_u,A.b_8,A.b0k],t.I)
A.QN=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Where does every sound come from?",A.E,"Now it's your turn to explain it to Si Jabrik. Where does every sound come from? And what happens to the sound if we suddenly hold the vibrating rubber band?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.a2n)
A.Or=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","In outer space, there is no sound at all!",A.a_,"Now we've answered our question from the start: what reaches our ears is vibration. Did you know? Sound needs air to travel. In outer space there is no air \u2014 it's completely empty. So out there, even the biggest explosion would make no sound at all! Astronauts have to talk by radio to hear each other. Next time there's a thunderstorm, stay indoors and count the seconds from the lightning to the thunder: every 3 seconds means the lightning is about 1 kilometre away. Light arrives almost at once; sound is much slower!","A round of applause \u2014 and listen to the clapping travel through the air!",null,null,!1,null,"The angklung from West Java is made of bamboo tubes: long tubes make low notes and short tubes make high notes. In 2010, UNESCO listed the angklung as part of the world's intangible cultural heritage.",'Sound that vibrates more than 20,000 times a second (ultrasound) is too high for our ears. All over the world, doctors and midwives use it in ultrasound scanners to "see" a baby inside its mother\'s tummy, and boats use it to find shoals of fish.',null)
A.aSu=s([A.N9,A.Rk,A.M_,A.PB,A.QN,A.Or],t.n)
A.aQM=s(["An empty box/container (a cardboard box or an old jar)","Several rubber bands of different sizes"],t.s)
A.aLP=s(["Stretch the rubber bands across the open top of the box.","Pluck a rubber band \u2014 listen to the sound and watch it vibrate.","Touch the rubber band to stop the vibration \u2014 the sound stops too.","Pull the rubber band tighter, then pluck it: the note gets higher."],t.s)
A.a0e=new B.J("Rubber-Band Guitar","Sound comes from vibration; tightness changes how high or low the note is.",A.aQM,A.aLP,"Sound comes from things that vibrate. A plucked rubber band vibrates and makes the air vibrate, which reaches our ears as sound. When the vibration is stopped, the sound stops. A tighter rubber band vibrates faster and makes a higher note.",A.D,"Watch out for rubber bands snapping when pulled too tight.",10)
A.aTL=s(["Several identical glass bottles","Water","A spoon/pencil to tap with (and, optionally, blow across the top)"],t.s)
A.aN4=s(["Fill the bottles with water to different heights.","Tap each bottle with a spoon \u2014 listen to its note.","A bottle with more water gives a lower note when tapped.","Try putting the bottles in order so you can play a tune."],t.s)
A.Z6=new B.J("Musical Bottles","How high or low a sound is depends on the length of the column that vibrates.",A.aTL,A.aN4,"When tapped, the bottle + water vibrate. More water makes the vibration slower \u2192 a lower note; little water \u2192 faster vibration \u2192 a higher note. Sound really does come from vibration, and its pitch can be changed.",A.D,"Take care with glass bottles: tap GENTLY with a spoon so they do not break, with the teacher supervising. If a bottle cracks or breaks, the teacher clears it up.",15)
A.aRO=s(["A bowl + plastic/a cut balloon to cover its mouth + a rubber band","A little salt or rice","Something nearby to make a loud sound (a pan to bang)"],t.s)
A.aS3=s(["Stretch the plastic tightly over the mouth of the bowl and hold it in place with the rubber band.","Sprinkle a little salt on top of the plastic.","Bang the pan loudly NEAR the bowl (do not touch it).",'Observe: the grains of salt jump about, "dancing".'],t.s)
A.YC=new B.J("Dancing Salt","Sound waves are vibrations that can move light objects.",A.aRO,A.aS3,"Sound travels through the air as vibrations. The vibrations make the tight plastic vibrate, so the salt on top jumps about too. This makes invisible sound vibrations visible.",A.D,"Do not bang the pan too close to children's ears. If you use a cut balloon, throw away the leftover pieces straight away (they can be swallowed); watch for children with a latex allergy.",15)
A.aAw=s([A.a0e,A.Z6,A.YC],t.T)
A.b61=new B.aD("Does not yet know that sound comes from vibration.","Knows sound comes from vibration, but does not yet understand that sound needs a medium to travel.","Explains that sound comes from vibration AND needs a medium (air) to travel.")
A.b1s=new B.aC(u.pm,u.cz,"Opening (5 minutes): Have the children touch their throats while making a sound and feel the vibration. Ask the hook question.","Main activity (20 minutes): Show that sound comes from vibration (rubber band, ruler, vocal cords). Explain that sound travels through the air to the ear. Explore high and low notes with the rubber-band guitar.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: sound travels through air, so in outer space, where there is no air, no sound can be heard.","Pupils can explain that sound comes from vibration and needs a medium (air) to travel.",A.ph,u.d,A.b61)
A.blz=new B.aw("bunyi_getaran","Sound & Vibration","\ud83c\udfb5",A.ao,A.bv,u.pm,u.cz,30,"Touching their throats while making a sound lets children feel the vibration straight away. Link this to the Sources & Forms of Energy lesson (sound energy).",A.aSu,A.aAw,A.b1s,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to make a simple sound maker (rubber bands, or glasses with different amounts of water) and explain why the notes are different.",'Pluck a stretched rubber band; watch it vibrate and listen to the sound, then hold the band \u2014 the sound stops. Say "vibrations make sound".',u.V)
A.PG=new B.f(A.Z,"One press, and it's on!","Press the switch \u2014 the light comes on. How?",A.E,"Everyone, as soon as we press the switch, the light comes on. Press it again, and it goes off. So what's happening inside the wires that makes the light turn on and off?",u.n,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Pi=new B.f(A.Y,"Electricity needs a path","Electricity flows along a path that is fully connected.",A.v,"Look at this torch. Inside it there's a battery, some wires and a little bulb. If we take one wire off, what happens? What do you think \u2014 how does electricity travel from the battery to the bulb to make it light up?",null,"Draw a battery, wires forming a loop, and a bulb. Show a complete loop (light on) vs a broken one (light off).",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.beo=new B.m("baterai","\ud83d\udd0b","Battery","BATTERY: the source of electricity. It has a + end and a \u2212 end. This is where the electricity comes from to flow round the circuit. Torches, radios, phones and solar lamps all use batteries.")
A.bcm=new B.m("kabel","\ud83d\udd0c","Wire","WIRE: the road the electricity flows along. It's made of metal (a conductor) that electricity passes through easily.")
A.bel=new B.m("sakelar","\ud83d\udd1b","Switch","SWITCH: joins and breaks the path. Pressed = path connected (light on), released = path broken (light off).")
A.beW=new B.m("lampu","\ud83d\udca1","Bulb","BULB: turns electricity into light. It only lights up when the whole circuit is fully connected.")
A.aQ4=s([A.beo,A.bcm,A.bel,A.beW],t.w)
A.M3=new B.f(A.W,"Parts of a circuit","Tap each part to find out.",A.v,"Let's get to know the parts of an electric circuit!",null,"Label each part on the circuit drawing from before.",A.aQ4,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.QG=new B.f(A.X,"Light the bulb!","Connect a battery + bulb to make a circuit.",A.v,"Now let's try to light a bulb ourselves!",'If you have the materials: connect the battery, wires and small bulb into a closed circuit until it lights up. If not: make a "hand circuit" \u2014 the children hold hands in a ring; let go once = the flow is broken.',null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b_q=new B.D("Electricity still flows even if one wire is broken.","Electricity only flows in a closed circuit (a complete path with no breaks). One break makes the whole series circuit go out.")
A.aZQ=new B.D("A bulb lights up just by touching the battery, without a path.","Electricity needs a loop from the + end to the \u2013 end of the battery. Without a complete path, the bulb will not light up.")
A.b0V=new B.D("Electricity from a wall socket or the cables on a pole is as weak as a torch battery.","No! Electricity from a socket, the cables on a pole or a generator is far stronger and can hurt you. Our experiments use ONLY small batteries. Never go near a fallen power cable \u2014 tell an adult straight away.")
A.aVF=s([A.b_q,A.aZQ,A.b0V],t.I)
A.OP=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Why does the bulb go out when a wire is removed?",A.E,"Now it's your turn to explain to Si Jabrik. Why does the bulb go out when one of the wires is taken off? And what do we call an electric path that's fully connected?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aVF)
A.Qg=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Home lights are in parallel \u2014 one goes out, the rest stay on!",A.a_,"Remember our question from the start \u2014 why does the light come on when we press the switch? Now you know the answer! Listen carefully to this fact. The lights in our homes are wired in PARALLEL \u2014 each one has its own path. That's why when one light goes out, the others stay on. If they were wired in series, like a cheap string of small decorative lights, one broken bulb could make them all go out! Tonight, if there's a torch at home, slide or press its switch slowly: right at the \"click\", the circuit closes and the bulb lights up.",u.D,null,null,!1,null,"The solar street lamps in many villages and towns in Indonesia are clever circuits: in the daytime the solar panel charges a battery, then a light sensor closes the circuit when it gets dark, so the lamp switches itself on.","Electric eels in the rivers of the Amazon, a giant rainforest in South America, can make electric shocks of hundreds of volts \u2014 hundreds of times stronger than a torch battery. They use them to stun the fish they hunt.",null)
A.aN0=s([A.PG,A.Pi,A.M3,A.QG,A.OP,A.Qg],t.n)
A.al_=s(["1 battery (1.5 V) \u2014 size AA/D is best","1 small torch bulb \u2014 if you have none, take one out of an old torch or a broken toy that has a light","2 short pieces of thin wire/cable"],t.s)
A.aKQ=s(["Predict before trying: can the bulb light up if only ONE wire is connected?","Connect one wire from the + end of the battery to one contact on the bulb.","Connect the second wire from the \u2212 end of the battery to the bulb's other contact.","When both connections are complete (a closed circuit), the bulb LIGHTS UP.","Remove one of the wires \u2014 the bulb goes out (an open circuit).","Change just one thing and keep everything else the same \u2014 e.g. add one battery, same bulb & wires. Compare how bright it is."],t.s)
A.Zh=new B.J("A Simple Torch","Electricity flows around a closed circuit to light a bulb.",A.al_,A.aKQ,"Electricity only flows if the path is fully connected from the + end to the \u2212 end of the battery, through the bulb \u2014 this is called a closed circuit. If a connection is broken (an open circuit), the flow stops and the bulb goes out. That is how the light switches at home work.",A.D,"ONLY use small (AA/D) batteries that are not leaking \u2014 NEVER button/coin cells (dangerous if swallowed). Do not join the battery's + and \u2212 ends with a wire and no bulb (the wire gets hot). NEVER use mains electricity from a wall socket.",15)
A.aNP=s(["Battery + small bulb + wires (the circuit from the main lesson \u2014 if you have no bulb, use the bulb from an old torch)","Test objects: metal spoon, nail, rubber, plastic, wood, pencil"],t.s)
A.aTi=s(["Make a battery\u2013bulb circuit with one gap (two loose wire ends).","Predict before testing each object: conductor or not?","Touch the test object across both wire ends to bridge the gap.","Observe: bulb lights up = the object conducts electricity (conductor).","Record which objects light the bulb and which do not \u2014 keep the circuit the same and change only the test object (fair test)."],t.s)
A.Ze=new B.J("Conductor or Not?","Some materials conduct electricity (metals), some do not.",A.aNP,A.aTi,"Materials that conduct electricity (conductors), like metals, light the bulb; materials that do not (insulators), like plastic, rubber and wood, do not. That is why wires are made of metal but covered in plastic to keep them safe.",A.D,"ONLY use small batteries that are not leaking \u2014 NEVER button/coin cells (dangerous if swallowed). Do not join the battery's + and \u2212 ends with a wire and no bulb (the wire gets hot). NEVER test a mains wall socket.",15)
A.aLt=s(["Battery + small bulb + wires circuit (if you have no bulb, use the bulb from an old torch)","2 drawing pins + 1 paper clip + a piece of card as a base"],t.s)
A.aTH=s(["Push 2 drawing pins into the card close together; hook the paper clip onto one of them so it can swing round.","Connect the circuit wires to the two drawing pins.","Swing the clip until it touches the second pin \u2014 the bulb LIGHTS UP (switch closed).","Swing the clip away \u2014 the bulb GOES OUT (switch open).","Predict first: if the clip is replaced by a safety pin or a coin, will the bulb still light? Test them one at a time \u2014 keep the circuit the same and change only the connector."],t.s)
A.a_G=new B.J("Paper Clip Switch","A switch connects & breaks a circuit to turn a bulb on.",A.aLt,A.aTH,"A switch is a device for connecting and breaking the path of electricity. When the clip joins the two pins, the circuit is closed and the bulb lights; when it is moved away, the circuit is open and the bulb goes out \u2014 just like a light switch at home.",A.D,"Take care with the sharp points of the drawing pins. ONLY use small batteries that are not leaking \u2014 NEVER button/coin cells (dangerous if swallowed). Do not join the battery's + and \u2212 ends with a wire and no bulb (the wire gets hot). NEVER test the electricity from a mains wall socket.",15)
A.aTv=s([A.Zh,A.Ze,A.a_G],t.T)
A.b5S=new B.aD("Does not yet understand that a bulb needs a closed path (circuit) to light up.","Knows electricity needs a closed circuit, but cannot yet tell series and parallel apart.","Explains a closed circuit AND the difference between series and parallel circuits.")
A.b1d=new B.aC("Pupils can explain what electricity needs in order to flow (a closed circuit) and tell the difference between series and parallel circuits in simple terms.",u.m6,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Switch the classroom light on and off (if there is one) to spark curiosity.","Main activity (20 minutes): Explain closed vs open circuits and the roles of the battery and the switch. Introduce series circuits (one path) and parallel circuits (branching paths). Do the battery + bulb experiment if the materials are available.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: lights at home are wired in parallel, so if one goes out, the others stay on.","Pupils can explain that electricity needs a closed circuit and state the difference between series and parallel.",A.b2,u.d,A.b5S)
A.bli=new B.aw("listrik_rangkaian","Electricity & Circuits","\ud83d\udca1",A.ao,A.bv,"Pupils can explain what electricity needs in order to flow (a closed circuit) and tell the difference between series and parallel circuits.",u.m6,30,'The experiment needs a battery + a small bulb + wires. If you have none, act out a circuit with a chain of children holding hands: break one link and the "flow" stops.',A.aN0,A.aTv,A.b1d,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to draw a battery-wire-bulb circuit that lights up, then point to where cutting it would make the bulb go out.","Use the analogy of water flowing round a looped hose; if the hose is cut, the water stops. In the same way, electricity needs a complete path with no breaks.",u.z)
A.NB=new B.f(A.Z,"Why only iron?","A magnet sticks to the fridge, but falls off wood. Why?",A.E,"Everyone, a magnet can stick tightly to an iron door, a tin can or a fridge door, but it falls off if we put it on wood or plastic. Why is a magnet so picky about what it sticks to? Secretly, magnets are also hiding inside a radio's loudspeaker!",u.n,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Te=new B.f(A.Y,"Magnet power","Magnets attract iron & steel and have two poles.",A.v,"A magnet can pull things without touching them \u2014 like magic! But not everything wants to be pulled. Look around the classroom: which things do you think will stick, and which won't? What's different about them?",null,"Draw a bar magnet with ends marked N (north) and S (south). Show clips being pulled to its ends.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bdq=new B.m("menarik","\ud83e\uddf2","Attracts iron","ATTRACTS IRON: a magnet pulls things made of iron/steel like clips and nails, but not plastic, wood, paper \u2014 or aluminium, which most drink cans are made of.")
A.baV=new B.m("kutub","\ud83d\udd34","Two poles","TWO POLES: every magnet has a north (N) and a south (S) pole. The pull is strongest at the two ends, the poles.")
A.bbr=new B.m("tarik_tolak","\u2194\ufe0f","Pull & push","PULL & PUSH: different poles (N\u2013S) attract each other, the same poles (N\u2013N or S\u2013S) repel each other.")
A.bdr=new B.m("menembus","\ud83d\udcc4","Goes through","GOES THROUGH: a magnet can pull from a short distance and through thin paper or glass \u2014 it doesn't have to touch directly.")
A.aLM=s([A.bdq,A.baV,A.bbr,A.bdr],t.w)
A.MM=new B.f(A.W,"What magnets do","Tap each property to find out.",A.v,"Let's find out about the amazing things magnets can do!",null,"Write the properties of magnets on the blackboard.",A.aLM,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.PM=new B.f(A.X,"Test & make a compass!","Test objects, then make a needle compass.",A.v,"Now you're all magnet scientists!","Ask the children to test which objects the magnet attracts. If you have a needle & water: stroke the needle along the magnet in one direction about 20 times, float it on a leaf in water, and watch it point north-south. If the teacher's phone has a compass app, compare the directions (a phone has a tiny magnetic sensor inside); still keep magnets away from the phone.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.aZT=new B.D("Magnets attract all kinds of metal.","Magnets only attract iron, steel and nickel. Aluminium, copper and gold are not attracted. Have the children test different coins and spoons and, if you have any, old cans: scrap collectors use a magnet to sort steel cans from aluminium ones.")
A.b_o=new B.D("Magnets are just for toys and sticking things up.","Magnets work hard all around us. A bicycle lamp dynamo, the generators of a hydroelectric power station on a river, and wind turbines make electricity by spinning magnets near coils of wire. The other way round, electricity + magnets spin the motors in fans, water pumps and electric motorbikes.")
A.b0w=new B.D("Like poles attract each other.","Like poles (N\u2013N / S\u2013S) repel; unlike poles (N\u2013S) attract. Show it slowly with two magnets so the children can feel it.")
A.aSP=s([A.aZT,A.b_o,A.b0w],t.I)
A.Nm=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Two north poles brought together \u2014 pull or push?",A.E,"Si Jabrik isn't sure. Can you help him? If we bring two north poles of a magnet together, will they pull together or push apart? And what kinds of things can a magnet attract?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aSP)
A.SJ=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","The Earth is a giant magnet!",A.a_,"Remember our question from the start \u2014 why does a magnet stick to the fridge but not to wood? Now you know the answer! Did you know? Our EARTH itself is a giant magnet, with a north pole and a south pole. That's why a compass needle always points north \u2014 it's pulled by the Earth's magnetism. Sailors and explorers have used this to find their way for hundreds of years! Scientists have also found that sea turtles use the Earth's magnetism like a map, to cross the oceans and then return to near the beach where they hatched. But how exactly does a turtle \"feel\" magnetism? Scientists are still finding out \u2014 maybe one day you'll be the one to solve it!","A round of applause for the Earth, the giant magnet!",null,null,!1,null,"The black sand on many Indonesian beaches, for example on the south coast of Java, contains grains of iron from volcanoes. Hold a magnet near it and the grains jump up and stick \u2014 this iron sand is even mined to make steel.","A maglev train in Japan, which floats above its track because magnets push it up, reached 603 km/h in a test run. In hospitals, MRI scanners use a magnet tens of thousands of times stronger than the Earth's to take pictures inside the body without an operation.",null)
A.a2o=s([A.NB,A.Te,A.MM,A.PM,A.Nm,A.SJ],t.n)
A.aTq=s(["1 magnet (a fridge magnet/toy magnet)","Some paper clips, small nails, plastic & paper objects","For the compass: a sewing needle, a magnet, a leaf/piece of cork, a bowl of water"],t.s)
A.aM5=s(["Predict before trying: which objects will the magnet attract, and which won't it?","Bring the magnet close to different objects. Note which ones are pulled.","Clips & nails (iron) are pulled; plastic & paper are not. If you have old cans, test them too: sardine/milk cans (steel) are pulled, most drink cans (aluminium) are not \u2014 mind the sharp edges.","Stroke the needle along the magnet in one direction about 20 times so it becomes a magnet.","Lay the needle on a floating leaf/piece of cork \u2014 it turns to point north-south.","\ud83d\udca1 If the needle sinks or won't turn: use a wide, light dry leaf/piece of cork, and stroke the needle longer (30\u201340 times, still in one direction) so it is magnetised strongly enough."],t.s)
A.a0z=new B.J("Magnets & a Needle Compass","Magnets attract iron things and always point north-south.",A.aTq,A.aM5,"Magnets only attract things made of iron/steel, not plastic or paper. A magnet has two poles (north & south); like poles repel, unlike poles attract. The Earth itself is like a giant magnet, so a magnetised needle always points north \u2014 that is how a compass works.",A.D,"The teacher hands out the needles & nails, then collects and counts them again; be careful with the points. Small magnets must not go in the mouth. Keep magnets away from phones & bank (ATM) cards.",15)
A.aTt=s(["1 magnet","1 iron nail or needle","A few paper clips/small safety pins"],t.s)
A.aOK=s(["Try first: hold an ordinary nail near the clips \u2014 it does not attract them yet.","Stroke the nail with one pole of the magnet in the SAME DIRECTION about 30 times (not back and forth).","Hold the nail near the clips \u2014 now the nail picks up the clips!","Leave it / drop it a few times: its magnetism gets weaker again.","Predict first: does 10 strokes or 30 strokes make a stronger magnet? Change only the number of strokes; keep the nail & magnet the same (fair test)."],t.s)
A.a_m=new B.J("Make Your Own Magnet","Iron can be made into a temporary magnet by stroking it in one direction.",A.aTt,A.aOK,"Stroking in one direction lines up the tiny parts inside the iron so they face the same way, so the nail becomes a temporary magnet. If it is shaken or heated, they get jumbled up again and the magnetism is lost.",A.D,"The teacher hands out the nails/needles, then collects them again; take care with the points. Small magnets must not go in the mouth. Keep magnets away from phones & bank (ATM) cards.",15)
A.aRd=s(["A magnet","Paper clips","A sheet of paper, a plastic cup, or thin glass"],t.s)
A.aQW=s(["Put a paper clip on top of a sheet of paper.","Move the magnet UNDER the paper.","Observe: the clip moves too, even though nothing touches it directly.","Also try it through the side of a plastic cup with clips inside.","Predict first: can the magnet still pull through 3 sheets of paper? Change just one thing \u2014 how thick the barrier is \u2014 and keep the magnet & clips the same (fair test)."],t.s)
A.a_j=new B.J("Magnets Work Through Things","Magnetic force can pass through thin non-metal materials.",A.aRd,A.aQW,"The pull of a magnet can pass through thin materials that are not metal, such as paper, plastic and glass. That is why a magnet can move iron objects from behind a thin barrier.",A.D,"Keep magnets away from phones and bank (ATM) cards.",10)
A.aLX=s([A.a0z,A.a_m,A.a_j],t.T)
A.b6Z=new B.aD("Cannot yet predict which objects a magnet attracts, or thinks all metals are attracted.","Knows a magnet attracts certain objects, but does not yet understand the rule of poles.","Names objects a magnet attracts AND explains like poles repel, unlike poles attract.")
A.b1Y=new B.aC("Pupils can explain the properties of magnets (they attract iron objects, have two poles, attract and repel) and one way they are used.",u.nK,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Stick a magnet onto different objects in the classroom to spark curiosity.","Main activity (20 minutes): Test which objects a magnet attracts. Introduce the two poles and attraction and repulsion. Show that a magnet attracts from a distance/through paper. Make a simple needle compass if possible.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: the Earth is a giant magnet, which is why a compass always points north.","Pupils can name objects a magnet attracts and explain that like poles repel and unlike poles attract.",A.b2,u.d,A.b6Z)
A.bl0=new B.aw("magnet","Magnets","\ud83e\uddf2",A.ao,A.bv,"Pupils can explain the properties of magnets (they attract iron, have two poles, attract and repel) and one way they are used.",u.nK,30,"A fridge magnet and a few metal/plastic objects are enough for an exciting experiment. Let the children predict first before testing each object.",A.a2o,A.aLX,A.b1Y,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to test 6 objects and sort them into attracted by the magnet vs not, then guess why from what each object is made of.",'Bring two magnets together slowly; feel when they push apart and when they pull together. Say together: "same pushes, different pulls".',u.z)
A.Nz=new B.f(A.Z,"Where does electricity come from?","The lamp is on \u2014 but where does its electricity come from?",A.E,"Everyone, look at the lights in our classroom. They're on because there's electricity. But where does that electricity come from? And could it ever run out?","Ask the children to name electrical things they use at home.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.LM=new B.f(A.Y,"Electricity's journey home","Power station \u2192 cables \u2192 our home.",A.v,"The electricity in our homes is made in a place called a power station. Then it travels along lots of cables all the way to our houses. Most power stations in Indonesia still burn coal or oil. What do you think will happen if the coal and oil run out?",null,"Simple drawing: power station (chimney) \u2192 cable line \u2192 house with a lamp switched on.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bb4=new B.m("matahari","\u2600\ufe0f","Sun","SUN: solar panels can turn sunlight into electricity. Indonesia gets sunshine all year round \u2014 many street lamps and villages already run on solar power.")
A.bdo=new B.m("angin","\ud83d\udca8","Wind","WIND: big windmills (turbines) spin in the wind and make electricity, especially near the coast. At Sidrap, in South Sulawesi, 30 giant wind turbines on towers about 80 metres tall started spinning side by side in 2018.")
A.bdc=new B.m("air","\ud83d\udca7","Water","WATER: fast-flowing water in rivers or dams can turn giant wheels (in hydroelectric power stations) and make electricity.")
A.ber=new B.m("panas_bumi","\ud83c\udf0b","Geothermal","GEOTHERMAL: Indonesia has lots of volcanoes. Heat from deep inside the Earth can be used to make electricity.")
A.aMt=s([A.bb4,A.bdo,A.bdc,A.ber],t.w)
A.O3=new B.f(A.W,"Alternative energy around us","Tap each energy source \u2014 find out more.",A.v,"Luckily, there are lots of other energy sources all around us. Let's find out!",null,'Write the heading "Alternative Energy" and stick up a picture of each source.',A.aMt,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.MY=new B.f(A.X,"Collect the sun's energy!","Put it in a foil-lined box in the sun \u2014 feel the heat.",A.v,"Now let's try collecting energy from the sun ourselves, just like a solar panel!","Make a Mini Solar Oven: line a cardboard box with aluminium foil, put a piece of chocolate/cheese/butter inside, cover it with clear plastic, then leave it in strong sunlight for 20\u201330 minutes. Compare it with a piece left in the shade.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.aZl=new B.D("Electricity will never run out, so there's no need to save it.","Most of our electricity comes from coal and oil, which are limited and can run out. Saving electricity helps those fuels last longer for everyone.")
A.aZX=new B.D("Alternative energy like sun and wind only exists in other countries.","In fact, Indonesia has lots of alternative energy sources: sunshine all year round, wind on the coast, rivers for hydroelectric power stations, and volcanoes for geothermal energy.")
A.b03=new B.D("Saving energy just means switching off electric lights.","Firewood, kerosene, gas and petrol are energy sources too. Putting a lid on the pan so food cooks faster, and switching off a motorbike engine while waiting, also save energy.")
A.aV6=s([A.aZl,A.aZX,A.b03],t.I)
A.Tk=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","What is one thing you can do to save energy at school?",A.E,"Now it's your turn to explain to Si Jabrik. Can you suggest one thing you could do at school to save energy? And tell us why it would help.",u.a,null,null,!0,"Ask the children to explain the REASON behind the energy-saving action they suggest.",null,null,A.aV6)
A.Tx=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Solar panels can now power a whole school!",A.a_,"Remember our question at the start? Now you know: electricity comes from power stations, and most of them burn fuel that can run out. That's why we need to save energy and find other kinds of energy. Did you know? Today, solar panels on a roof can power a whole school, with no cables from a faraway power station! But there's a big puzzle: how can we store solar power for the night, and wind power for when the wind stops, cheaply and in huge amounts? Scientists all over the world are still working on it. Maybe one day one of you will solve it!",'Ask the children to clap while "acting out" the sun shining with their hands.',null,null,!1,null,"Solar panels can even float: on the Cirata Reservoir in West Java, hundreds of thousands of solar panels float on the water of a human-made lake and turn sunlight into electricity.","The Three Gorges Dam on the Yangtze River in China is one of the biggest power stations in the world: the river's flow turns dozens of giant turbines.",null)
A.aTS=s([A.Nz,A.LM,A.O3,A.MY,A.Tk,A.Tx],t.n)
A.aUd=s(["An old cardboard box or shoebox","Aluminium foil, or the silver paper from used food wrappers","Clear plastic (for the cover)","A small piece of chocolate, cheese, or butter"],t.s)
A.aKn=s(["Line the inside of the box with aluminium foil or silver paper, sticking it down as neatly as possible so it reflects light.","Put a small piece of chocolate, cheese, or butter in the middle of the box.","Cover the top of the box with clear plastic so the heat is trapped inside.","Leave the box in strong sunlight for 20\u201330 minutes, with the open side facing the sun.","Compare it with an identical piece left in the shade for the same amount of time."],t.s)
A.a_E=new B.J("Mini Solar Oven","The sun is a source of heat energy that can be collected.",A.aUd,A.aKn,"The aluminium foil reflects sunlight towards one spot, and the clear plastic traps the heat inside the box (like a small greenhouse). The chocolate/cheese/butter in the box in the sun melts faster than the piece left in the shade, because the box collects heat energy from the sun. This is the basic idea behind real solar ovens and solar panels: collecting energy from the sun.",A.D,"Do not look directly at light reflected off the aluminium foil. The food is just for watching; if anyone will taste it, check for food allergies first (e.g. milk, chocolate).",30)
A.aKw=s(["A square of coloured paper","Scissors","A straw","Sticky tape","Thread and a small paper clip (as a weight)"],t.s)
A.aNt=s(["The teacher cuts the square of paper from each corner towards the middle, but not all the way through (leave a little uncut in the middle).","Fold every other point in to the centre of the paper (one from each corner) to make a pinwheel, then tape it to one end of the straw.","Tie the thread, with the small paper clip as a weight, to the other end of the straw.","Blow on the windmill or take it somewhere windy, then watch: the windmill spins and lifts the paper clip weight."],t.s)
A.Zf=new B.J("Paper Windmill","Wind can turn a windmill and give it power (like a wind turbine).",A.aKw,A.aNt,"When wind (or your breath) hits the slanted paper blades, it pushes each blade so the windmill spins. This spinning has power \u2014 enough to lift a light weight like a paper clip. Real wind turbines work in a similar way: the wind turns huge blades, and their spinning is turned into electricity.",A.D,"Scissors are for the teacher only \u2014 the teacher cuts the paper. The children just fold, tape, blow and observe.",20)
A.aDX=s([A.a_E,A.Zf],t.T)
A.b6t=new B.aD("Cannot yet name an alternative energy source or an energy-saving action.","Names 1 alternative energy source or 1 energy-saving action, but does not yet explain why.","Names at least 2 alternative energy sources AND suggests 1 energy-saving action with a reason.")
A.b2e=new B.aC(u.iU,u.a9,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the children to name electrical appliances in their homes. Ask the hook question: where does that electricity come from?","Main activity (20 minutes): Explain that most electricity comes from power stations that burn coal or oil \u2014 fuels that can run out, which can lead to an energy crisis (expensive electricity or power cuts). Introduce the alternative energy sources found around Indonesia: sun, wind, water, geothermal heat, and biogas from livestock manure. Do the Mini Solar Oven experiment so the children feel the sun's energy first-hand.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers \u2014 ask the children to suggest one energy-saving action at school. Finish with a fact: solar panels on the roof can now power a school.","Pupils can name at least 2 alternative energy sources AND suggest 1 energy-saving action with a reason.",A.zK,u.d,A.b6t)
A.bls=new B.aw("hemat_energi_alternatif","Saving Energy & Alternative Energy","\u2600\ufe0f",A.ao,A.bv,u.iU,u.a9,30,"Talk about the energy crisis calmly, without frightening the children and without touching on politics \u2014 just the facts: fuel can run out, so we need to save energy and find alternatives. The Mini Solar Oven experiment makes the sun's energy feel real.",A.aTS,A.aDX,A.b2e,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to list the electrical appliances at home that are often left on when nobody is using them, then work out how much could be saved in a day.",'Point to the lights in the classroom: "The electricity that lights these comes from a power station far away \u2014 and most of its fuel can run out. That\'s why we need to save energy."',u.c)
A.OR=new B.f(A.Z,"Why a plastic handle?","The pan is hot, but its plastic handle stays cool. Why?",A.E,"Everyone, when we cook, the pan gets really hot. But we can still hold its plastic handle. Why doesn't the handle get hot too?",u.R,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Mf=new B.f(A.Y,"Heat can move","Heat flows from hot things to cold things.",A.v,"Imagine this: you put a spoon in hot tea, and after a while its handle gets hot too. Where did that heat go? Near a campfire, your hands feel warm even though they aren't touching the fire. What do you think \u2014 how many ways can heat travel?",null,"Make 3 empty columns. Draw a spoon in a hot glass, a pot of boiling water, and the sun. Fill in each column's name after the children discover it.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bbo=new B.m("konduksi","\ud83e\udd44","Conduction","CONDUCTION: heat travels through solid things that are touching. Example: a metal spoon gets hot when dipped in hot water.")
A.bdv=new B.m("konveksi","\u2668\ufe0f","Convection","CONVECTION: heat moves through flowing liquids or gases. Example: boiling water swirls around, hot air and smoke from a cooking fire rise.")
A.bdU=new B.m("radiasi","\u2600\ufe0f","Radiation","RADIATION: heat moves with nothing in between, by giving off rays. Example: you feel the heat of the sun or a campfire from far away.")
A.bah=new B.m("isolator","\ud83e\udde4","Heat blockers","INSULATORS: materials heat can't easily pass through, like plastic, wood and cloth. Used for pan handles and oven gloves.")
A.aWs=s([A.bbo,A.bdv,A.bdU,A.bah],t.w)
A.Q1=new B.f(A.W,"Three ways heat moves","Tap each way to find out.",A.v,"Let's find out the three ways heat moves!",null,"Write an example of each way in its column.",A.aWs,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.O6=new B.f(A.X,"Test the spoons!","Metal vs plastic spoon in warm water.",A.v,"Now let's test which material heats up fast!","The teacher pours the warm water. Dip the metal and plastic spoons in at the same time. Ask the children to hold the top ends and feel which one warms up first (the metal one).",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b16=new B.D("Heat from the sun reaches Earth through flowing hot air.","Outer space is empty, with no air. The Sun's heat arrives by giving off rays (radiation), which can cross empty space without needing air or any other material to travel through.")
A.b0h=new B.D("Metal and plastic both carry heat equally well.","Metal is a good heat conductor (it gets hot quickly); plastic and wood are heat blockers (insulators). That's why pan handles are made of plastic.")
A.b0a=new B.D("Thick jackets and blankets make heat.","Jackets and blankets don't make heat. They are insulators that keep our body heat from escaping quickly \u2014 that's why we stay warm on a cold night.")
A.aTF=s([A.b16,A.b0h,A.b0a],t.I)
A.NW=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","How does heat from the sun reach Earth?",A.E,"Can anyone explain this for Si Jabrik? How does heat from the sun reach Earth? And why does a metal spoon get hot quickly, but a plastic handle doesn't?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aTF)
A.Tn=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","The sun's heat crosses empty space with no air!",A.a_,"Remember our question \u2014 why are pan handles made of plastic or wood? Now you know the answer! Did you know? Between the Sun and Earth there is empty space with no air. Conduction and convection need something to travel through, so they can't get across. But RADIATION can cross empty space \u2014 that's why the sun's heat still reaches us from 150 million kilometres away! Try this tonight: touch a metal spoon and a wooden or plastic spoon that have been lying in the kitchen for a long time. The metal feels colder, but they are the same temperature! Metal carries heat away from your finger quickly \u2014 even our hands can be fooled.",u.D,null,null,!1,null,"In Papua there is a traditional way of cooking with hot stones: the stones are heated in a fire, then piled up with sweet potatoes, vegetables and meat, and covered with leaves. Heat moves from the stones into the food until it is cooked, and the leaf cover stops the heat escaping quickly.","The Parker Solar Probe, the spacecraft that has flown closest to the Sun, shelters behind a heat shield about 11 cm thick. Its front can reach about 1,400 \xb0C, but the instruments behind it stay at about 30 \xb0C \u2014 as warm as a classroom at midday.",null)
A.aUo=s([A.OR,A.Mf,A.Q1,A.O6,A.NW,A.Tn],t.n)
A.aSd=s(["1 metal spoon and 1 plastic spoon","A glass of warm/hot water (POURED BY THE TEACHER)","Hands to feel with"],t.s)
A.aVf=s(["The teacher pours warm water into the glass (not too hot).","Dip the metal spoon and the plastic spoon in at the same time, holding the top ends.","Wait one minute, then feel the ends you are holding.","The METAL spoon's end feels warm before the plastic one does."],t.s)
A.a0P=new B.J("Metal Spoon vs Plastic Spoon","Heat travels (conduction) faster through metal than through plastic.",A.aSd,A.aVf,"Heat travels from the water up to the end of the spoon through the material itself \u2014 this is called conduction. Metal is a good conductor of heat, so it feels warm quickly. Plastic is a poor conductor (an insulator), so it stays cool for longer. That's why pan handles are often made of plastic/wood.",A.ct,"Use WARM water, not boiling. The teacher does the pouring. Make sure no child gets splashed.",10)
A.aS6=s(["A large clear container of cold water","A small bottle of hot water + colouring (food colouring OR strong turmeric water/tea) \u2014 PREPARED BY THE TEACHER","String to lower the small bottle"],t.s)
A.aAu=s(["Fill the large container with ordinary cold water.","The teacher prepares the small bottle of hot coloured water.","Slowly lower the hot coloured bottle to the bottom of the cold water container.","Observe: the coloured (hot) water RISES to the top, then spreads out."],t.s)
A.a0T=new B.J("Coloured Water Convection","Hot water rises, cold water sinks (heat transfer by convection).",A.aS6,A.aAu,"Hot water is lighter (more spread out) so it RISES; cold water is heavier so it sinks. Heat moving through a flowing liquid or gas like this is called convection \u2014 just like hot air rising and sea breezes forming.",A.ct,"The teacher prepares & pours the hot water. Keep children away from the hot water.",15)
A.aPV=s(["2 glasses/bottles with the same amount of water","BLACK and WHITE cloth/paper for wrapping","A sunny spot"],t.s)
A.aLB=s(["Wrap one glass in black cloth and the other in white cloth.","Put both side by side in the sun for about 30\u201360 minutes.","Feel (or measure) the temperature of the water in both.","The water in the black wrapping feels warmer."],t.s)
A.a08=new B.J("Dark Colours Get Hotter","Dark colours absorb more of the sun's radiant heat.",A.aPV,A.aLB,"Dark colours absorb more of the sun's heat energy (radiation), while light colours reflect it. That is why black clothes feel hotter in the daytime, and light-coloured clothes feel cooler.",A.D,"Use ordinary water; do not look straight at the sun.",60)
A.awj=s([A.a0P,A.a0T,A.a08],t.T)
A.b6c=new B.aD("Cannot yet name a way that heat moves.","Names one or two ways heat moves, but not yet with suitable examples.","Names the three ways (conduction, convection, radiation) AND one example of each.")
A.b29=new B.aC(u.f7,u.bX,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Link it to the experience of holding a spoon in a glass of hot tea.","Main activity (20 minutes): Introduce conduction (through solids), convection (through flowing liquids/gases) and radiation (with nothing in between, like heat from the sun). Do the metal vs plastic spoon experiment.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: heat from the sun reaches Earth by radiation, crossing empty space where there is no air.","Pupils can name the three ways heat moves and give one example of each.",A.b2,u.e,A.b6c)
A.bmi=new B.aw("perpindahan_panas","Heat Transfer","\ud83d\udd25",A.ao,A.bv,u.f7,u.bX,30,"Link it to the kitchen: pans, a spoon in hot tea, a campfire. The children have already felt all three ways without knowing their names.",A.aUo,A.awj,A.b29,null,!0,!1,"Ask the children to sort things in the classroom into heat conductors (metal) and heat blockers (plastic, wood, cloth), and give their reasons.",'Dip a metal spoon and a plastic spoon in warm (safe) water \u2014 feel the metal warm up faster. Say "metal carries heat, plastic blocks it".',null)
A.Ou=new B.f(A.Z,"Why is it easy now?","A stuck tin lid \u2014 use a spoon and it pops right open.",A.E,"Everyone, some tin lids are really hard to open with your fingers. But prise one with a spoon, and pop \u2014 it opens easily! Why does it suddenly feel so much easier?",u.R,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Ti=new B.f(A.Y,"Tools that lighten the load","Simple machines make work easier.",A.v,"Try opening a tin lid with just your fingers \u2014 hard, isn't it? Now imagine using a spoon. Why is it so much easier, when our strength is just the same? Have you ever seen a mechanic undo a tight bolt on a motorbike wheel with a long spanner? The spanner makes the work feel lighter too. Look around the classroom: what other tools make heavy work feel light?",null,"Draw 3 examples: a seesaw (lever), an uphill road (inclined plane), a well pulley (pulley).",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bbg=new B.m("tuas","\u2696\ufe0f","Lever","LEVER: a bar that turns on a pivot point. Examples: a seesaw, scissors, a bottle opener, a spoon prising open a lid.")
A.beQ=new B.m("bidang_miring","\ud83d\udcd0","Inclined plane","INCLINED PLANE: a gentle slope that makes it easier to raise things. Examples: stairs, an uphill road, and the wheelchair ramp in front of a health centre or school.")
A.bcl=new B.m("katrol","\ud83d\udd18","Pulley","PULLEY: a grooved wheel with a rope for lifting loads. Examples: a well bucket, a flagpole, a crane.")
A.bep=new B.m("roda","\ud83d\udede","Wheel & axle","WHEEL & AXLE: a wheel turning on an axle makes it easier to move things. Examples: a cart, a bicycle, a steering wheel.")
A.aO3=s([A.bbg,A.beQ,A.bcl,A.bep],t.w)
A.OY=new B.f(A.W,"Types of simple machine","Tap each type to find out.",A.v,"Let's find out the different types of simple machine!",null,"Write examples of each type on the blackboard.",A.aO3,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.T2=new B.f(A.X,"Make a lever!","Lift a load with a spoon + pencil.",A.v,"Now let's make our own lever!","Put a pencil down as the pivot, with the spoon lying across it. Put an eraser on the short end and press down on the long end. Ask the children to feel how easily the load lifts.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.aZK=new B.D("Simple machines mean we don't need any effort at all.","You still need effort, but it feels lighter and easier. Simple machines save effort; they don't create free energy.")
A.b0e=new B.D("A winding uphill road that goes further is pointless.","A gentler, longer slope makes climbing feel lighter, even though the distance you travel gets longer.")
A.aO5=s([A.aZK,A.b0e],t.I)
A.Mb=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","What kind of simple machine are stairs?",A.E,"Si Jabrik is confused \u2014 help him out! What kind of simple machine are stairs or an uphill road? And which kind are scissors?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aO5)
A.NJ=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","The pyramids were built with ramps, without engines!",A.a_,"Remember our question at the start? Now you know: the spoon works as a lever. This fact might surprise you. Thousands of years ago, the people of Egypt built giant pyramids from stones weighing several tonnes \u2014 without engines or modern cranes. They used inclined planes (gentle ramps) and levers to move the giant stones. Even today, experts still argue about exactly what those ramps looked like. Simple machines really are great helpers!","A round of applause for clever human thinking! Challenge at home: push a door shut, once near the hinges and once at the edge by the handle. Which feels easier? Keep your fingers away from the gap. Share it tomorrow.",null,null,!1,null,"On road and river projects, excavators \u2014 digging machines with a long arm \u2014 lift tonnes of soil with an arm made of giant levers pushed by hydraulics (oil under high pressure). In Indonesia, both women and men work as operators of heavy machines like this \u2014 maybe you will too one day!","In Scotland, the Falkirk Wheel is a giant wheel that lifts boats 24 metres up and down between two canals (human-made rivers). Because its two sides balance like a seesaw, turning it takes only a little electricity.",null)
A.aQ7=s([A.Ou,A.Ti,A.OY,A.T2,A.Mb,A.NJ],t.n)
A.aNq=s(["1 spoon or strong ruler","1 pencil (as the fulcrum)","A small, fairly heavy object (an eraser/small stone)"],t.s)
A.aSr=s(["Put the pencil on the table as the fulcrum (pivot point).","Lay the spoon across the pencil like a seesaw.","Put the load on the short end and press down on the long end.","Notice: the load lifts with less effort!"],t.s)
A.a0b=new B.J("A Spoon Lever","A lever makes lifting a load easier using a fulcrum (pivot point).",A.aNq,A.aSr,"A lever is a simple machine: a bar that turns on a fulcrum (pivot point). By putting the load close to the fulcrum and pressing from the far end, we can lift a heavy load with less force. Simple machines do not reduce the work, but they make it feel lighter.",A.D,null,10)
A.aUA=s(["Strong thread/string","An empty cotton reel, or a smooth hook/rail, as the wheel","A small load (a bag with things in it) + a high post/rail"],t.s)
A.a2c=s(['Hang the "wheel" (pulley) from a high, sturdy place.',"Pass the string over the wheel; tie the load to one end.","Pull the other end of the string DOWN to lift the load up.","Feel it: pulling down is more comfortable than lifting straight up."],t.s)
A.YS=new B.J("Simple Pulley","A pulley changes the direction of the pull, so lifting feels easier.",A.aUA,A.a2c,"A pulley is a simple machine: a wheel with a groove. It changes the direction of the force: we pull the string down and the load goes up. That is why well buckets and flagpoles use pulleys.",A.D,"Make sure the hanging point is sturdy; keep feet clear in case the load falls.",20)
A.aVe=s(["A board/thick book as a ramp","A heavy object (a bottle of water) + a rubber band/string"],t.s)
A.aMR=s(["Lift the object straight up onto the table \u2014 feel how heavy it is.","Lean the board against the table to make a ramp.","Predict first: is it easier to lift it straight up or to take it up the ramp?","Pull/push the object up the ramp.","Compare: going up the ramp feels easier.","Change just one thing: the slope of the board (gentle vs steep); keep the object & board the same \u2014 feel the difference (fair test)."],t.s)
A.Zn=new B.J("Ramp (Inclined Plane)","An inclined plane reduces the effort needed to raise an object.",A.aVe,A.aMR,"An inclined plane lets us raise an object with less effort, although the distance becomes longer. That is why mountain roads zigzag and ramps are used to push goods up into lorries.",A.D,"Make sure the board is steady; keep feet clear of objects sliding down.",15)
A.aR5=s([A.a0b,A.YS,A.Zn],t.T)
A.b73=new B.aD("Cannot yet name a type of simple machine, or thinks all such tools are engines.","Names types of simple machine, but not yet with examples of how they are used.","Names 2 types of simple machine AND one example of how each is used.")
A.b2s=new B.aC("Pupils can name types of simple machines (lever, inclined plane, pulley) and explain how they make work easier.",u.jz,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Show how heavy it is to lift something directly with your hands.","Main activity (20 minutes): Introduce the lever, the inclined plane and the pulley with everyday examples (scissors, a seesaw, an uphill road, a well bucket on a rope). Do the spoon lever experiment.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: giant pyramids were built thousands of years ago using inclined planes, without engines.","Pupils can name at least 2 types of simple machine and one example of how each is used.",A.ph,u.e,A.b73)
A.bkY=new B.aw("pesawat_sederhana","Simple Machines","\ud83d\udee0\ufe0f",A.ao,A.bv,"Pupils can name types of simple machines and explain how they make work easier.",u.jz,30,"The classroom is full of simple machines: scissors, screws, door handles, stairs. Point to the real thing before you name it.",A.aQ7,A.aR5,A.b2s,null,!0,!1,"Ask the children to find 4 simple machines around the school and name their type (lever, inclined plane, pulley, wheel).",'Compare lifting a book straight up with pushing it up a sloping board \u2014 it feels lighter. Say "a slope makes the work lighter".',null)
A.QD=new B.f(A.Z,"Why does hair stand up?","Take off a hat/shirt and hair sometimes stands up. Why?",A.E,'Everyone, has your hair ever suddenly stood up when you took off a shirt or a hat? Or have you felt a tiny "zap" when you touched a door handle? Why do you think that happens?',"Let the children share their experiences. There are no wrong answers yet.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Rz=new B.f(A.Y,"Electricity from rubbing","Rubbing things together creates static electricity.",A.v,"Has anyone felt their hair stand up when taking off a shirt or a hat? Try rubbing a balloon on your hair, then hold it near some tiny pieces of paper. What do you think \u2014 why can the balloon suddenly pull the paper without even touching it?",null,"Draw a balloon being rubbed on hair, then the balloon pulling tiny pieces of paper (arrows showing the pull).",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bcA=new B.m("gosokan","\ud83c\udf88","Rubbing","RUBBING: rubbing a balloon on hair gives it a static electric charge. Without rubbing, it's just an ordinary balloon.")
A.ba2=new B.m("menarik","\ud83e\uddf2","Attracting","ATTRACTING: a charged object pulls light things like paper and hair, a bit like a magnet but for a different reason.")
A.be9=new B.m("sementara","\u23f3","Short-lived","SHORT-LIVED: static charge soon fades, especially in humid air. That's why this experiment often fails on rainy days.")
A.beA=new B.m("petir","\ud83c\udf29\ufe0f","Lightning","LIGHTNING: an example of giant static electricity. Charge builds up in the clouds, then jumps as a flash. Lightning nearby? Go inside a house or building.")
A.aLT=s([A.bcA,A.ba2,A.be9,A.beA],t.w)
A.Oz=new B.f(A.W,"Secrets of static electricity","Tap each card to find out.",A.v,"Let's explore the secrets of static electricity!",null,"Write the static electricity facts on the blackboard.",A.aLT,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.O7=new B.f(A.X,"Magic balloon!","Rub a balloon on hair. Pull up tiny bits of paper.",A.v,"Now let's make a magic pulling balloon!","Rub a balloon on dry hair for about 10 seconds, then hold it near tiny pieces of paper \u2014 the paper jumps up and sticks! Also try holding it near a friend's hair and watch the hair stand up.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.aZP=new B.D("The balloon pulls the paper because it is sticky or has glue on it.","It's not glue \u2014 rubbing the balloon gives it a static electric charge, and that charge is what pulls the paper. Nothing is sticky.")
A.b_w=new B.D("Static electricity is exactly the same as electricity from a wall socket.","Both involve charge, but static electricity stays put and lasts only a moment (a tiny spark); mains electricity from the electricity company (PLN) flows all the time through wires. Lightning is giant static electricity.")
A.b05=new B.D("In a thunderstorm, it is safe to shelter under a tall tree.","Tall trees are actually often struck by lightning. It is safer to go inside a house or building. If you are in a rice field, a garden or an open field, head straight for the nearest building.")
A.aLc=s([A.aZP,A.b_w,A.b05],t.I)
A.Qc=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","What makes the balloon able to pull the paper?",A.E,"Now it's your turn to explain to Si Jabrik. What makes the balloon suddenly able to pull the pieces of paper? And what big thing in the sky is also static electricity?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aLc)
A.O9=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","A lightning bolt is hotter than the surface of the Sun!",A.a_,'Now we can answer our question from the start: rubbing makes electric charges move. This next fact might surprise you. Lightning is giant static electricity. When lightning strikes, the air along its path can heat up to hotter than the surface of the Sun \u2014 in the blink of an eye! That sudden heat is what makes the loud "BOOM" of thunder. But scientists are still working out exactly how lightning gets started inside a cloud!',"A round of applause for static electricity, from balloons to lightning! Challenge at home: rub a comb, a plastic ruler or a straw on dry hair, then hold it near tiny bits of paper. Which one pulls hardest? Write down the winner!",null,null,!1,null,'A static spark too small for us to feel can still damage a chip \u2014 the tiny "brain" inside phones and computers. That\'s why workers in electronics factories, including in Indonesia, wear anti-static wrist straps that carry the charge from their bodies to the ground.','Over Lake Maracaibo in Venezuela (South America), thunderstorms happen on most nights of the year, sometimes with dozens of flashes a minute. Sailors once used it as a natural "lighthouse" to find their way.',null)
A.aLn=s([A.QD,A.Rz,A.Oz,A.O7,A.Qc,A.O9],t.n)
A.aVh=s(["1 blown-up balloon","Your own dry hair (or a woollen cloth)","Tiny pieces of paper"],t.s)
A.aSv=s(["Tear paper into tiny pieces and put them on the table.","Rub the balloon on dry hair for about 10\u201315 seconds.","Bring the balloon close to the paper pieces \u2014 the paper jumps up and sticks!","Also try bringing the balloon near a friend's hair: the hair stands up, pulled towards the balloon."],t.s)
A.YA=new B.J("The Magic Pulling Balloon","Rubbing creates static electricity that can attract light objects.",A.aVh,A.aSv,'When the balloon is rubbed on hair, tiny electric charges move across and the balloon becomes "charged". The charge attracts light things like paper and hair \u2014 this is called static electricity. It is the same thing that makes lightning in the sky, only much, much smaller.',A.D,"Safe for children to do, with care: for young children, the teacher blows up the balloon; pick up the pieces of a burst balloon straight away (they can be swallowed); watch for children with a latex allergy. Works best when the air is dry, not humid.",10)
A.aLe=s(["A balloon (or a plastic comb)","Dry hair or a woollen cloth","A tap/a very thin stream of water \u2014 if there is no tap, use an old bottle with a small hole made with a nail, or pour slowly from a water scoop"],t.s)
A.aUX=s(["Make a stream of water as thin as a thread: open the tap a tiny bit, or pour slowly from the bottle with a small hole while a friend holds it.","Rub the balloon/comb on hair for about 15 seconds.","Bring the balloon close to the stream of water (without touching).","Observe: the stream of water BENDS towards the balloon!"],t.s)
A.YY=new B.J("Bending Water","A statically charged object attracts a thin stream of water.",A.aLe,A.aUX,"Rubbing gives the balloon a charge of static electricity. The charge attracts the water molecules passing by, so the stream bends towards it \u2014 the same effect as a balloon picking up paper, but this time it pulls water.",A.D,"Works best when the air is dry. Wipe up spilt water so the floor is not slippery. The teacher makes the hole in the bottle with the nail. If you use a balloon: the teacher blows it up for young children, throw away burst balloons straight away (they can be swallowed), and watch for children with a latex allergy.",10)
A.aQU=s(["A mixture of salt + ground pepper on a plate","A plastic spoon or a comb","Dry hair or a woollen cloth"],t.s)
A.aU_=s(["Mix a little salt and ground pepper on the plate.","Rub the plastic spoon/comb on hair for about 15 seconds.","Bring it close to the mixture (without touching).","Observe: the lighter pepper jumps up and sticks to the spoon; the salt stays behind."],t.s)
A.a_n=new B.J("Separating Pepper & Salt","A statically charged object attracts lighter things first.",A.aQU,A.aU_,"Rubbing gives the spoon a charge of static electricity. The charge attracts light things; pepper is much lighter than salt, so the pepper is pulled up first \u2014 and the mixture separates without being touched.",A.D,"This mixture is not for eating after the experiment.",10)
A.aS1=s([A.YA,A.YY,A.a_n],t.T)
A.b6V=new B.aD("Does not yet understand that rubbing can create a pull.","Knows objects can attract after being rubbed, but cannot yet give a real example.","Explains that rubbing creates static electricity AND gives one real example.")
A.b2f=new B.aC(u.no,u.e4,'Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Collect the children\'s experiences of hair standing up or a small "shock" when touching a door handle.',"Main activity (20 minutes): Explain that static electricity comes from rubbing. Do the experiment of a balloon attracting paper and hair. Contrast it with current electricity (circuits), which they have already learned.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: lightning is giant static electricity jumping from the clouds to the Earth.","Pupils can explain that rubbing creates static electricity and give one real example.",A.b2,u.e,A.b6V)
A.blN=new B.aw("listrik_statis","Static Electricity","\u26a1",A.ao,A.bv,u.no,u.e4,30,"This experiment works best in dry weather. Clearly separate it from current electricity (the Electricity & Circuits lesson) so the children don't mix them up.",A.aLn,A.aS1,A.b2f,null,!0,!0,"Ask the children to find 2 other examples of static electricity (hair standing up, a shock when touching a door handle) and explain why they happen.",'Rub a balloon or a ruler on hair, then bring it near pieces of paper \u2014 watch them get pulled. Say "rub \u2192 charged \u2192 attracts".',null)
A.Oi=new B.f(A.Z,"Is the Sun walking?","Morning: Sun in the east. Evening: in the west. Did it walk?",A.E,"Everyone, in the morning the Sun comes up in the east, and in the evening it goes down in the west. Does the Sun really travel all the way around us?","Let the children guess. Many think the Sun is the one moving.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Mk=new B.f(A.Y,"The Earth spins","The Earth spins like a spinning top.",A.v,"Look outside for a moment: is it light or dark right now? What about tonight? Every day, light and dark take turns. Have you ever seen a solar lamp? In the daytime, a solar panel \u2014 a board that turns light into electricity \u2014 charges its battery. But at night, the panel stops making electricity! Where do you think the Sun goes when night comes? Does it go to sleep?",null,"Draw the Sun on the left and the Earth as a ball on the right. Shade the right side of the Earth (dark = night) and leave the left side light (day).",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bck=new B.m("siang","\ud83c\udf1e","Day","DAY: the side of the Earth that is facing the Sun. It gets light, so it's bright and warm.")
A.bc4=new B.m("malam","\ud83c\udf19","Night","NIGHT: the side of the Earth facing away from the Sun. It gets no light, so it's dark. We see the moon and the stars.")
A.beT=new B.m("rotasi","\ud83d\udd04","Rotation","ROTATION: the Earth spins all the way round once in about 24 hours. That's why day and night take turns every day.")
A.aOw=s([A.bck,A.bc4,A.beT],t.w)
A.Mp=new B.f(A.W,"How does the day change?","Tap each card to find out.",A.v,"Let's find out why day and night take turns!",null,"Label the DAY side and the NIGHT side on your Earth drawing.",A.aOw,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.SH=new B.f(A.X,"Be the Earth & the Sun!","Turn slowly \u2014 feel day turn into night.",A.v,"Now let's act it out \u2014 you're going to be the Earth!","One child is the Sun (standing still). Another child is the Earth, turning slowly on the spot. Face towards the Sun = day, back to the Sun = night. Or use a ball + a torch.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b_W=new B.D("The Sun travels from east to west.","It's the Earth that spins, not the Sun. The Earth spins towards the east, so the Sun seems to appear in the east. Remember the ball + torch demonstration.")
A.aZc=new B.D("Night is dark because the Sun goes away or switches off.","The Sun keeps on shining \u2014 it's just that our side of the Earth is facing away from it. At that moment, it's daytime on the other side of the Earth.")
A.aU0=s([A.b_W,A.aZc],t.I)
A.PR=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Why does it get dark at night?",A.E,"Si Jabrik is confused \u2014 help him out! Why does it get dark at night? And what is really moving \u2014 the Sun or the Earth?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aU0)
A.SV=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","The Earth spins at about 1,600 km/h, but we can't feel it!",A.a_,"Remember our question \u2014 does the Sun really move around us? Now you know the answer! Did you know? At the equator, the Earth spins at about 1,600 kilometres an hour \u2014 faster than a jet plane! But we don't feel dizzy, because everything around us spins along with us, nice and smoothly.","A round of applause for the Earth, spinning us around! Task for tomorrow: stand a twig upright in a sunny patch of ground, then mark the tip of its shadow with a small stone in the morning, at midday and in the afternoon. The shadow moves because our Earth is spinning!",null,null,!1,null,"Because the Earth spins towards the east, the Sun rises in Papua about three hours earlier than in Aceh. That's why Indonesia has three time zones \u2014 WIB, WITA and WIT \u2014 which radio and TV presenters often mention.","In Troms\xf8, a town in Norway far in the north of the world, the Sun does not set at all for about two months in summer. In Indonesia, close to the equator, day and night are almost the same length all year round.",null)
A.aWx=s([A.Oi,A.Mk,A.Mp,A.SH,A.PR,A.SV],t.n)
A.aVA=s(["1 ball or orange (as the Earth)","A torch (as the Sun)","A marker pen/small sticker (to mark our town)"],t.s)
A.aUv=s(["Put a dot on the ball \u2014 that's the town where we live.","In a fairly dark room, shine the torch (the Sun) on one side of the ball.","The side lit by the torch = DAY; the dark side behind = NIGHT.","Turn the ball slowly. Watch our dot move from light into dark."],t.s)
A.a0G=new B.J("Spinning Earth","The Earth's rotation makes day and night take turns.",A.aVA,A.aUv,"The Earth spins on its axis like a spinning top. The side facing the Sun has day; the side facing away has night. One full turn takes about 24 hours \u2014 that's one day. The Sun doesn't really \"rise\"; it's the Earth that is turning.",A.D,u.l,15)
A.aRa=s(["A place where you can see the sky","A notebook"],t.s)
A.aVt=s(["In the morning, observe which direction the Sun is in (east).","At midday, notice that the Sun is almost overhead.","In the afternoon, observe the Sun leaning towards the west.","Record the three observations and discuss the pattern."],t.s)
A.a0u=new B.J("Following the Sun's Position","The Sun seems to move from east to west because the Earth rotates.",A.aRa,A.aVt,"During the day, the Sun seems to move from east to west. In fact it is not the Sun that moves, but the Earth that spins (rotation). That is what makes morning, midday and afternoon look different.",A.D,"DO NOT look straight at the Sun \u2014 just notice its direction & the shadows.",480)
A.ayu=s([A.a0G,A.a0u],t.T)
A.b6j=new B.aD("Still sure that the Sun moves around the Earth.","Knows the Earth spins, but cannot yet link this to the lit side (day) and the dark side (night).","Explains day/night as the Earth spinning on its axis \u2014 the side facing the Sun = day.")
A.b1n=new B.aC(u.b_,"Does the Sun really move around us?","Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Many children think the Sun is the one moving \u2014 let them share their ideas first.",'Main activity (20 minutes): Use a ball + a torch to show that the Earth spins on its axis. Point out the lit side (day) and the dark side (night). Turn the ball to show the change-over. Correct the misconception that "the Sun moves".',"Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with a fact: at the equator, the Earth spins very fast \u2014 about 1,600 km/h \u2014 but we can't feel it.","Pupils can explain that day/night happens because the Earth spins, not because the Sun goes around the Earth.",A.b2,u.d,A.b6j)
A.ble=new B.aw("siang_malam","Day & Night","\ud83c\udf0d",A.ao,A.bw,u.b_,"Does the Sun really move around us?",30,"A fairly dark room makes the ball + torch demonstration much clearer. Close the curtains for a while if you can.",A.aWx,A.ayu,A.b1n,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to explain why, when it is daytime for us, it can be night-time in another country.","Use a ball + a torch, slowly. Point to one dot (our town) moving from light into dark as the ball turns.",u.k)
A.M1=new B.f(A.Z,"Why 4 seasons there, but not here?","Some countries have spring, summer, autumn, and winter. And Indonesia?",A.E,"Everyone, we already know the Earth spins on its axis, and that's why we have day and night. But the Earth also goes on a much, much longer journey: all the way around the Sun! Have you ever heard a friend or relative abroad talk about snowy winters, autumn, or spring? So why do you think we never get a snowy winter here in Indonesia?","Ask who has seen a photo/video of snow or falling autumn leaves from another country. Let them guess first.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.R4=new B.f(A.Y,"The Earth's long journey around the Sun","The Earth spins on its axis (rotation) AND travels around the Sun (revolution).",A.v,"Rotation is the Earth spinning on its own axis. One full spin takes 24 hours \u2014 and that gives us day and night. Revolution is different: the Earth travels around the Sun, and one full trip takes about 365 days \u2014 that's one year! Farmers and fishers have long noticed that the seasons come round in turn every year. And here's what makes it interesting: the Earth's axis isn't straight up and down. It's tilted by about 23.5 degrees, and it always tilts the same way, all the way round.",null,"Draw the Sun in the middle, then a circle for the Earth's path around the Sun. Draw the Earth at 4 points on that path, each with its axis tilted in the same direction.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bd_=new B.m("poros_miring","\ud83d\udcd0","Tilted Axis","TILTED AXIS: the Earth spins on an axis tilted by 23.5 degrees, and it always tilts the same way all year round. This is the main key to the seasons.")
A.bd4=new B.m("belahan_utara","\u2b06\ufe0f","Northern Hemisphere","NORTHERN HEMISPHERE: when this half leans towards the Sun, it's summer there. Six months later, when it leans away, it's winter there.")
A.baw=new B.m("belahan_selatan","\u2b07\ufe0f","Southern Hemisphere","SOUTHERN HEMISPHERE: its seasons are the opposite of the north's. When it's summer in the north, it's winter in the south \u2014 and the other way round.")
A.bcj=new B.m("khatulistiwa","\ud83c\udf34","Equator","EQUATOR (the line around the middle of the Earth): Indonesia is close to it. The Sun is almost always high in the sky here, all year round, so there's no winter/summer like in countries far from the equator \u2014 just a rainy season & a dry season, driven by the monsoon winds.")
A.aM9=s([A.bd_,A.bd4,A.baw,A.bcj],t.w)
A.SP=new B.f(A.W,"What causes the seasons","Tap each part \u2014 find out what makes the seasons.",A.v,"Let's explore the parts that make the seasons happen!",null,"Label each part on the drawing of the Earth's path.",A.aM9,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Ph=new B.f(A.X,"Act out the Earth going around the Sun!","Move the ball around the torch \u2014 watch the bright side.",A.v,"Now let's try it ourselves with a ball and a torch!",'Do the "Torch & Tilted Ball" experiment: push a thin palm-leaf stick or chopstick through the ball as a tilted axis, then move it around the torch (the Sun) in a fairly dark room, keeping the tilt pointing the same way. Watch which side of the ball stays bright for longer at each part of the path. (The teacher pushes the stick in.)',null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.aZ2=new B.D("Summer happens because the Earth is closer to the Sun.","The distance between the Earth and the Sun stays almost the same all year. Seasons happen because the tilt of the Earth's axis makes sunlight fall more directly on one hemisphere (summer there) and more at a slant on the other (winter there) \u2014 not because the distance changes.")
A.b_O=new B.D("Indonesia also has spring and autumn, like other countries.","Indonesia is close to the equator, so the Sun is almost always high in the sky all year round. We only have a rainy season and a dry season, which depend more on the monsoon winds.")
A.b06=new B.D('The rainy season always starts in the same month every year, for example in the months ending in "-ber".',"In many regions the rainy season does come around the end of the year, but it can start earlier or later, and in parts of Maluku it comes in the middle of the year. That's why Indonesia's weather agency (BMKG) makes a forecast of when the season will start in each region, and farmers use it to decide when to plant.")
A.aUy=s([A.aZ2,A.b_O,A.b06],t.I)
A.LU=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","What really causes the seasons?",A.E,"Now it's your turn to explain to Si Jabrik. What really makes the seasons change in countries far from the equator? And why does Indonesia have only two seasons?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aUy)
A.QY=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Every 4 years, February has 29 days!",A.a_,"Remember our question at the start? Now you know: seasons happen because the Earth's axis is tilted as it travels around the Sun \u2014 not because of the distance. And here's a surprise: one trip around the Sun doesn't take exactly 365 days. It takes about 365 and a QUARTER days! That extra quarter of a day is saved up for 4 years until it makes 1 whole day. Then it's added on as the 29th of February \u2014 that's a LEAP YEAR!","Give a round of applause \u2014 then ask the children to work out the last leap year and the next one (multiples of 4). Challenge: tomorrow morning, note behind which tree or roof the Sun rises (don't stare at it). Look again a month later: has the sunrise point moved?",null,null,!1,null,"For generations, farmers in Java have used a traditional farming calendar that divides the year into 12 small seasons, based on signs in nature, to decide when to plant.","At Stonehenge in England, giant stones set up about 4,500 years ago line up with the sunrise on the longest day of the year. People long ago watched the Sun to understand the seasons too \u2014 just like your challenge!",null)
A.aU7=s([A.M1,A.R4,A.SP,A.Ph,A.LU,A.QY],t.n)
A.akW=s(["1 small ball (an orange or a plastic ball) as the Earth","1 thin palm-leaf stick or chopstick pushed right through the ball, as the axis","A torch as the Sun","A fairly dark room"],t.s)
A.aNp=s(["Push the stick/chopstick right through the middle of the ball \u2014 this is the Earth's axis. Tilt the axis a little; do not hold it straight up.","Switch on the torch in the middle of the room as the Sun. Dim the room lights.","Move the ball around the torch in a circular path, keeping the direction of the axis tilt the SAME (pointing the same way) all the way round.","Observe: on one side of the path, the top part of the ball (near the axis) faces the torch for longer and is brighter. On the opposite side of the path, it is the bottom part of the ball's turn to be brighter."],t.s)
A.a_s=new B.J("Torch & Tilted Ball","The tilt of the Earth's axis + its revolution around the Sun = seasons.",A.akW,A.aNp,"The Earth's axis is tilted by about 23.5 degrees, and the direction of the tilt always stays the same throughout its revolution around the Sun (365 days). Because of this, sometimes the northern hemisphere leans towards the Sun (summer in the north), and sometimes the southern hemisphere leans towards it (summer in the south). This axis tilt + revolution is what causes the four seasons to change \u2014 NOT changes in the distance between the Earth and the Sun.",A.D,"The stick/chopstick has a sharp end \u2014 the teacher pushes it into the ball; the children hold only the blunt part, and stay supervised.",15)
A.aP0=s(["1 torch","1 sheet of paper","1 pencil","A fairly dark room (so it is easier to see)"],t.s)
A.aP4=s(["Put the paper on a table or stick it to the wall.","Shine the torch straight at the paper (at 90 degrees) from a fixed distance. Draw around the bright patch of light with the pencil.","Now tilt the torch (keeping the same distance from the paper) and shine it on the same paper. Draw around the patch of light again.","Compare the two shapes: which is smaller and brighter, and which is bigger and dimmer?"],t.s)
A.a_O=new B.J("Straight vs Tilted Torch","Light that falls at a slant spreads out wider and feels cooler.",A.aP0,A.aP4,"Light from a torch shining straight at the paper falls on a small area, so the light and heat are concentrated (brighter/warmer). Light from a tilted torch spreads over a wider area, so the light and heat are more spread out (dimmer/cooler). Sunlight works the same way: the hemisphere leaning towards the Sun gets more direct sunlight (warmer, summer), while the hemisphere leaning away gets more slanted sunlight (cooler, winter) \u2014 just as the morning sun feels cooler than the midday sun.",A.D,null,10)
A.aU8=s([A.a_s,A.a_O],t.T)
A.b77=new B.aD("Cannot yet tell the difference between the Earth's rotation and revolution.","Knows the Earth goes around the Sun (revolution), but cannot yet explain why seasons happen.","Explains that the tilt of the Earth's axis + revolution causes the seasons, and that the Earth\u2013Sun distance is NOT the cause.")
A.b2g=new B.aC("Pupils can tell the difference between the Earth's rotation and revolution, and explain that the tilt of the Earth's axis causes the seasons to change.",u.hh,'Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Briefly recall the "Day & Night" lesson (rotation, 24 hours) to contrast it with revolution, which is the topic today (365 days).',"Main activity (20 minutes): Explain the Earth's revolution around the Sun and the Earth's axis, tilted at 23.5 degrees. Explore the parts: tilted axis, northern hemisphere, southern hemisphere, equator. Demonstrate with a ball on a thin palm-leaf stick, with the axis tilt kept fixed, moving around a torch, and compare a torch shining straight vs tilted on paper.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. Correct the misconception that the seasons are caused by the distance between the Earth and the Sun. Finish with the leap-year surprise.","Pupils can explain that seasons happen because of the tilt of the Earth's axis as it revolves, not because of the distance between the Earth and the Sun.",A.b2,u.d,A.b77)
A.blE=new B.aw("revolusi_bumi_musim","Earth's Revolution & Seasons","\ud83c\udf0f",A.ao,A.bw,"Pupils can explain that the Earth's revolution around the Sun, together with the tilt of the Earth's axis, causes the seasons to change.",u.hh,30,'If there is time, first mention the "Day & Night" lesson (rotation) to show the difference \u2014 revolution is a different story, on a different timescale (365 days, not 24 hours). Stress it again and again: seasons are NOT caused by the Earth\u2013Sun distance.',A.aU7,A.aU8,A.b2g,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to work it out: if 1 year is really about 365.25 days, why do we need a leap year (29 February) every 4 years?","Hold a ball with a fixed tilt and walk around a table (the Sun). Show that the top of the ball sometimes leans towards the table and sometimes leans away \u2014 that is how the seasons change.",u.k)
A.MA=new B.f(A.Z,"Is Earth alone?","Is Earth the only planet in the sky?",A.E,"Everyone, we live on Earth. But is Earth the only planet, or are there other planets out there? What do you think \u2014 how many are there?",u.n,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.SD=new B.f(A.Y,"The Sun's family","The Sun + 8 planets = the Solar System.",A.v,"Look at this photo of the night sky. Among the stars there are bright dots that aren't stars at all! What could they be? Far from bright lights \u2014 on a beach, on a mountain, on a small island \u2014 our eyes can see thousands of stars. Too much lamplight hides them; this is called light pollution. Does Earth go around the Sun all alone, or does it have friends? Let's meet them one by one!",null,"Draw the Sun on the left, then a row of empty circles from near to far. Fill in the planet names and Earth's position together with the children during DISCOVER.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bfb=new B.m("matahari","\u2600\ufe0f","Sun","SUN: the star at the centre of the Solar System. It is the only one that shines by itself, giving heat and light to all the planets.")
A.bbq=new B.m("bumi","\ud83c\udf0d","Earth","EARTH: the third planet, where we live. The only planet known to have water and life.")
A.bcg=new B.m("jupiter","\ud83e\ude90","Jupiter","JUPITER: the biggest planet in the Solar System, so big that more than 1,000 Earths could fit inside it.")
A.bb0=new B.m("orbit","\ud83d\udd01","Orbit","ORBIT: a planet's path around the Sun. Near planets orbit quickly, far planets orbit very slowly.")
A.aWu=s([A.bfb,A.bbq,A.bcg,A.bb0],t.w)
A.Sf=new B.f(A.W,"Meet the Solar System","Tap each card to find out.",A.v,"Let's meet the members of our Solar System!",null,"Add labels to the Solar System drawing from before.",A.aWu,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Ow=new B.f(A.X,"Planets in a line!","Act out the planets orbiting the Sun.",A.v,"Now let's be a living Solar System!","One child is the Sun in the middle. Eight children line up as the planets in order, then walk around the Sun. Notice that near planets get round faster than far ones.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b_p=new B.D("The Sun goes around the Earth.","Earth and the other planets go around the Sun. The Sun stays at the centre of the Solar System as a star.")
A.b13=new B.D("The Sun is a planet.","The Sun is a star \u2014 it shines and gives off heat by itself. Planets do not shine by themselves; they only reflect the Sun's light.")
A.aYS=new B.D("The Solar System has 9 planets, including Pluto.","Since 2006, astronomers have classed Pluto as a dwarf planet, because it is small and its path is crowded with other objects. So the Solar System has 8 planets. Experts can change their minds when they find new evidence \u2014 that's how science works.")
A.aTO=s([A.b_p,A.b13,A.aYS],t.I)
A.MB=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Counting from the Sun, which planet is Earth?",A.E,"Who can explain this for Si Jabrik? Counting out from the Sun, which number planet is Earth? And what's at the centre of the Solar System?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aTO)
A.RB=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Sunlight takes 8 minutes to reach Earth!",A.a_,"Remember our question from the start \u2014 is Earth the only planet, or are there others? Now you know the answer! Here's the surprise: the Sun is very far away \u2014 about 150 million kilometres. Its light takes about 8 MINUTES to reach Earth. So the sunlight you see right now actually left the Sun 8 minutes ago! Scientists are still trying to find out: is there life anywhere else? One place they are studying is the ocean under the ice of Europa, one of Jupiter's moons.","A round of applause for our huge Solar System! Tonight, look for a bright dot whose light does not twinkle \u2014 it might be a planet, not a star!",null,null,!1,null,"Because its skies are often clear and dark, Indonesia has been building a national observatory \u2014 a home for telescopes to study stars and planets \u2014 on Mount Timau in East Nusa Tenggara, with a main telescope mirror about 3.8 metres wide. The next Indonesian astronomer \u2014 a girl or a boy \u2014 could be sitting in this classroom!","On Mars, the red planet next door to Earth, six-wheeled robot rovers the size of a car have been driving around for years. They are controlled from Earth, drill into rocks and look for signs that there was once water \u2014 and maybe life.",null)
A.aV4=s([A.MA,A.SD,A.Sf,A.Ow,A.MB,A.RB],t.n)
A.aT5=s(["9 children (1 is the Sun, 8 are planets) or 9 labelled sheets of paper","A school yard/roomy classroom","A marker for writing the planet names"],t.s)
A.aPj=s(["One child stands in the middle as the SUN.","Eight children line up in order: Mercury (nearest) to Neptune (farthest).",'Each "planet" walks around the Sun (orbits).',"Observe: near planets go round quickly, far planets go round slowly."],t.s)
A.a0y=new B.J("Planets in a Line","Eight planets go around the Sun at different distances.",A.aT5,A.aPj,"Our Solar System is made up of the Sun at the centre and eight planets going around it: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune. Earth is the third planet, the only one known to have life. Planets do not shine by themselves \u2014 they reflect the Sun's light.",A.D,null,15)
A.a2e=s(["A roll of toilet paper or a long rope","Name cards for the 8 planets + the Sun","A long yard/corridor"],t.s)
A.aQ0=s(["Put the Sun card at one end.","Unroll the paper/rope; place the planets further and further apart as you go out (Mercury close, Neptune very far).","Ask the children to walk from the Sun to each planet.","Feel how much further away the outer planets are than the inner planets."],t.s)
A.YK=new B.J("Planet Distance Scale","The distances between planets are huge and uneven.",A.a2e,A.aQ0,"The inner planets (Mercury\u2013Mars) are close together, while the outer planets (Jupiter\u2013Neptune) are very far apart. Modelling the distances lets children feel how vast the Solar System is \u2014 maps in books are often misleading because the distances are squashed together.",A.D,null,20)
A.aRf=s(["Objects of different sizes: a big ball (the Sun), an orange, a marble, seeds/grains"],t.s)
A.aNM=s(["Predict before choosing the objects: which planet is the biggest? The smallest?","Choose objects to stand for the size of the Sun and each planet.","Put them in order and compare their sizes.","Show that the Sun (the big ball) is much bigger than all the planets.","Show Jupiter (biggest) vs Earth (small) vs Mercury (smallest)."],t.s)
A.YX=new B.J("Comparing Planet Sizes","The Sun is much bigger than the planets; the planets are different sizes.",A.aRf,A.aNM,"The Sun is much bigger than any planet. Among the planets, Jupiter is the biggest and Mercury the smallest; Earth is one of the small ones. This is a SIZE scale \u2014 different from the DISTANCE scale, which is also huge.",A.D,null,15)
A.aDY=s([A.a0y,A.YK,A.YX],t.T)
A.b74=new B.aD("Cannot yet name the planets, or thinks the Sun goes around the Earth.","Names a few planets, but not yet in order, or does not yet understand that the planets go around the Sun.","Names 4 planets in order AND explains that the planets go around the Sun.")
A.b1D=new B.aC(u.ob,u.M,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Have the children imagine Earth as one tiny dot among the other planets.","Main activity (20 minutes): Introduce the Sun at the centre and the eight planets in order. Act out the planets lining up/orbiting. Stress that Earth is the third planet, the only inhabited one we know of.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: sunlight takes \xb18 minutes to reach Earth.","Pupils can name at least 4 planets in order and explain that the planets go around the Sun.",A.b2,u.d,A.b74)
A.blL=new B.aw("tata_surya","The Solar System","\ud83e\ude90",A.ao,A.bw,u.ob,u.M,30,'The "planets in a line" model with children in the school yard makes the scale of the Solar System feel real and fun. Walking the orbits helps it click.',A.aV4,A.aDY,A.b1D,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to put the 8 planets in order from the Sun and guess which are hotter or colder based on their distance.","Act it out: one child is the Sun in the middle (standing still), one is Earth walking around it. Make clear that the Sun is at the centre.",u.k)
A.Re=new B.f(A.Z,"Why does its shape change?","Tonight a half moon, last week a full one. Why?",A.E,"Everyone, some nights the moon looks completely round, some nights it's just a thin crescent, and some nights you can't see it at all. Why do you think the moon's shape keeps changing?",u.n,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Ms=new B.f(A.Y,"The Moon borrows light","The Moon reflects the Sun's light.",A.v,"Who has ever watched the moon for a few nights in a row? Did its shape stay the same or keep changing? But the moon is a round ball \u2014 so why does it sometimes look like a crescent, and sometimes like a half? And where does the moon get its light from?",null,"Draw the Sun, the Earth, and the Moon in several positions around the Earth. Shade the dark part of the Moon.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bd9=new B.m("bulan_baru","\ud83c\udf11","New moon","NEW MOON: the Moon's bright side faces away from Earth, so the Moon can hardly be seen from Earth.")
A.bbh=new B.m("sabit","\ud83c\udf19","Crescent","CRESCENT: only a small part of the Moon's bright side can be seen, in a thin curved shape.")
A.bbZ=new B.m("separuh","\ud83c\udf13","Half moon","HALF MOON (quarter): half of the Moon looks bright, the other half is dark.")
A.ba_=new B.m("purnama","\ud83c\udf15","Full moon","FULL MOON: the whole side of the Moon facing Earth is lit by the Sun, so it looks completely round and bright. Fishers know: at full moon, the high tide comes higher.")
A.aWz=s([A.bd9,A.bbh,A.bbZ,A.ba_],t.w)
A.Nj=new B.f(A.W,"Phases of the Moon","Tap each phase to find out.",A.v,"Let's get to know the shapes (phases) of the moon!",null,"Name each phase on the drawing of the Moon's positions.",A.aWz,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Of=new B.f(A.X,"Act out the moon phases!","A ball + a torch show the moon's phases.",A.v,"Now let's act out the phases of the moon!","Make the room fairly dark. One child holds the torch (Sun), another holds the ball (Moon) and carries it around a third child's head (Earth). Watch the lit part of the ball change to crescent, half, full.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b_Q=new B.D("The Moon shines by itself like a lamp.","The Moon does not shine by itself; it reflects the Sun's light. The side of the Moon facing the Sun is the part that looks bright.")
A.aZq=new B.D("The Moon changes shape because clouds cover it each night.","The Moon's phases change because the positions of the Sun-Earth-Moon change how much of the lit part we see. The Earth's shadow only falls on the Moon during an eclipse.")
A.b0H=new B.D("The Moon only appears in the sky at night.","The Moon is often seen in the daytime too, especially in the morning or afternoon when it is a half moon. Try looking for a pale moon in the blue sky!")
A.aN_=s([A.b_Q,A.aZq,A.b0H],t.I)
A.Qk=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","Does the Moon make its own light or reflect light?",A.E,"Now it's your turn to explain to Si Jabrik. Does the Moon make its own light, or does it reflect light from something else? And when it looks completely round, what is that phase called?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aN_)
A.NO=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Astronaut footprints on the Moon can last millions of years!",A.a_,"So now we know the answer to our question: the Moon itself doesn't change shape \u2014 what changes is how much of its sunlit side we can see. This next fact might surprise you. On the Moon there's no air and no wind. So the footprints of the astronauts who landed on the Moon decades ago are still there today \u2014 and they could last for millions of years, because there's no wind to blow them away! Scientists are also still working out exactly how the Moon formed. The best idea so far: long ago, an object the size of Mars crashed into the young Earth, and the pieces gathered to make the Moon.","A round of applause for the Moon, our night-time friend! Tonight and tomorrow night, if the moon is out, draw its shape in your exercise book at the same time. That is your very own observation data!",null,null,!1,null,"The pull of the Moon (helped by the Sun) makes the sea rise and fall: the tides. By working out where the Moon will be, Indonesia's weather agency (BMKG) can predict extra-high tides and warn about coastal flooding \u2014 sea water coming onto the land \u2014 so people on the coast can get ready.","The Moon always shows the same side to the Earth; the other side gets sunlight too, it just never faces us. In 2019, a lander and rover from China became the first to land on that side of the Moon, which can't be seen from Earth.",null)
A.ayq=s([A.Re,A.Ms,A.Nj,A.Of,A.Qk,A.NO],t.n)
A.ays=s(["1 ball (as the Moon)","A torch (as the Sun)","Your own head (as the Earth)"],t.s)
A.aNh=s(["Make the room fairly dark. One child holds the torch (Sun) and shines it on the ball.","Another child holds the ball (Moon) and turns round slowly on the spot; their head is the Earth.","Watch how the lit part of the ball changes as they turn.","Sometimes it is fully lit (full moon), sometimes dark (new moon), sometimes partly lit."],t.s)
A.Z4=new B.J("Moon Phases with a Ball","The Moon's shape seems to change because of where it is compared with the Sun.",A.ays,A.aNh,"The Moon does not shine by itself \u2014 it reflects the Sun's light. As the Moon goes around the Earth, how much of its sunlit side we can see keeps changing: new moon, crescent, half moon, all the way to full moon. These are the phases of the Moon, which repeat about every 29\u201330 days.",A.D,u.l,15)
A.aVj=s(["A tray filled with a thick layer of flour, with a little cocoa/coffee powder sprinkled on top","Marbles or small stones of different sizes"],t.s)
A.aNX=s(["Smooth the flour flat and sprinkle a thin layer of cocoa powder on top.","Drop marbles onto the tray from different heights.","Lift the marbles out and observe the holes (craters) and the splash around them.","Compare: big marbles / higher drops make bigger craters."],t.s)
A.Zz=new B.J("Make Moon Craters","Moon craters are made by space rocks hitting its surface.",A.aVj,A.aNX,"Craters on the Moon form when space rocks (meteors) hit its surface, throwing material out all around. Because the Moon has no air and no weather, its craters last millions of years \u2014 they are not worn away like on Earth.",A.D,"Do it on a mat or outside so it is easy to clean up.",20)
A.aQb=s(["An exercise book & a pencil","A night sky you can see"],t.s)
A.aLp=s(["Every night (if the sky is clear), observe the shape of the Moon.","Draw its shape and write the date.","Do this for about two weeks.","Put the drawings in order and look at the pattern."],t.s)
A.a_W=new B.J("Moon Calendar","The Moon's shape changes in a regular cycle (about 29\u201330 days).",A.aQb,A.aLp,"From night to night, the Moon's shape changes regularly: a crescent grows into a full moon, then shrinks again. One full cycle takes about 29\u201330 days \u2014 that is the basis of lunar calendars (the Hijri and Javanese calendars).",A.D,"Night-time observing is best done with an adult.",20160)
A.aPw=s([A.Z4,A.Zz,A.a_W],t.T)
A.b6y=new B.aD("Still thinks the Moon shines by itself like a lamp.","Knows the Moon reflects the Sun's light, but does not yet name the phases of the Moon.","Explains that the Moon reflects the Sun's light AND names at least 2 phases of the Moon.")
A.b28=new B.aC(u.Y,u.it,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Have the children remember the different shapes of the moon they have seen at night.","Main activity (20 minutes): Explain that the Moon reflects the Sun's light and does not shine by itself. Act out the phases of the Moon with a ball + torch. Introduce the phases: new moon, crescent, half moon, full moon.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: there is no air on the Moon, so astronauts' footprints can last for millions of years.","Pupils can explain that the Moon reflects the Sun's light and name at least 2 phases of the Moon.",A.b2,u.d,A.b6y)
A.bma=new B.aw("bulan_fase","The Moon & Its Phases","\ud83c\udf19",A.ao,A.bw,u.Y,u.it,30,"Encourage the children to watch the moon for a few nights at home. Seeing its shape change for themselves makes the idea of moon phases far more memorable.",A.ayq,A.aPw,A.b28,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to watch the Moon for a few nights and draw how its phase changes, then predict the next phase in the sequence.","Use a ball and a torch: the torch (Sun) shines on the ball (Moon); turn the ball and watch the lit part change \u2014 those are the Moon's phases.",u.k)
A.TA=new B.f(A.Z,"Why doesn't water run out?","We use water every day. Why does it never run out?",A.E,"Everyone, every day people, animals and plants use water. But the water on Earth never seems to run out. Where does water go after we use it, and how does it come back?",u.n,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.NX=new B.f(A.Y,"Water goes round","Evaporates \u2192 clouds \u2192 rain \u2192 back to Earth.",A.v,"Look closely: wet washing on the line dries and the water seems to vanish, the clouds get darker, then rain falls and flows into the rivers. So why does water never run out? What do you think \u2014 where does water go on its journey, so it can come back again?",null,"Draw the sea at the bottom, an arrow of vapour rising to a cloud, an arrow of rain falling on a mountain, then a river flowing back to the sea. Make it a loop.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bdw=new B.m("penguapan","\u2600\ufe0f","Evaporation","EVAPORATION: the Sun's heat turns water in seas & rivers into vapour that rises into the sky. This is where the cycle starts.")
A.bd1=new B.m("pengembunan","\u2601\ufe0f","Condensation","CONDENSATION: vapour in the sky cools and gathers into tiny water droplets \u2014 forming CLOUDS.")
A.b9Y=new B.m("hujan","\ud83c\udf27\ufe0f","Rain","RAIN (precipitation): the droplets in the clouds get heavier and fall to Earth as rain, filling rivers and the ground.")
A.bdu=new B.m("aliran","\ud83c\udfde\ufe0f","Flowing back","FLOWING: rainwater soaks into the ground or flows through ditches and rivers back to the sea, then the cycle starts again. Keep ditches free of rubbish so the water flows freely!")
A.aer=s([A.bdw,A.bd1,A.b9Y,A.bdu],t.w)
A.MZ=new B.f(A.W,"Stages of the water cycle","Tap each stage to find out.",A.v,"Let's follow each stage of water's journey!",null,"Label each stage on the cycle drawing from before.",A.aer,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.Qd=new B.f(A.X,"Make a mini cycle!","A water bag on the window = a mini cycle.",A.v,"Now let's make our very own water cycle!","Put a little water into a clear plastic bag and tape it to a window that gets sunshine. A few hours later, look for droplets condensing at the top and then dripping down. Discuss which stages are happening.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b_m=new B.D("Clouds are made of smoke or cotton wool.","Clouds are masses of very tiny water droplets made from vapour cooling in the sky, not smoke or cotton wool.")
A.b_7=new B.D("Rainwater is new water made in the sky.","Water is recycled over and over: it evaporates, becomes clouds, falls as rain, goes back to the sea. The same water goes round and never runs out.")
A.aTh=s([A.b_m,A.b_7],t.I)
A.Op=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","What makes seawater evaporate and rise?",A.E,"Si Jabrik is confused \u2014 help him out! What makes seawater evaporate and rise up into the sky? And after it becomes a cloud, how does the water come back down to Earth?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aTh)
A.T0=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","Your drinking water today is millions of years old!",A.a_,"Remember our question from the start \u2014 why does the water on Earth never run out, even though we keep using it? Now you know the answer! Did you know? Because water keeps going round and never runs out, the water you drink today has been on Earth for MILLIONS of years. The very same drop might once have fallen as rain long, long ago, become dew, and then ended up in your glass. Water really does last forever!","A round of applause for the water cycle that never gets tired! Tomorrow morning, look at the dew on leaves or grass, then look again at midday. Where did that water go?",null,null,!1,null,"Raindrops grow around very tiny specks, such as dust or sea salt. So in Indonesia, aeroplanes sometimes scatter salt powder into clouds full of water vapour to make the rain fall sooner \u2014 this is called weather modification \u2014 to help put out forest fires and reduce haze.",'In the Atacama Desert in Chile \u2014 one of the driest places on Earth \u2014 people put up big nets to "catch" fog. Water droplets stick to the nets, then drip into containers as water that people can use.',null)
A.aPR=s([A.TA,A.NX,A.MZ,A.Qd,A.Op,A.T0],t.n)
A.aO2=s(["1 clear plastic bag \u2014 if you have no zip-lock bag, an ordinary plastic bag tied tightly works too","A little water + a little blue food colouring (optional)","Sticky tape + a window that gets sunshine"],t.s)
A.aNk=s(["Put a little water in the plastic bag and seal it tightly.","Tape the bag to a window pane that gets sunlight.","Wait a few hours. Look at the top of the bag.","Water droplets appear at the top (condensing), then drip down (mini rain)."],t.s)
A.a_F=new B.J("Water Cycle in a Bag","Water evaporates, condenses, then falls again \u2014 a mini water cycle.",A.aO2,A.aNk,"The Sun's heat makes the water in the bag evaporate into water vapour. The vapour touches the cooler plastic at the top, condenses into water droplets, then falls back down. This is the water cycle: evaporate \u2192 condense \u2192 rain, repeating again and again without end, just like in nature.",A.D,null,180)
A.aV5=s(["A glass jar + lid","Hot water (PREPARED BY THE TEACHER) + ice cubes","Matches (HELD BY THE TEACHER)"],t.s)
A.aKO=s(["The teacher pours a little hot water into the jar and swirls it so the sides warm up.","The teacher lights a match, puts it in briefly, then puts it out (a little smoke for the particles).","Close the jar straight away and put ice cubes on top of the lid.","Observe: mist/cloud forms inside the jar."],t.s)
A.a_t=new B.J("Make a Cloud in a Jar","Clouds form when water vapour condenses on tiny particles.",A.aV5,A.aKO,"Water vapour from the hot water rises, then cools near the ice. The vapour condenses on the tiny smoke particles, forming water droplets \u2014 that is a cloud. In the sky, clouds form the same way, on dust in the air.",A.ct,"Hot water & matches are handled ONLY by the teacher. Children watch from a safe distance.",15)
A.aMI=s([A.a_F,A.a_t],t.T)
A.b6L=new B.aD("Cannot yet explain, step by step, where rain comes from.","Puts part of the water cycle in order, but does not yet mention the Sun's role.","Puts the stages of the water cycle in order AND explains that the Sun causes evaporation.")
A.b1v=new B.aC(u.iy,u.fk,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Link it to rain, rivers and the drinking water we use every day.","Main activity (20 minutes): Trace the water cycle \u2014 evaporation by the Sun, condensation into clouds, then rain falling back to Earth. Do the water-bag-on-the-window experiment as a mini model.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: the same water has been going round on Earth for millions of years and never really runs out.","Pupils can put the stages of the water cycle in order and explain the Sun's role in evaporation.",A.l9,u.d,A.b6L)
A.blk=new B.aw("siklus_air","The Water Cycle","\ud83c\udf27\ufe0f",A.ao,A.bw,u.iy,u.fk,30,"Link it to everyday experience: rain, laundry drying on the line, morning dew. The children have already seen every stage of the water cycle \u2014 they just need to join them into one story.",A.aPR,A.aMI,A.b1v,null,!1,!1,"Ask the children to draw the whole water cycle with arrows and name each stage (evaporation, condensation, rain, flowing back).",'Show steam from warm water collecting on a cold lid and then dripping \u2014 that\'s a mini water cycle. Say "evaporate \u2192 condense \u2192 drip".',u.y)
A.RF=new B.f(A.Z,"Why so many earthquakes?","Indonesia has lots of volcanoes & earthquakes. Why?",A.E,"Everyone, Indonesia has lots and lots of volcanoes, and sometimes we have earthquakes. What do you think is happening inside the Earth to make volcanoes erupt and the ground shake?",u.n,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Sc=new B.f(A.Y,"The Earth has layers","Inside the Earth there is hot melted rock.",A.v,"In Indonesia the ground often shakes, and we have lots of volcanoes puffing out smoke. In other countries it doesn't happen this often. What do you think is going on inside our Earth that makes the ground wobble and the mountains spit out fire? Don't worry \u2014 many volcanoes have an observation post, where officers watch the volcano every day and tell people when they need to move away.",null,"Draw a slice of the Earth: the outer crust, then a hot red layer (magma) inside. Show a volcano with a magma channel.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.bcQ=new B.m("magma","\ud83d\udd25","Magma","MAGMA: very hot melted rock inside the Earth. When it comes out at the surface through a volcano, it is called lava.")
A.bb6=new B.m("gempa","\u3030\ufe0f","Earthquake","EARTHQUAKE: the Earth shaking when parts of its crust suddenly shift. It can feel gentle or very strong.")
A.be4=new B.m("letusan","\ud83c\udf0b","Eruption","ERUPTION: magma, gas and ash are pushed out of a volcano. The ash can make the soil around it more fertile.")
A.ba0=new B.m("aman","\ud83d\udee1\ufe0f","Staying safe","STAYING SAFE in an earthquake: protect your head, get down under a strong table, keep away from glass, then calmly go out to an open space.")
A.aqu=s([A.bcQ,A.bb6,A.be4,A.ba0],t.w)
A.Rq=new B.f(A.W,"Volcanoes, earthquakes & safety","Tap each card to find out.",A.v,"Let's find out about volcanoes, earthquakes and how to stay safe!",null,"Write the earthquake safety steps on the blackboard.",A.aqu,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.P1=new B.f(A.X,"Mini volcano!",'Baking soda + vinegar = "lava" bubbling over.',A.v,"Now let's make a mini volcano erupt!",'Build a sand volcano around a bottle with baking soda + washing-up liquid + red colouring inside. Pour in the vinegar and watch the "lava" foam spill over. Then practise the earthquake safety steps together.',null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.b0A=new B.D("In an earthquake, you should run straight out of the building.","It's safer to get down, take cover under a strong table, and protect your head. Running while the ground shakes risks falling or being hit by falling things; go outside once the shaking has stopped.")
A.aYR=new B.D("If you feel a strong earthquake near the coast, first wait for news on a phone or the radio.","Don't wait. If the shaking feels strong or long near the coast, or the sea suddenly draws back, calmly go straight to high ground along the evacuation route. Also follow your village's signals, such as the village alarm drum or a siren.")
A.b19=new B.D("A volcano erupts because there is a big fire inside it.","It's not fire, but magma \u2014 very hot melted rock \u2014 being pushed up. The heat comes from deep inside the Earth.")
A.aNe=s([A.b0A,A.aYR,A.b19],t.I)
A.Mo=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","What's the first thing to do in an earthquake?",A.E,"Si Jabrik isn't sure yet. Can you help him? What should we do first when an earthquake happens? And what do we call the hot melted rock inside the Earth?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aNe)
A.LK=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f",'Indonesia sits on the world\'s "Ring of Fire"!',A.a_,"Now we can answer our question from the start: Indonesia sits right where the Earth's plates meet. Listen carefully to this fact. Indonesia lies on the Pacific \"Ring of Fire\" \u2014 the most active belt of volcanoes in the world, with hundreds of volcanoes. That's why our soil is so fertile (from volcanic ash), but it also means we must always be ready for earthquakes. So far, no scientist in the world can predict exactly when an earthquake will happen \u2014 researchers, including in Indonesia, are still looking for a way. That's why knowing about nature helps us be prepared!","A round of applause for prepared, brave children! Task at home: with your family, find a safe place to take cover and the way out of the house, then agree on one meeting place outside.",null,null,!1,null,"Staff at Indonesia's weather agency (BMKG) watch the Earth's shaking day and night with hundreds of earthquake recorders (seismographs) all over Indonesia. If an earthquake could cause a tsunami, a warning goes out within a few minutes by radio, TV, phone and sirens on the coast. In that monitoring room, women and men earthquake experts take turns on duty \u2014 who knows, one day it could be you!","In Japan, which also has many earthquakes, lots of buildings stand on thick rubber-and-steel pads. When the ground shakes, the pads soak up the shaking so the building sways much more gently.",null)
A.aV3=s([A.RF,A.Sc,A.Rq,A.P1,A.Mo,A.LK],t.n)
A.aU1=s(["A small bottle / glass in the middle of a mound of sand or soil","2 spoons of baking soda","Vinegar + a little washing-up liquid + red food colouring (optional)"],t.s)
A.aLm=s(["Build a mound of sand around the bottle so it looks like a mountain.","Put the baking soda into the bottle, and add a little washing-up liquid & red colouring.","Pour the vinegar into the bottle.","Watch: red foam bubbles up and spills out like lava from an erupting volcano!"],t.s)
A.a_z=new B.J("Mini Volcano Eruption",'The gas that forms pushes the "lava" out, like a volcano erupting.',A.aU1,A.aLm,"When baking soda and vinegar mix, they quickly make a gas (carbon dioxide). The gas builds up and pushes the foam out of the bottle. In a real volcano, gas and melted rock (magma) deep inside the Earth are pushed upwards and erupt out as lava.",A.D,"Vinegar & washing-up liquid are NOT for drinking. Protect eyes from splashes; cover the table.",15)
A.aOP=s(['A tray as the "ground"',"Cheap building materials: matchboxes, pieces of bamboo/wood, small stones, or pieces of cardboard"],t.s)
A.aMc=s(["Build two towers on the tray: one tall and thin, one wider at the bottom.","Shake the tray gently, then harder and harder (like an earthquake).","Observe which tower falls first.","Try to improve them: a wide base & a sturdy structure last longer."],t.s)
A.a0K=new B.J("Earthquake-Proof Buildings","The shape & base of a building affect how well it stands up to shaking.",A.aOP,A.aMc,"When the ground shakes, buildings with a wide base and a sturdy structure stand up better than tall, thin ones. That is why engineers design earthquake-resistant buildings, especially in at-risk areas like Indonesia.",A.D,null,20)
A.aKA=s([A.a_z,A.a0K],t.T)
A.aOf=s(["Critical reasoning","Caring for others"],t.s)
A.b6S=new B.aD("Does not yet know what causes earthquakes, or does not yet know to take cover.","Names a cause of earthquakes/eruptions, but does not yet know the safety steps.","Names one cause of earthquakes/eruptions AND two safety steps during an earthquake.")
A.b2d=new B.aC(u.eO,u.mU,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Link it to news or experiences of earthquakes/volcanoes that the children know about.","Main activity (20 minutes): Explain that the Earth has layers, and movement inside it causes earthquakes and volcanic eruptions. Do the mini volcano experiment. Practise the safety steps for an earthquake (protect your head, keep away from glass).",'Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. End with the fact: Indonesia lies on the Pacific "Ring of Fire", the most active belt of volcanoes in the world.',"Pupils can name one cause of earthquakes/eruptions and two safety steps during an earthquake.",A.aOf,u.e,A.b6S)
A.blF=new B.aw("gunung_berapi_gempa","Volcanoes & Earthquakes","\ud83c\udf0b",A.ao,A.bw,u.eO,u.mU,30,'This topic matters for disaster preparedness, especially in Indonesia. Slip in a short drill: "protect your head, get down, keep away from glass".',A.aV3,A.aKA,A.b2d,null,!0,!1,"Ask the children to put the earthquake safety steps in order (drop \u2013 cover \u2013 hold on) and point out places to take cover in the classroom.",'Practise "drop, cover your head, hold on" under the desks together as a drill. Repeat until the children know the order by heart.',u.J)
A.S9=new B.f(A.Z,"The Sun disappears?","In an eclipse, the day suddenly goes dark. What happens?",A.E,"Everyone, sometimes in the daytime it suddenly goes dark for a little while, or the full moon turns a dark reddish colour. This is called an eclipse. What do you think is happening up in the sky?",u.n,null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.Lv=new B.f(A.Y,"Three in a line","An eclipse happens when Sun\u2013Earth\u2013Moon line up.",A.v,'Has anyone heard a story about the Sun or the Moon suddenly "vanishing"? Let\'s switch on a torch, then block its light with a ball. Look at the shadow on the wall. What do you think has to happen to the Sun, Earth and Moon so that one of them gets covered?',null,"Draw the Sun\u2013Earth\u2013Moon in a line. Show the shadow: once with the Moon in Earth's shadow, once with the Moon covering the Sun.",null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.beV=new B.m("gerhana_bulan","\ud83c\udf11","Lunar eclipse","LUNAR ECLIPSE: the Moon moves into Earth's shadow and looks dark or reddish. Safe to look at with your eyes.")
A.bez=new B.m("gerhana_matahari","\ud83c\udf1e","Solar eclipse","SOLAR ECLIPSE: the Moon covers the Sun and the day goes dark for a moment. DO NOT look straight at it \u2014 it harms your eyes.")
A.beb=new B.m("bayangan_g","\ud83c\udf17","Shadow","SHADOW: an eclipse is all about shadows. The object in front blocks the light, and its shadow falls on the one behind.")
A.beX=new B.m("sejajar","\ud83d\udccf","Lined up","LINED UP: eclipses are rare because the Sun, Earth and Moon must be in a perfectly straight line.")
A.aWi=s([A.beV,A.bez,A.beb,A.beX],t.w)
A.Ob=new B.f(A.W,"Two kinds of eclipse","Tap each card to find out.",A.v,"Let's find out about the two kinds of eclipse and their rules!",null,"Label the lunar & solar eclipse on the drawing.",A.aWi,!0,null,null,null,null)
A.SQ=new B.f(A.X,"Act out an eclipse!","Balls + a torch show both kinds of eclipse.",A.v,"Now let's act out an eclipse!","Make the room fairly dark. Shine the torch (Sun) at the big ball (Earth). For a lunar eclipse, put the small ball in Earth's shadow. For a solar eclipse, put the small ball between the torch and Earth. Watch the shadow.",null,null,!1,null,null,null,null)
A.aZg=new B.D("A lunar eclipse happens because clouds cover the Moon.","A lunar eclipse happens when the Moon moves into Earth's shadow (Sun-Earth-Moon lined up), not because of clouds.")
A.aZm=new B.D("It is fine to look straight at a solar eclipse if only briefly.","No; sunlight still damages the eyes, even briefly. Watch it a safe way, for example by projecting it through a pinhole.")
A.aYT=new B.D("It is safe to watch a solar eclipse through sunglasses or as a reflection in a bucket of water.","Not safe \u2014 both still let in light that damages the eyes. The safe way: make a pinhole in a piece of paper, then look at the image of the Sun that falls on another piece of paper.")
A.aTm=s([A.aZg,A.aZm,A.aYT],t.I)
A.Md=new B.f(A.U,"Check understanding","In a lunar eclipse, whose shadow does the Moon enter?",A.E,"Si Jabrik is confused \u2014 help him out! In a lunar eclipse, whose shadow does the Moon move into? And why must we never look straight at a solar eclipse?",u.a,null,null,!0,null,null,null,A.aTm)
A.MT=new B.f(A.V,"WHAT?! \ud83e\udd2f","A total solar eclipse is seen only along a narrow path!",A.a_,"Now we can answer our question from the start: a shadow is covering the light. And here's the surprising part: in a total solar eclipse, the Moon's shadow on Earth is very small \u2014 just a narrow path. So a total eclipse in one place is very rare \u2014 it might happen only once every few hundred years! People travel a long way just to see it for a few minutes.","A round of applause for the dance of the Sun, Earth and Moon! Tomorrow at midday, look at the round spots of light on the ground under a leafy tree: they are images of the Sun, coming through gaps in the leaves like pinholes! During an eclipse, the spots turn into crescents.",null,null,!1,null,"Experts can work out when and where an eclipse will happen decades ahead, down to the minute. Indonesia's weather agency (BMKG) then announces it, so schools and communities can get ready to watch it safely.","During the total solar eclipse of 1919, scientists photographed stars near the Sun that are normally hidden by its glare. The photos showed that starlight is bent by the Sun's pull, just as Albert Einstein had predicted.",null)
A.aLY=s([A.S9,A.Lv,A.Ob,A.SQ,A.Md,A.MT],t.n)
A.aSx=s(["A big ball (Earth) and a small ball (Moon)","A torch (Sun)","A fairly dark room"],t.s)
A.aSa=s(["Shine the torch (Sun) at the big ball (Earth) in the dark room.","LUNAR ECLIPSE: put the small ball (Moon) behind Earth, inside Earth's shadow \u2014 the Moon goes dark.","SOLAR ECLIPSE: put the small ball (Moon) between the torch and Earth \u2014 the Moon's shadow falls on Earth.","Watch how a shadow covers one of the objects."],t.s)
A.a09=new B.J("Eclipse Model with Balls","An eclipse happens when the Sun, Earth and Moon line up.",A.aSx,A.aSa,"An eclipse happens when the Sun, Earth and Moon are in a line. Lunar eclipse: the Moon moves into Earth's shadow. Solar eclipse: the Moon covers the Sun and its shadow falls on Earth. Eclipses only happen now and then because the three are rarely exactly lined up.",A.D,"Do not shine the torch into anyone's eyes. IMPORTANT: never look at a real solar eclipse with the naked eye \u2014 it can damage the eyes.",15)
A.a2u=s(["A small coin","A big object in the distance (a wall clock, a ball, or the moon/sun* )"],t.s)
A.aMZ=s(["Hold the small coin close to one eye.","Close the other eye and line the coin up with the big object far away.","Move the coin until it completely covers the big object.","Discuss: how can a small coin cover a big object?"],t.s)
A.Zm=new B.J('A Coin Covers the "Sun"',"A small object close up can cover a big object far away.",A.a2u,A.aMZ,"Because it is close to the eye, a small coin can cover a big object far away. The Moon is the same: it is much smaller than the Sun, but because it is much closer to Earth, it can cover the Sun during an eclipse. (*Never look straight at the Sun.)",A.D,"Do NOT use the real Sun \u2014 use another safe object far away.",10)
A.aOo=s([A.a09,A.Zm],t.T)
A.b70=new B.aD("Cannot yet tell a lunar eclipse from a solar eclipse.","Knows there are two kinds of eclipse, but still mixes up the positions of Earth and the Moon.","Tells a lunar eclipse from a solar eclipse by the positions of Earth and the Moon.")
A.b27=new B.aC(u.aU,u.oJ,"Opening (5 minutes): Ask the hook question. Link it to stories or news about eclipses the children have heard.","Main activity (20 minutes): Explain that an eclipse happens when the Sun, Earth and Moon are in a line. Demonstrate a lunar and a solar eclipse with balls + a torch. Stress the shadow that does the covering.","Closing (5 minutes): Questions and answers. Remind them not to look straight at a solar eclipse. Close with the fact: a total solar eclipse can only be seen from a small part of Earth.","Pupils can tell a lunar eclipse from a solar eclipse by the positions of Earth and the Moon.",A.b2,u.e,A.b70)
A.bmg=new B.aw("gerhana","Eclipses","\ud83c\udf18",A.ao,A.bw,u.aU,u.oJ,30,"A natural follow-on from the Moon & Its Phases lesson. Be firm about the safety rule: never look straight at a solar eclipse.",A.aLY,A.aOo,A.b27,null,!0,!1,"Ask the children to explain the difference between a lunar and a solar eclipse using the order of the Sun, Earth and Moon.","Demonstrate with a torch (Sun), a big ball (Earth) and a small ball (Moon); line them up to show where the shadow falls.",u.k)
